WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.950 --> 00:00:14.320
Tim Ferriss: Gentlemen.

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:18.110
David.

00:00:18.110 --> 00:00:21.900
Daniil.

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:25.570
So where did you grow up?

00:00:25.570 --> 00:00:28.720
And what did childhood look like?

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:32.079
Daniil Liberman: So the two of us were born
in Moscow.

00:00:32.079 --> 00:00:34.450
Still Soviet Union at that time.

00:00:34.450 --> 00:00:42.120
To a family of very known neuroscientists.

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:45.200
Biophysicists.

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:55.940
Who the family were sort of upper middle class
for the Soviet measure.

00:00:55.940 --> 00:01:01.920
But as soon as the Soviet Union collapsed
we — with the Soviet Union, the family collapsed.

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:04.089
It was a below poverty type of situation.

00:01:04.089 --> 00:01:06.689
Because our dad was born in 1925.

00:01:06.689 --> 00:01:12.210
So in 1990 he was pretty old already —
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:01:12.210 --> 00:01:15.990
Daniil Liberman: — and didn't have a chance
to adapt to the reality.

00:01:15.990 --> 00:01:17.150
He always —
David Liberman: And it was six of us.

00:01:17.150 --> 00:01:19.170
Daniil Liberman: It was six of us kids.

00:01:19.170 --> 00:01:21.009
David Liberman: Elementary school.

00:01:21.009 --> 00:01:23.310
Daniil Liberman: Elementary school kids.

00:01:23.310 --> 00:01:24.510
So a complicated situation.

00:01:24.510 --> 00:01:29.950
Both parents had to continue the work as they
wanted actually to finalize and finish the

00:01:29.950 --> 00:01:36.890
work of their life, and at the same time somehow
survive and feed kids and get some clothes

00:01:36.890 --> 00:01:37.890
for us and stuff.

00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:41.439
David Liberman: It's like six of us one year
apart.

00:01:41.439 --> 00:01:44.880
Daniil Liberman: Our mom was —
Tim Ferriss: Was a mom.

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:50.650
Daniil Liberman: She was busy with making
her breakthrough works in how neurons work

00:01:50.650 --> 00:01:53.380
and the signaling system in the neurons.

00:01:53.380 --> 00:01:57.979
At the same time, having six of us in eight
years, less than eight years.

00:01:57.979 --> 00:01:59.009
David Liberman: Yes.

00:01:59.009 --> 00:02:01.649
Tim Ferriss: How did she — sorry to interrupt.

00:02:01.649 --> 00:02:05.721
But how does any human do that, let alone
a mom?

00:02:05.721 --> 00:02:08.270
Not to get us into all these confusing gender
waters.

00:02:08.270 --> 00:02:10.869
But let's just say for the time being mom.

00:02:10.869 --> 00:02:11.869
Primary caregiver?

00:02:11.869 --> 00:02:12.869
Would that be fair to say?

00:02:12.869 --> 00:02:13.869
David Liberman: Yes.

00:02:13.869 --> 00:02:14.869
Daniil Liberman: Yes.

00:02:14.869 --> 00:02:18.810
Tim Ferriss: So how did she manage both the
scientific exploration with this groundbreaking

00:02:18.810 --> 00:02:19.940
work and six kids?

00:02:19.940 --> 00:02:22.069
I can barely manage one dog —
Daniil Liberman: I say always.

00:02:22.069 --> 00:02:23.930
Tim Ferriss: — and what qualifies as my
job?

00:02:23.930 --> 00:02:25.470
So how did she do that?

00:02:25.470 --> 00:02:31.440
David Liberman: I think that after the fourth
one, we kind of a little bit managed ourself.

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:32.440
Tim Ferriss: Right.

00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:35.420
David Liberman: But still it's not the answer
to the question.

00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:36.501
Daniil Liberman: The kids were taking care
of the kids.

00:02:36.501 --> 00:02:37.501
No, there was a —
Tim Ferriss: Training managers.

00:02:37.501 --> 00:02:38.950
Daniil Liberman: — grandma and grandpa from
time to time.

00:02:38.950 --> 00:02:44.580
So from time to time they would drop half
of us at the grandpa and grandma's place,

00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:47.489
and then took half of us with them to the
laboratory.

00:02:47.489 --> 00:02:49.230
That's how we —
David Liberman: Spent a lot of time.

00:02:49.230 --> 00:02:50.230
Daniil Liberman: — spent a lot.

00:02:50.230 --> 00:02:52.030
Surrounded by the fish and snails.

00:02:52.030 --> 00:02:56.170
David Liberman: We were surrounded by all
this equipment.

00:02:56.170 --> 00:02:57.170
Daniil Liberman: Lasers.

00:02:57.170 --> 00:02:58.170
Microscopes.

00:02:58.170 --> 00:03:02.000
David Liberman: They got first PCs much earlier
than anyone else.

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:06.650
That's why we also were quite excited about
that.

00:03:06.650 --> 00:03:10.650
I think that in general they were super passionate
—

00:03:10.650 --> 00:03:11.650
Daniil Liberman: About the work.

00:03:11.650 --> 00:03:12.650
David Liberman: — about their work.

00:03:12.650 --> 00:03:14.709
That's what we definitely picked from our
parents.

00:03:14.709 --> 00:03:16.160
Daniil Liberman: And about each other.

00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:18.000
So there were six of us.

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:19.730
David Liberman: That's for sure.

00:03:19.730 --> 00:03:20.730
Tim Ferriss: It's a full team, yeah.

00:03:20.730 --> 00:03:21.730
It's a full soccer team.

00:03:21.730 --> 00:03:25.150
So you have lots of excitement.

00:03:25.150 --> 00:03:26.159
Maybe lots of chaos.

00:03:26.159 --> 00:03:28.830
You have goldfish, snails, grandparents, lasers.

00:03:28.830 --> 00:03:32.290
Sounds like a pretty awesome setup for curious
kids, honestly.

00:03:32.290 --> 00:03:34.310
Daniil Liberman: For curious kids.

00:03:34.310 --> 00:03:42.330
Tim Ferriss: And what did you pick up on intellectually
as a way of thinking from your parents?

00:03:42.330 --> 00:03:47.010
And I ask that because I know both of you
for instance, and I'm not trying to lead with

00:03:47.010 --> 00:03:50.740
this question, but pay a lot of attention
to data.

00:03:50.740 --> 00:03:52.959
You're sort of geeks for parsing the data.

00:03:52.959 --> 00:03:56.840
Whether it's to refine how you think about
or identify problems and then also how you

00:03:56.840 --> 00:03:58.010
might solve problems.

00:03:58.010 --> 00:04:03.610
What did you observe in your parents or pick
up from them when it came to how they thought

00:04:03.610 --> 00:04:04.610
about things?

00:04:04.610 --> 00:04:09.739
Daniil Liberman: Now that you said that, I
realized that our parents were doing those

00:04:09.739 --> 00:04:16.090
experiments which required them years of repeating
the same stuff.

00:04:16.090 --> 00:04:22.040
Trying to get data which would make sense
on top of their hypothesis of how everything

00:04:22.040 --> 00:04:23.040
works.

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:28.780
So I guess we've been surrounded by the papers.

00:04:28.780 --> 00:04:31.370
Piles of papers with just numbers.

00:04:31.370 --> 00:04:33.580
Literally, I remember this.

00:04:33.580 --> 00:04:34.800
David Liberman: They were like this.

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:35.930
How we call this?

00:04:35.930 --> 00:04:36.930
Cards?

00:04:36.930 --> 00:04:37.930
Daniil Liberman: Cards.

00:04:37.930 --> 00:04:38.930
Punch cards.

00:04:38.930 --> 00:04:39.930
Tim Ferriss: Oh, the punch cards.

00:04:39.930 --> 00:04:41.720
David Liberman: The punch cards, yes.

00:04:41.720 --> 00:04:43.850
Daniil Liberman: Yes, the punch cards.

00:04:43.850 --> 00:04:44.850
Everywhere the punch cards.

00:04:44.850 --> 00:04:56.640
Before the PC was, there were this huge type
recording machines with all this data as well.

00:04:56.640 --> 00:05:02.620
But what's interesting, both of them was always
challenging the status quo of the current

00:05:02.620 --> 00:05:03.770
understanding of science.

00:05:03.770 --> 00:05:09.090
David Liberman: So if just someone claims
something and you cannot really prove it for

00:05:09.090 --> 00:05:14.160
yourself with data, probably something is
missing and you need to dig deeper.

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:17.509
Daniil Liberman: And that's how they lived.

00:05:17.509 --> 00:05:20.020
And they had their own discussions with everyone
around.

00:05:20.020 --> 00:05:23.550
And we sort of picked it up as we were growing
already.

00:05:23.550 --> 00:05:24.930
I don't know.

00:05:24.930 --> 00:05:26.210
15ish years old.

00:05:26.210 --> 00:05:36.680
Our dad was even older, so he needed someone
to escort him to the conferences and meetings

00:05:36.680 --> 00:05:37.680
of scientists.

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:45.330
Just basically it's Moscow in winter and the
roads are icy, I don't know, five, six months

00:05:45.330 --> 00:05:46.620
a year.

00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:47.850
Something like this.

00:05:47.850 --> 00:05:52.100
So he needed someone to basically help him
get there.

00:05:52.100 --> 00:05:58.720
And being there all the time, we saw his conversations
with the rest of the scientific community

00:05:58.720 --> 00:06:04.180
in his institute as they were just laughing
out of nowhere.

00:06:04.180 --> 00:06:05.990
I mean just, "Oh, yeah.

00:06:05.990 --> 00:06:06.990
Of course.

00:06:06.990 --> 00:06:11.470
Again, Liberman is talking about the quantum
nature of the consciousness.

00:06:11.470 --> 00:06:13.430
Blah, blah, blah, blah."

00:06:13.430 --> 00:06:14.430
Something like that.

00:06:14.430 --> 00:06:15.430
And we were looking at this.

00:06:15.430 --> 00:06:16.729
It's like why?

00:06:16.729 --> 00:06:19.400
He has some ideas and proofs.

00:06:19.400 --> 00:06:22.610
So it was sort of a —
David Liberman: Resistance.

00:06:22.610 --> 00:06:25.250
Daniil Liberman: — resistance to the rest
of the world.

00:06:25.250 --> 00:06:28.880
We felt his resistance and were challenged
with this as well.

00:06:28.880 --> 00:06:30.560
Like, "Oh, we going to prove everyone."

00:06:30.560 --> 00:06:33.310
Tim Ferriss: Libermans against the world.

00:06:33.310 --> 00:06:34.310
Daniil Liberman: Libermans against the world.

00:06:34.310 --> 00:06:35.310
Exactly.

00:06:35.310 --> 00:06:36.700
Tim Ferriss: All right, so we're going to
leapfrog from that.

00:06:36.700 --> 00:06:42.170
So as far as a next logical place, I like
hopping around because I see things on paper,

00:06:42.170 --> 00:06:45.860
I find certain things exciting to talk about,
and then we can fill in the gaps.

00:06:45.860 --> 00:06:53.719
So I'm just going to actually answer my own
question and I'm going to take you to around

00:06:53.719 --> 00:06:56.169
— then we can fill in the gaps as we go.

00:06:56.169 --> 00:07:02.470
So you're around 18 to 19 years old and you're
presenting to Russian Parliament.

00:07:02.470 --> 00:07:07.410
What are you presenting about and how is the
reception?

00:07:07.410 --> 00:07:10.270
David Liberman: Yeah, so a little bit of background.

00:07:10.270 --> 00:07:11.270
Tim Ferriss: Sure.

00:07:11.270 --> 00:07:18.220
David Liberman: So after Soviet Union collapsed,
the entire population of the country lost

00:07:18.220 --> 00:07:20.319
their savings in the banks.

00:07:20.319 --> 00:07:21.669
Twice during the decade.

00:07:21.669 --> 00:07:22.669
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:07:22.669 --> 00:07:26.410
David Liberman: So it was a quite harsh time
for everyone.

00:07:26.410 --> 00:07:29.770
And for us it was —
Daniil Liberman: Strange.

00:07:29.770 --> 00:07:31.819
David Liberman: — really strange.

00:07:31.819 --> 00:07:38.080
We weren't able to understand how with all
the modern technologies, with computers and

00:07:38.080 --> 00:07:42.660
et cetera, how it's all this corruption and
how it's even possible.

00:07:42.660 --> 00:07:45.040
Tim Ferriss: That your savings could just
disappear.

00:07:45.040 --> 00:07:47.000
David Liberman: Yes, definitely some technologies
—

00:07:47.000 --> 00:07:48.720
Daniil Liberman: Should be there.

00:07:48.720 --> 00:07:50.690
David Liberman: — should be there to solve
these problems.

00:07:50.690 --> 00:07:57.080
Daniil Liberman: We had internet pretty early
and we were hackers pretty — it was at that

00:07:57.080 --> 00:08:00.740
time of the internet era when everything was
possible.

00:08:00.740 --> 00:08:01.740
David Liberman: Yeah.

00:08:01.740 --> 00:08:04.090
Daniil Liberman: Access to everything was
possible.

00:08:04.090 --> 00:08:13.330
So at that moment we read lots of the hackers'
manifestos and some libertarian ideas wandering

00:08:13.330 --> 00:08:14.509
around the internet.

00:08:14.509 --> 00:08:22.110
And we saw all these ideas of decentralized
type of data storage, which can be —

00:08:22.110 --> 00:08:23.979
David Liberman: Protected.

00:08:23.979 --> 00:08:28.979
Daniil Liberman: — bulletproof from —
Tim Ferriss: Various attacks or —

00:08:28.979 --> 00:08:32.120
Daniil Liberman: — attacks and modifications.

00:08:32.120 --> 00:08:33.120
Like falsifications.

00:08:33.120 --> 00:08:34.120
Tim Ferriss: Right.

00:08:34.120 --> 00:08:35.349
It's resilient against falsification.

00:08:35.349 --> 00:08:36.349
Daniil Liberman: Yes.

00:08:36.349 --> 00:08:37.349
Resilient against falsification.

00:08:37.349 --> 00:08:44.570
Blockchain type of ideas before Satoshi actually
released his first paper on the blockchain.

00:08:44.570 --> 00:08:47.470
Which was never the one word.

00:08:47.470 --> 00:08:49.500
It was blocklike chain of blocks.

00:08:49.500 --> 00:08:50.500
David Liberman: But BitTorrent and other type
of —

00:08:50.500 --> 00:08:51.500
Daniil Liberman: But BitTorrent and other
type of —

00:08:51.500 --> 00:08:54.200
David Liberman: — these storages existed
even before.

00:08:54.200 --> 00:08:55.200
Tim Ferriss: Sure.

00:08:55.200 --> 00:08:56.200
Daniil Liberman: It is about this.

00:08:56.200 --> 00:08:57.200
We're circulating over the internet.

00:08:57.200 --> 00:09:05.829
So with this combination of those ideas, we
came up to a solution as we saw it for eradicating

00:09:05.829 --> 00:09:06.980
corruption in Russia.

00:09:06.980 --> 00:09:11.180
To make all of the governmental expenses completely
transparent.

00:09:11.180 --> 00:09:13.360
David Liberman: Store it on the people's machines.

00:09:13.360 --> 00:09:17.660
Daniil Liberman: And store it in the people's
machines so that none of the corrupt officials

00:09:17.660 --> 00:09:20.029
can actually even do anything with the data.

00:09:20.029 --> 00:09:26.390
So we are presenting this idea, and pretty
soon when people realize what we're talking

00:09:26.390 --> 00:09:29.070
about they basically ask us to go out.

00:09:29.070 --> 00:09:31.320
David Liberman: Shut down the mic.

00:09:31.320 --> 00:09:32.700
Daniil Liberman: Shut down the mic.

00:09:32.700 --> 00:09:34.070
He's like, "The meeting is over.

00:09:34.070 --> 00:09:35.070
Thank you so much."

00:09:35.070 --> 00:09:36.070
Tim Ferriss: "Sorry, we're having some technical
difficulties."

00:09:36.070 --> 00:09:37.070
Daniil Liberman: Exactly.

00:09:37.070 --> 00:09:38.070
Tim Ferriss: "If you could step off the stage."

00:09:38.070 --> 00:09:39.250
Daniil Liberman: And then the guy who was
actually —

00:09:39.250 --> 00:09:40.250
David Liberman: Who invited us.

00:09:40.250 --> 00:09:41.250
Daniil Liberman: Who invited us.

00:09:41.250 --> 00:09:44.510
He was some sort of a deputy for the head
of the party at the time.

00:09:44.510 --> 00:09:46.980
At the time there was multiple parties.

00:09:46.980 --> 00:09:52.650
Not only United Russia, but this guy came.

00:09:52.650 --> 00:09:54.360
David Liberman: Approached us.

00:09:54.360 --> 00:09:59.290
Daniil Liberman: Approached us in the corridor
saying, "Boys, I can see you are very smart

00:09:59.290 --> 00:10:01.100
and all these ideas are just brilliant.

00:10:01.100 --> 00:10:04.899
But stop talking about this, otherwise someone
will kill you.

00:10:04.899 --> 00:10:06.290
No, not us.

00:10:06.290 --> 00:10:07.290
We're good guys.

00:10:07.290 --> 00:10:13.070
But there are bad people who are making lots
of money and this is against what they want."

00:10:13.070 --> 00:10:15.829
Tim Ferriss: Okay.

00:10:15.829 --> 00:10:21.589
So now at that point, do you pretty immediately
pivot to something else?

00:10:21.589 --> 00:10:26.710
Daniil Liberman: Pretty immediately we realize
that what we need to pivot into is grassrooting

00:10:26.710 --> 00:10:30.839
those ideas instead of trying to make them
a top-to-bottom approach.

00:10:30.839 --> 00:10:34.620
David Liberman: It's quite —
Tim Ferriss: Instead of being the missionaries

00:10:34.620 --> 00:10:36.980
of anti-corruption.

00:10:36.980 --> 00:10:38.030
Distribute the risk a bit.

00:10:38.030 --> 00:10:39.030
Daniil Liberman: Yes.

00:10:39.030 --> 00:10:43.649
David Liberman: It started to be quite clear
that people in the government —

00:10:43.649 --> 00:10:45.149
Daniil Liberman: Will never ever.

00:10:45.149 --> 00:10:49.560
David Liberman: — are not those people who
really can adopt those ideas.

00:10:49.560 --> 00:10:51.959
And it was another story to that.

00:10:51.959 --> 00:10:55.800
In a week after that, they actually invited
us again to come.

00:10:55.800 --> 00:11:00.810
And we came and they asked us to work on a
project.

00:11:00.810 --> 00:11:02.850
And we said, "Well, what is the project?"

00:11:02.850 --> 00:11:07.839
And they said, "Well, you mentioned something
about digitalization and how digitalization

00:11:07.839 --> 00:11:09.940
can be anonymous at the same time.

00:11:09.940 --> 00:11:17.110
Can you create an anonymous HR agency which
will sell seats in government?"

00:11:17.110 --> 00:11:19.410
Daniil Liberman: And we're like, "Wait.

00:11:19.410 --> 00:11:20.410
What?"

00:11:20.410 --> 00:11:21.700
David Liberman: He's like, "Yes."

00:11:21.700 --> 00:11:25.769
So imagine people who actually making lots
of money and who's capable of paying lots

00:11:25.769 --> 00:11:27.910
of money for a seat in the government.

00:11:27.910 --> 00:11:29.079
They are people who — business.

00:11:29.079 --> 00:11:30.829
Daniil Liberman: They manage better than us.

00:11:30.829 --> 00:11:31.829
David Liberman: They manage better.

00:11:31.829 --> 00:11:35.100
They can manage stuff better than us just
from the street.

00:11:35.100 --> 00:11:36.100
Daniil Liberman: Yes.

00:11:36.100 --> 00:11:39.459
David Liberman: Just happened to be here just
by chance because someone was a friend of

00:11:39.459 --> 00:11:40.459
someone.

00:11:40.459 --> 00:11:45.990
So if we actually invite people who did something
in business and have money to pay for the

00:11:45.990 --> 00:11:48.639
position in government, they can do a better
job for the country.

00:11:48.639 --> 00:11:51.621
Daniil Liberman: Our first reaction was like,
"Do you really have —

00:11:51.621 --> 00:11:52.700
David Liberman: Price list for that?

00:11:52.700 --> 00:11:53.700
Daniil Liberman: — prices?"

00:11:53.700 --> 00:11:54.700
David Liberman: He's like, "Yes, for sure."

00:11:54.700 --> 00:11:56.860
We're like, "Can you give us an example?"

00:11:56.860 --> 00:12:00.709
And the guy was like —
Daniil Liberman: Deputy Minister of Finance

00:12:00.709 --> 00:12:02.140
was half a million dollars.

00:12:02.140 --> 00:12:03.140
Tim Ferriss: Wow.

00:12:03.140 --> 00:12:04.570
Daniil Liberman: And Minister of Healthcare.

00:12:04.570 --> 00:12:05.570
David Liberman: Ecology.

00:12:05.570 --> 00:12:07.280
Daniil Liberman: I mean, Ecology was $3 million.

00:12:07.280 --> 00:12:10.010
David Liberman: So the question was immediately,
"Ecology?

00:12:10.010 --> 00:12:14.060
We don't give a shit about ecology in Russia."

00:12:14.060 --> 00:12:15.720
He's like, "Exactly.

00:12:15.720 --> 00:12:20.930
That's how the person who will take the position
will have a return on investment pretty much."

00:12:20.930 --> 00:12:22.940
Daniil Liberman: They use all these fancy
words.

00:12:22.940 --> 00:12:26.510
David Liberman: They'll get their money back
pretty much in a year on bribes.

00:12:26.510 --> 00:12:27.870
Tim Ferriss: Oh.

00:12:27.870 --> 00:12:30.829
Daniil Liberman: So we quite soon realized
that they are —

00:12:30.829 --> 00:12:34.350
Tim Ferriss: You're like, "Well, We appreciate
the invitation."

00:12:34.350 --> 00:12:36.000
Daniil Liberman: "We appreciate the invitation.

00:12:36.000 --> 00:12:37.540
Never call us again.

00:12:37.540 --> 00:12:38.800
Thank you."

00:12:38.800 --> 00:12:41.310
That was our reaction.

00:12:41.310 --> 00:12:49.060
But yes, that was a moment when we decided
to pivot to building our own businesses and

00:12:49.060 --> 00:12:50.180
—
David Liberman: Making our own money.

00:12:50.180 --> 00:12:51.180
Daniil Liberman: — gathering resources.

00:12:51.180 --> 00:12:54.370
David Liberman: Gathering resources and then
building type of technologies ourselves.

00:12:54.370 --> 00:12:56.820
Tim Ferriss: Then you can buy the Ministry
of Ecology seat.

00:12:56.820 --> 00:12:58.269
Daniil Liberman: One day.

00:12:58.269 --> 00:12:59.720
David Liberman: One day.

00:12:59.720 --> 00:13:01.959
Or we can do other revolution or something
like that.

00:13:01.959 --> 00:13:03.000
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:13:03.000 --> 00:13:08.000
Now at that point do you — while you're
observing all of this and it's imprinting

00:13:08.000 --> 00:13:09.180
your young minds.

00:13:09.180 --> 00:13:15.600
Are you thinking, "Eventually we are going
to want to build businesses somewhere else,"

00:13:15.600 --> 00:13:20.730
or did you anticipate that you would be building
in Russia for the foreseeable future?

00:13:20.730 --> 00:13:22.860
How were you thinking about things at that
point?

00:13:22.860 --> 00:13:28.630
David Liberman: For us, somehow at this particular
moment, it was still unreachable.

00:13:28.630 --> 00:13:32.730
The idea that we can fly somewhere and build
businesses there.

00:13:32.730 --> 00:13:33.730
Tim Ferriss: Right.

00:13:33.730 --> 00:13:39.690
David Liberman: It is only years after that
we start to be more confident in our abilities

00:13:39.690 --> 00:13:41.670
and start to think about that.

00:13:41.670 --> 00:13:44.829
Daniil Liberman: It was miserable the first
time we flew to United States.

00:13:44.829 --> 00:13:46.250
Just on our own.

00:13:46.250 --> 00:13:47.310
Miserable experience.

00:13:47.310 --> 00:13:49.019
Very low level.

00:13:49.019 --> 00:13:50.720
Poor level of English.

00:13:50.720 --> 00:13:54.080
No understanding whatsoever what the country
looks like and what's going on.

00:13:54.080 --> 00:13:55.339
David Liberman: We're trying to raise investment.

00:13:55.339 --> 00:13:56.339
Daniil Liberman: We were trying to raise investment.

00:13:56.339 --> 00:13:57.449
David Liberman: Was total flop.

00:13:57.449 --> 00:13:58.449
Daniil Liberman: Miserable.

00:13:58.449 --> 00:14:01.240
Tim Ferriss: That sounds like a hard slog
of a first trip.

00:14:01.240 --> 00:14:02.550
Daniil Liberman: Yes, Exactly.

00:14:02.550 --> 00:14:03.550
Tim Ferriss: Okay.

00:14:03.550 --> 00:14:07.660
So at the time then you're just focused on
building businesses.

00:14:07.660 --> 00:14:10.360
And what type of business were you building
at that point?

00:14:10.360 --> 00:14:11.660
Daniil Liberman: We had ideas.

00:14:11.660 --> 00:14:12.660
2001, 2002.

00:14:12.660 --> 00:14:18.910
David Liberman: Even before that, our first
company was an ISP.

00:14:18.910 --> 00:14:20.240
Internet service provider.

00:14:20.240 --> 00:14:25.949
It was kind of — we wanted to have a good
internet connection and it was super unaffordable.

00:14:25.949 --> 00:14:28.540
$5,000 to connect your apartment.

00:14:28.540 --> 00:14:33.230
Tim Ferriss: Wow, that sounds very unreachable
for most people.

00:14:33.230 --> 00:14:34.230
David Liberman: Super unreachable.

00:14:34.230 --> 00:14:35.230
For us as well.

00:14:35.230 --> 00:14:37.830
Daniil Liberman: Especially for the family
of eight people who was making a couple hundred

00:14:37.830 --> 00:14:40.540
dollars a month for the household.

00:14:40.540 --> 00:14:42.060
It was unreachable.

00:14:42.060 --> 00:14:48.620
So at some point we realized that if we want
to have such level of internet at our own

00:14:48.620 --> 00:14:49.880
apartment, what we can do.

00:14:49.880 --> 00:14:52.290
We can go through all of the neighbors.

00:14:52.290 --> 00:14:54.120
And it was like what, 1600?

00:14:54.120 --> 00:14:55.910
David Liberman: We went door to door.

00:14:55.910 --> 00:14:59.209
Daniil Liberman: 1600 apartments in the —
David Liberman: Small district.

00:14:59.209 --> 00:15:00.649
Daniil Liberman: — small district.

00:15:00.649 --> 00:15:01.740
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:15:01.740 --> 00:15:03.540
Daniil Liberman: It's quite dense.

00:15:03.540 --> 00:15:04.850
Tim Ferriss: The neighborhood.

00:15:04.850 --> 00:15:06.610
Daniil Liberman: The neighborhood.

00:15:06.610 --> 00:15:09.690
Like dense neighborhoods of [inaudible] in
Moscow.

00:15:09.690 --> 00:15:12.880
And we literally rang all the doorbells.

00:15:12.880 --> 00:15:15.389
And the question was, "Do you have a computer?"

00:15:15.389 --> 00:15:18.040
If the answer was yes, "Do you know what internet
is?"

00:15:18.040 --> 00:15:21.209
If the answer was no, "Okay, let us explain
you.

00:15:21.209 --> 00:15:22.209
Let's have some tea."

00:15:22.209 --> 00:15:26.529
And realized you are literally sneaking into
people's apartment and starting to explain

00:15:26.529 --> 00:15:30.680
to them what they are losing by not having
internet already connected to their computer.

00:15:30.680 --> 00:15:32.640
David Liberman: So we pre-sold couple hundreds
of —

00:15:32.640 --> 00:15:34.920
Daniil Liberman: Pre-sold couple hundreds
contracts.

00:15:34.920 --> 00:15:39.509
And with that we were like, "Okay, we can
now go to the bank.

00:15:39.509 --> 00:15:41.089
Get a..."

00:15:41.089 --> 00:15:43.600
How is it called?

00:15:43.600 --> 00:15:45.399
Tim Ferriss: A loan.

00:15:45.399 --> 00:15:46.399
Daniil Liberman: Consumer loan.

00:15:46.399 --> 00:15:47.399
Just basically consumer loan.

00:15:47.399 --> 00:15:50.699
And we use the money to buy all the equipment
to buy the cable.

00:15:50.699 --> 00:15:52.139
David Liberman: Optics.

00:15:52.139 --> 00:15:53.569
Daniil Liberman: Fiber.

00:15:53.569 --> 00:15:55.959
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, fiberoptic cable.

00:15:55.959 --> 00:15:57.880
Daniil Liberman: Fiberoptic cable.

00:15:57.880 --> 00:16:01.740
And basically connect all these households
and people paid.

00:16:01.740 --> 00:16:04.470
And we covered the loan pretty fast.

00:16:04.470 --> 00:16:07.959
David Liberman: It still was a quite challenging
project.

00:16:07.959 --> 00:16:10.490
Tim Ferriss: So I'm wondering just what you're
buying with the loans.

00:16:10.490 --> 00:16:12.879
You're buying servers, sticking them in a
bathroom?

00:16:12.879 --> 00:16:16.889
How was it being connected to the existing
infrastructure?

00:16:16.889 --> 00:16:17.889
Daniil Liberman: So existing.

00:16:17.889 --> 00:16:20.200
First we needed to — basically someone to
bring the cable in.

00:16:20.200 --> 00:16:22.970
It was the most expensive part of the operation.

00:16:22.970 --> 00:16:23.970
Tim Ferriss: Digging trenches.

00:16:23.970 --> 00:16:24.970
Daniil Liberman: Digging trenches.

00:16:24.970 --> 00:16:25.970
David Liberman: We hired.

00:16:25.970 --> 00:16:26.970
Daniil Liberman: We hired the engineering
team.

00:16:26.970 --> 00:16:31.160
We were lucky because the largest —
David Liberman: Hub.

00:16:31.160 --> 00:16:36.240
Daniil Liberman: — communication hub for
the Moscow internet was basically two kilometers

00:16:36.240 --> 00:16:37.240
away from us.

00:16:37.240 --> 00:16:38.240
Tim Ferriss: That's great luck.

00:16:38.240 --> 00:16:39.579
Daniil Liberman: It's great luck.

00:16:39.579 --> 00:16:43.740
David Liberman: Because our parents from science,
we were in the part of Moscow where —

00:16:43.740 --> 00:16:45.310
Daniil Liberman: Where most of the scientists
was.

00:16:45.310 --> 00:16:48.750
David Liberman: — most of the scientists
were and the universities were there as well.

00:16:48.750 --> 00:16:50.170
And that's why —
Tim Ferriss: Makes sense.

00:16:50.170 --> 00:16:51.170
Daniil Liberman: Makes sense.

00:16:51.170 --> 00:16:53.339
David Liberman: — internet first emerged
at this part.

00:16:53.339 --> 00:16:56.600
Daniil Liberman: So we hired these people,
the engineering team, to bring the cable.

00:16:56.600 --> 00:17:06.429
And then by night mostly we were connecting
20-story buildings to each other by shooting

00:17:06.429 --> 00:17:10.290
the cable using the athletic air bolt.

00:17:10.290 --> 00:17:11.809
Tim Ferriss: A crossbow?

00:17:11.809 --> 00:17:13.339
Daniil Liberman: Crossbow, yeah.

00:17:13.339 --> 00:17:14.480
David Liberman: Yes.

00:17:14.480 --> 00:17:21.720
It's just local officials, they were bribed
by the bigger player and they didn't allow

00:17:21.720 --> 00:17:24.630
us to actually —
Daniil Liberman: They wouldn't give us the

00:17:24.630 --> 00:17:25.630
keys.

00:17:25.630 --> 00:17:28.180
David Liberman: — get into the roofs and
to properly set the cables.

00:17:28.180 --> 00:17:29.290
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, competition.

00:17:29.290 --> 00:17:30.690
Daniil Liberman: Competition was there.

00:17:30.690 --> 00:17:32.070
Even though we had the license.

00:17:32.070 --> 00:17:33.070
David Liberman: License.

00:17:33.070 --> 00:17:35.750
Daniil Liberman: And by law they couldn't
prohibit us to do this, but they were not

00:17:35.750 --> 00:17:36.750
allowing us.

00:17:36.750 --> 00:17:40.020
That's why at night we would sneak onto the
roof.

00:17:40.020 --> 00:17:46.250
Pretty dangerous operation of getting onto
the roof even without having an access through

00:17:46.250 --> 00:17:47.250
the door.

00:17:47.250 --> 00:17:52.750
And then from the roof to the roof, we basically
were putting these cables on our own using

00:17:52.750 --> 00:17:53.810
the crossbows.

00:17:53.810 --> 00:17:56.190
Shooting from one roof to another roof.

00:17:56.190 --> 00:17:59.870
David Liberman: This company was then acquired
by a bigger player.

00:17:59.870 --> 00:18:03.260
There was a significant consolidation on the
market.

00:18:03.260 --> 00:18:09.880
Tim Ferriss: So between the ninja crossbow
expansion and the acquisition, how long did

00:18:09.880 --> 00:18:10.880
that take?

00:18:10.880 --> 00:18:11.880
Daniil Liberman: Two years.

00:18:11.880 --> 00:18:12.880
Tim Ferriss: Two years.

00:18:12.880 --> 00:18:13.880
David Liberman: Three years.

00:18:13.880 --> 00:18:14.880
Tim Ferriss: All right.

00:18:14.880 --> 00:18:15.880
Moving fast.

00:18:15.880 --> 00:18:16.880
Daniil Liberman: Moving fast.

00:18:16.880 --> 00:18:20.990
Then later on we realized that what we actually
need to do is super computing.

00:18:20.990 --> 00:18:22.910
We were like, "We are smart in mathematics.

00:18:22.910 --> 00:18:23.970
We can build out.

00:18:23.970 --> 00:18:25.090
We can write algorithms.

00:18:25.090 --> 00:18:26.650
And this is something."

00:18:26.650 --> 00:18:29.600
We'd foreseen the future of cloud computing.

00:18:29.600 --> 00:18:33.910
Using machines in internet to be —
David Liberman: To parallelize.

00:18:33.910 --> 00:18:38.870
Daniil Liberman: To parallelize compute over
the — this was our idea originally.

00:18:38.870 --> 00:18:43.039
Then we realized that it's impossible to do
in Russia type of business.

00:18:43.039 --> 00:18:47.590
And we decided that what we can do, we can
start building games.

00:18:47.590 --> 00:18:49.200
Tim Ferriss: Now before you go on.

00:18:49.200 --> 00:18:54.530
Was the computer science and whatever coding
experience you had, was that self-taught?

00:18:54.530 --> 00:18:55.640
Was it school?

00:18:55.640 --> 00:18:56.640
Was it parents?

00:18:56.640 --> 00:18:59.650
David Liberman: Our college degree is computer
science.

00:18:59.650 --> 00:19:02.240
But in reality.

00:19:02.240 --> 00:19:12.150
In Russia at this moment, no one will really
teach you properly computer science because

00:19:12.150 --> 00:19:16.120
your teachers would know less than —
Tim Ferriss: Than you would.

00:19:16.120 --> 00:19:17.170
Daniil Liberman: If you have internet.

00:19:17.170 --> 00:19:18.170
Tim Ferriss: If you're motivated.

00:19:18.170 --> 00:19:19.170
Daniil Liberman: If you're motivated.

00:19:19.170 --> 00:19:21.450
David Liberman: So we were lucky.

00:19:21.450 --> 00:19:28.559
Because our parents were scientists, we got
PCs at our home much earlier than most of

00:19:28.559 --> 00:19:29.559
our peers.

00:19:29.559 --> 00:19:33.350
We got internet in our home much earlier than
most of our peers.

00:19:33.350 --> 00:19:36.170
And we were super excited about this technology.

00:19:36.170 --> 00:19:39.490
Daniil Liberman: We were curious, and I would
say naughty.

00:19:39.490 --> 00:19:44.270
So we would break the computers all the time
and we had to fix them all the time for the

00:19:44.270 --> 00:19:46.120
parents to continue their work.

00:19:46.120 --> 00:19:51.520
And that's how we actually learn how to —
David Liberman: We built our first website

00:19:51.520 --> 00:19:53.450
for our guild in online game.

00:19:53.450 --> 00:19:54.510
Daniil Liberman: Online gaming.

00:19:54.510 --> 00:19:55.510
Ultima Online.

00:19:55.510 --> 00:19:56.510
David Liberman: Ultima Online.

00:19:56.510 --> 00:19:57.510
Tim Ferriss: Great choice.

00:19:57.510 --> 00:19:58.510
Curious and naughty.

00:19:58.510 --> 00:20:00.960
That can be the name of your autobiography.

00:20:00.960 --> 00:20:04.870
The Life and Times of the Libermen.

00:20:04.870 --> 00:20:11.159
Okay, so you have the consolidation and then
you decide to focus on games after that?

00:20:11.159 --> 00:20:12.159
David Liberman: Yes.

00:20:12.159 --> 00:20:13.159
Daniil Liberman: Yes.

00:20:13.159 --> 00:20:14.159
Tim Ferriss: And what form did that take?

00:20:14.159 --> 00:20:16.120
David Liberman: We played Ultima Online.

00:20:16.120 --> 00:20:18.470
We were super excited about these type of
games.

00:20:18.470 --> 00:20:22.650
We thought that this is revolution because
people just hang out there.

00:20:22.650 --> 00:20:29.650
There is no aim or you don't need to just
kill all the enemy.

00:20:29.650 --> 00:20:30.650
Just people hang out there.

00:20:30.650 --> 00:20:31.650
Daniil Liberman: Sandbox.

00:20:31.650 --> 00:20:32.650
David Liberman: Sandbox, yeah.

00:20:32.650 --> 00:20:33.650
Daniil Liberman: You can have a marriage there.

00:20:33.650 --> 00:20:34.650
Build a house.

00:20:34.650 --> 00:20:35.650
David Liberman: Like Roblox.

00:20:35.650 --> 00:20:36.650
Tim Ferriss: Second Life type of feeling.

00:20:36.650 --> 00:20:37.650
David Liberman: Second Life, yes.

00:20:37.650 --> 00:20:38.650
Tim Ferriss: Instead of Doom.

00:20:38.650 --> 00:20:39.650
Daniil Liberman: Instead Of Doom.

00:20:39.650 --> 00:20:40.650
Exactly.

00:20:40.650 --> 00:20:44.640
We're very excited about this, but had to
play only overnight because of a dial-up connection

00:20:44.640 --> 00:20:47.780
and the phone was for everyone in the apartment.

00:20:47.780 --> 00:20:49.840
For us at night.

00:20:49.840 --> 00:20:52.620
That's why we actually wanted the better internet
all the time.

00:20:52.620 --> 00:20:54.080
And that's how the ISP started.

00:20:54.080 --> 00:20:57.250
David Liberman: So then when we decided to
build such game.

00:20:57.250 --> 00:21:04.160
At that moment, this idea was really not popular.

00:21:04.160 --> 00:21:12.490
Most of the — you even can find some quotes
from CEO of [inaudible] would say, "Online

00:21:12.490 --> 00:21:13.810
games is just a niche market."

00:21:13.810 --> 00:21:14.810
Daniil Liberman: It will never be.

00:21:14.810 --> 00:21:17.420
David Liberman: No one will play online games
and all this stuff.

00:21:17.420 --> 00:21:21.110
Daniil Liberman: He actually —
David Liberman: Then apologized many years

00:21:21.110 --> 00:21:22.110
after.

00:21:22.110 --> 00:21:23.110
Daniil Liberman: — officially admitted he
was wrong.

00:21:23.110 --> 00:21:26.419
Tim Ferriss: That's very, I suppose —
Daniil Liberman: Honest.

00:21:26.419 --> 00:21:27.549
Tim Ferriss: — honest.

00:21:27.549 --> 00:21:29.659
A lot of people don't own those things.

00:21:29.659 --> 00:21:32.039
Daniil Liberman: Yes, he was super cool in
that.

00:21:32.039 --> 00:21:33.440
He was like, "I said that.

00:21:33.440 --> 00:21:34.440
I was wrong.

00:21:34.440 --> 00:21:35.440
Now we are in the game."

00:21:35.440 --> 00:21:41.330
David Liberman: So we tried to launch several
one time.

00:21:41.330 --> 00:21:42.370
Failed.

00:21:42.370 --> 00:21:43.370
Our own savings.

00:21:43.370 --> 00:21:45.580
Daniil Liberman: Spent all of our own savings.

00:21:45.580 --> 00:21:48.120
David Liberman: Was out soon.

00:21:48.120 --> 00:21:54.860
But then World of Warcraft was released, and
we were the only ones who were —

00:21:54.860 --> 00:21:56.640
Daniil Liberman: For years trying to raise
money.

00:21:56.640 --> 00:21:58.520
David Liberman: — trying to raise for such
type of the project.

00:21:58.520 --> 00:21:59.520
And that's how at some point.

00:21:59.520 --> 00:22:01.650
Daniil Liberman: We got all that attention
at this moment.

00:22:01.650 --> 00:22:03.550
And we raised our first money.

00:22:03.550 --> 00:22:04.919
Tim Ferriss: So World of Warcraft comes out.

00:22:04.919 --> 00:22:05.919
It proves.

00:22:05.919 --> 00:22:06.919
It's a proof of concept —
Daniil Liberman: Yes.

00:22:06.919 --> 00:22:07.919
David Liberman: Yes.

00:22:07.919 --> 00:22:08.919
Tim Ferriss: — for what you are also trying
to get to in a way.

00:22:08.919 --> 00:22:10.020
David Liberman: Not only proof of concept.

00:22:10.020 --> 00:22:15.220
It was the first game ever netting $1 billion
of profit a year.

00:22:15.220 --> 00:22:18.679
It was just —
Tim Ferriss: I think people outside of games

00:22:18.679 --> 00:22:21.789
also today do not realize how big the numbers
are.

00:22:21.789 --> 00:22:22.789
David Liberman: Yes.

00:22:22.789 --> 00:22:29.679
Tim Ferriss: The opening weekend for the new
Call of Duty compared to every Hollywood blockbuster

00:22:29.679 --> 00:22:31.909
for a six month period or something like that.

00:22:31.909 --> 00:22:32.909
The numbers are astronomical.

00:22:32.909 --> 00:22:36.090
David Liberman: This market was really for
years larger than —

00:22:36.090 --> 00:22:37.140
Daniil Liberman: The movies.

00:22:37.140 --> 00:22:39.100
David Liberman: — the movie market.

00:22:39.100 --> 00:22:42.990
But 15 years ago it's —
Tim Ferriss: It's not as obvious.

00:22:42.990 --> 00:22:43.990
David Liberman: Yes.

00:22:43.990 --> 00:22:47.250
Daniil Liberman: But it was already obvious
that it can make lots of money.

00:22:47.250 --> 00:22:49.720
So we were able to convince some of the investors.

00:22:49.720 --> 00:22:51.740
Tim Ferriss: Investors tend to like that,
yes.

00:22:51.740 --> 00:23:00.470
David Liberman: And we got a lot of attention,
grew pretty fast, to 150 to 100 employees

00:23:00.470 --> 00:23:01.600
in different times.

00:23:01.600 --> 00:23:05.190
But then 2008 crisis came and just it's all
—

00:23:05.190 --> 00:23:08.539
Daniil Liberman: And we were not ready.

00:23:08.539 --> 00:23:15.200
World of Warcraft, it took Blizzard, the top
game development studio in the world, that

00:23:15.200 --> 00:23:19.950
what we didn't realize when we started, it
took them like five, six years to build a

00:23:19.950 --> 00:23:20.950
game.

00:23:20.950 --> 00:23:21.950
David Liberman: It's quite complicated.

00:23:21.950 --> 00:23:22.950
Tim Ferriss: With an army.

00:23:22.950 --> 00:23:24.590
Daniil Liberman: With an army and unlimited
resources.

00:23:24.590 --> 00:23:30.150
And us, we were like, "We can do it with a
couple million dollars."

00:23:30.150 --> 00:23:31.150
Okay.

00:23:31.150 --> 00:23:33.280
We were miserable in this case.

00:23:33.280 --> 00:23:42.210
But honestly in this experience we realized
that we can be cool in developing cheap cheat

00:23:42.210 --> 00:23:49.360
— not cheating, but how to say?

00:23:49.360 --> 00:23:50.450
Hacking.

00:23:50.450 --> 00:23:56.250
Hacking our way through the challenges, inventing
new stuff, inventing how we can build a —

00:23:56.250 --> 00:23:57.730
David Liberman: New type of process.

00:23:57.730 --> 00:24:00.549
Daniil Liberman: New type of processes and
technologies and et cetera.

00:24:00.549 --> 00:24:06.200
And even though we bankrupted the company
in 2009, by March, 2009 I had to fire everyone,

00:24:06.200 --> 00:24:08.220
like 150 people in one day.

00:24:08.220 --> 00:24:09.690
Tim Ferriss: Sounds fun.

00:24:09.690 --> 00:24:10.810
Daniil Liberman: Super fun.

00:24:10.810 --> 00:24:14.150
David Liberman: It was the most challenging
experience of our life.

00:24:14.150 --> 00:24:15.730
Daniil Liberman: The most challenging experience
of our life.

00:24:15.730 --> 00:24:23.400
And then three, four months later, we already
started an animation studio, and used all

00:24:23.400 --> 00:24:26.620
of the ideas which came to our mind during
this period.

00:24:26.620 --> 00:24:29.480
Like, we can actually do this and this and
that.

00:24:29.480 --> 00:24:33.700
We wouldn't do this in the games, but we can
do this in the animation process.

00:24:33.700 --> 00:24:40.200
Tim Ferriss: So you were able to take things
from the failed gaming studio and transfer

00:24:40.200 --> 00:24:44.070
them over into the —
David Liberman: In terms of experience.

00:24:44.070 --> 00:24:45.380
Tim Ferriss: In terms of experience, like
lessons learned.

00:24:45.380 --> 00:24:47.270
Daniil Liberman: Most experience, like lesson
was learned.

00:24:47.270 --> 00:24:53.970
We knew how to do stuff better, as we were
progressing from knowing nothing when we started,

00:24:53.970 --> 00:25:01.390
to basically having a pretty sophisticated
production process, we immediately reinvented

00:25:01.390 --> 00:25:02.450
the animation process.

00:25:02.450 --> 00:25:07.060
So we basically took the guy who was experienced
in producing 3D graphics and it was like,

00:25:07.060 --> 00:25:08.649
"Okay, why don't we change it all?

00:25:08.649 --> 00:25:14.510
Why don't we take what you know and then try
to figure out how to make it much more efficient?"

00:25:14.510 --> 00:25:20.289
Tim Ferriss: How did you decide on that, versus
the hundred other things that you might —

00:25:20.289 --> 00:25:23.870
David Liberman: First of all, it was a super
challenging time.

00:25:23.870 --> 00:25:25.470
The company was bankrupt.

00:25:25.470 --> 00:25:30.450
By that time, we actually already were quite
successful in different stuff.

00:25:30.450 --> 00:25:37.360
We already had a venture firm, raised $30
million to the venture firm.

00:25:37.360 --> 00:25:40.000
But this venture firm was took over.

00:25:40.000 --> 00:25:42.419
Daniil Liberman: By our investors of the gaming
studio.

00:25:42.419 --> 00:25:44.710
David Liberman: And so it's like ration style.

00:25:44.710 --> 00:25:46.650
Daniil Liberman: It's ration style, ration
style.

00:25:46.650 --> 00:25:49.090
David Liberman: The people with the guns and
the cars.

00:25:49.090 --> 00:25:54.690
Daniil Liberman: Literally, the security people
from the persons arrived at the office, took

00:25:54.690 --> 00:26:03.400
us in the black cars, driving us to the underground
of the, what is it, office building in the

00:26:03.400 --> 00:26:04.400
middle of Moscow.

00:26:04.400 --> 00:26:05.740
David Liberman: So first of all —
Daniil Liberman: And then threatening us that

00:26:05.740 --> 00:26:09.470
they will kill us and our family because they
know where everyone lives, unless we will

00:26:09.470 --> 00:26:10.470
return the money.

00:26:10.470 --> 00:26:11.640
David Liberman: So this type of business experience.

00:26:11.640 --> 00:26:12.640
Daniil Liberman: This type of business experience.

00:26:12.640 --> 00:26:14.049
Tim Ferriss: Hard-lined, strict investors.

00:26:14.049 --> 00:26:19.840
David Liberman: In that moment we knew that
we needed to, with the new projects, we needed

00:26:19.840 --> 00:26:28.370
to do something fast, because we still had
some obligations in place, which we just fired

00:26:28.370 --> 00:26:30.940
because we haven't paid the salary for last
month.

00:26:30.940 --> 00:26:33.260
Daniil Liberman: We tried to —
Tim Ferriss: So you had outstanding debt —

00:26:33.260 --> 00:26:35.860
Daniil Liberman: The outstanding debt was
like half a million dollars.

00:26:35.860 --> 00:26:37.880
And we were still living with our parents.

00:26:37.880 --> 00:26:43.910
David Liberman: So we knew that we don't want
to have investors anymore, and they see all

00:26:43.910 --> 00:26:45.159
this experience which we had.

00:26:45.159 --> 00:26:48.460
We wanted to do something which can generate
cash right away.

00:26:48.460 --> 00:26:49.840
Not —
Tim Ferriss: Enough for apartment building

00:26:49.840 --> 00:26:50.840
basements.

00:26:50.840 --> 00:26:51.840
David Liberman: Yes, yes, yes.

00:26:51.840 --> 00:26:52.840
Tim Ferriss: That was enough.

00:26:52.840 --> 00:26:53.840
David Liberman: Yes.

00:26:53.840 --> 00:26:55.830
Daniil Liberman: Then there was luck —
David Liberman: And you mentioned was —

00:26:55.830 --> 00:27:03.580
Daniil Liberman: — because a friend of ours
who is a famous actress in Russia, she told

00:27:03.580 --> 00:27:10.350
the CEO, the president of Channel One, which
is like NBC of Russia, like the largest network

00:27:10.350 --> 00:27:12.240
—
Tim Ferriss: Or one of the BBC channels in

00:27:12.240 --> 00:27:14.090
the UK or 20 Minutes —
Daniil Liberman: Exactly, exactly.

00:27:14.090 --> 00:27:15.899
David Liberman: That we can do magic with
graphics.

00:27:15.899 --> 00:27:18.490
Daniil Liberman: She told them that we can
do magic with the computer graphics.

00:27:18.490 --> 00:27:20.690
Tim Ferriss: How did you become friends with
the actress?

00:27:20.690 --> 00:27:24.640
Daniil Liberman: Friends of friends introduced
us and we basically became super cool friends.

00:27:24.640 --> 00:27:29.179
We were like writing scripts together for
the future movies and stuff.

00:27:29.179 --> 00:27:35.120
David Liberman: In reality, I'm not sure that
she really knew that we can do that, what

00:27:35.120 --> 00:27:37.710
they see, what —
Tim Ferriss: But she was very confident in

00:27:37.710 --> 00:27:38.710
selling it.

00:27:38.710 --> 00:27:40.640
David Liberman: What she knew was that we
fixed her internet.

00:27:40.640 --> 00:27:41.760
Daniil Liberman: Many times.

00:27:41.760 --> 00:27:42.880
David Liberman: Many times.

00:27:42.880 --> 00:27:45.539
Tim Ferriss: It goes a long way, keeping somebody
online.

00:27:45.539 --> 00:27:46.539
David Liberman: Yes.

00:27:46.539 --> 00:27:53.610
Tim Ferriss: So she sells — it's head of
this primary channel, that you can work magic

00:27:53.610 --> 00:27:54.610
with graphics.

00:27:54.610 --> 00:27:55.610
Daniil Liberman: We work magic with graphics.

00:27:55.610 --> 00:27:58.080
And we actually were doing magic with graphics,
but for games.

00:27:58.080 --> 00:28:03.390
David Liberman: And he wanted to create a
political satire show, which would be based

00:28:03.390 --> 00:28:05.990
on the last week events, which is impossible.

00:28:05.990 --> 00:28:10.679
And you mentioned usual production cycle is
nine to 12 months of production.

00:28:10.679 --> 00:28:13.860
Daniil Liberman: South Park is actually like
three, four months.

00:28:13.860 --> 00:28:18.500
But then they leave these several minutes
for something topical, which they can produce

00:28:18.500 --> 00:28:22.000
over a couple of weeks, right before the episode
is released, like they are doing this trick.

00:28:22.000 --> 00:28:23.000
Tim Ferriss: And that's South Park.

00:28:23.000 --> 00:28:24.000
They're the fastest.

00:28:24.000 --> 00:28:25.000
Daniil Liberman: They are the fastest.

00:28:25.000 --> 00:28:26.000
Exactly.

00:28:26.000 --> 00:28:32.049
David Liberman: And we invented the production
process, which allowed us to produce half-hour

00:28:32.049 --> 00:28:34.940
animated show, 3D graphics, in one week.

00:28:34.940 --> 00:28:36.600
Tim Ferriss: How did you do that?

00:28:36.600 --> 00:28:40.690
Daniil Liberman: The interesting part was
we just basically disassembled the entire

00:28:40.690 --> 00:28:44.590
process on smaller pieces and paralleled them.

00:28:44.590 --> 00:28:50.120
This idea of decentralization and parallelization
was like we couldn't live it.

00:28:50.120 --> 00:28:51.120
Tim Ferriss: Right.

00:28:51.120 --> 00:28:55.240
So rather than an assembly-line type of production,
you're like, how many of these can we have

00:28:55.240 --> 00:28:56.240
running in parallel?

00:28:56.240 --> 00:28:57.240
Daniil Liberman: In parallel.

00:28:57.240 --> 00:28:58.640
David Liberman: We found out —
Tim Ferriss: Were you able to borrow resources

00:28:58.640 --> 00:29:00.510
from the channel to work on those parallels?

00:29:00.510 --> 00:29:04.210
Daniil Liberman: No, we actually at the time
were able to borrow resources from IBM.

00:29:04.210 --> 00:29:05.210
Tim Ferriss: Okay.

00:29:05.210 --> 00:29:06.210
How does that work?

00:29:06.210 --> 00:29:09.420
Daniil Liberman: Friend of ours was the president
of IBM of Russia.

00:29:09.420 --> 00:29:11.580
David Liberman: And we agreed that they would
be able —

00:29:11.580 --> 00:29:14.740
Daniil Liberman: He was a DJ in the club during
the weekends.

00:29:14.740 --> 00:29:16.650
That's how we knew the guy.

00:29:16.650 --> 00:29:21.000
So during the weekends —
Tim Ferriss: Moral of the story, you never

00:29:21.000 --> 00:29:22.000
know who's DJing at the club.

00:29:22.000 --> 00:29:23.000
Make friends.

00:29:23.000 --> 00:29:24.909
Daniil Liberman: They're DJing at night during
the weekends, and then he's in a suit like

00:29:24.909 --> 00:29:27.360
a serious president of IBM during the weekdays.

00:29:27.360 --> 00:29:28.360
Tim Ferriss: That's crazy.

00:29:28.360 --> 00:29:29.360
Daniil Liberman: That's crazy.

00:29:29.360 --> 00:29:30.669
And the guy was like, "I fucking love what
you are doing."

00:29:30.669 --> 00:29:32.210
I'm sorry, can I say this?

00:29:32.210 --> 00:29:33.920
Tim Ferriss: Oh, yeah.

00:29:33.920 --> 00:29:36.289
Daniil Liberman: And he was like, "Yes.

00:29:36.289 --> 00:29:38.940
I will give you the equipment."

00:29:38.940 --> 00:29:42.590
And they gave us, I don't know, what was it?

00:29:42.590 --> 00:29:48.610
David Liberman: It was around a million dollars
worth of servers, with the idea that they

00:29:48.610 --> 00:29:56.840
will give us this equipment, not permanently,
but for some time for free, in exchange that

00:29:56.840 --> 00:29:59.540
IBM will use the fact that this show was created
with —

00:29:59.540 --> 00:30:02.360
Daniil Liberman: The most innovative show
was created using IBM equipment.

00:30:02.360 --> 00:30:05.870
David Liberman: Then they gives us equipment,
but IBM decided that it's too political.

00:30:05.870 --> 00:30:06.870
Daniil Liberman: The head of this IBM.

00:30:06.870 --> 00:30:08.740
David Liberman: And they never even used it.

00:30:08.740 --> 00:30:11.960
Daniil Liberman: Because the show was political
satire in Russia.

00:30:11.960 --> 00:30:16.980
So the headquarter in the United States was
like, "No, no, no, we don't…"

00:30:16.980 --> 00:30:18.929
Tim Ferriss: That does not sound low risk.

00:30:18.929 --> 00:30:20.269
Daniil Liberman: Yes.

00:30:20.269 --> 00:30:23.450
They allowed us to use the equipment but never
put the logo in there.

00:30:23.450 --> 00:30:25.809
Tim Ferriss: Okay, so they allowed you to
use the equipment.

00:30:25.809 --> 00:30:29.090
And then was the president just like, "Look,
we're going to forget it's there for a while,

00:30:29.090 --> 00:30:30.140
you guys can use it?"

00:30:30.140 --> 00:30:31.159
Daniil Liberman: Yes.

00:30:31.159 --> 00:30:32.159
Yes.

00:30:32.159 --> 00:30:33.159
It was exactly like this.

00:30:33.159 --> 00:30:34.159
Tim Ferriss: Very helpful DJ.

00:30:34.159 --> 00:30:38.130
Daniil Liberman: We made the first season
using that equipment.

00:30:38.130 --> 00:30:39.850
David Liberman: But then already from profit
—

00:30:39.850 --> 00:30:44.559
Daniil Liberman: And then the Channel One
paid us like $3 million for the first season.

00:30:44.559 --> 00:30:45.559
Tim Ferriss: That seems significant.

00:30:45.559 --> 00:30:47.860
Daniil Liberman: And with that money we were
able to buy all the equipment we needed —

00:30:47.860 --> 00:30:49.620
Tim Ferriss: Because you owed half a million
to the employees?

00:30:49.620 --> 00:30:50.620
Daniil Liberman: Yes.

00:30:50.620 --> 00:30:51.841
The first we paid the half a million —
David Liberman: We paid it in —

00:30:51.841 --> 00:30:52.990
Daniil Liberman: It was advanced payments.

00:30:52.990 --> 00:30:55.190
So we came to Channel One, it's like, "We
needed vast payment."

00:30:55.190 --> 00:30:56.190
They're like, "Why?"

00:30:56.190 --> 00:30:58.130
It was like the processes.

00:30:58.130 --> 00:30:59.860
And we basically paid the debts.

00:30:59.860 --> 00:31:01.269
Tim Ferriss: Wow.

00:31:01.269 --> 00:31:02.269
Okay.

00:31:02.269 --> 00:31:03.269
Okay.

00:31:03.269 --> 00:31:04.269
So what happens then?

00:31:04.269 --> 00:31:09.140
So you have this parallel processing that's
working with your semi-permanently borrowed

00:31:09.140 --> 00:31:14.789
IBM machines from the DJ, you make three million,
you manage to negotiate prepayments.

00:31:14.789 --> 00:31:19.130
So you satisfy your outstanding debts to paying
the ex-employees.

00:31:19.130 --> 00:31:20.130
And then what?

00:31:20.130 --> 00:31:21.880
David Liberman: This was the first time —
Daniil Liberman: And then this summer was

00:31:21.880 --> 00:31:23.000
hard work.

00:31:23.000 --> 00:31:24.389
David Liberman: Yes.

00:31:24.389 --> 00:31:30.830
But this was the first time when we actually
start to earn enough of our own funds and

00:31:30.830 --> 00:31:35.230
we return to — first we return to our ideas,
that we need to think about —

00:31:35.230 --> 00:31:37.049
Daniil Liberman: How to fight the corruption.

00:31:37.049 --> 00:31:39.929
David Liberman: How to fight the current — fight
corruption, what type of technologies can

00:31:39.929 --> 00:31:42.299
be created to —
Tim Ferriss: Were you guys not afraid, just

00:31:42.299 --> 00:31:44.210
for your personal or family safety?

00:31:44.210 --> 00:31:45.620
Daniil Liberman: We still are not.

00:31:45.620 --> 00:31:50.269
Honestly, the part of us not being afraid
probably somehow was related to our dad was

00:31:50.269 --> 00:31:52.649
never afraid of whoever was challenging him.

00:31:52.649 --> 00:31:59.560
But at the same time, back in 2000, as we
finished the school, we knew that if we have

00:31:59.560 --> 00:32:04.929
all these ideas of changing the world, it's
going to be impossible if we just stay home,

00:32:04.929 --> 00:32:06.600
play computer games, and stuff.

00:32:06.600 --> 00:32:12.529
So we decided that we would go to some business
trainings, personal development trainings,

00:32:12.529 --> 00:32:14.659
fear trainings.

00:32:14.659 --> 00:32:17.929
We started challenging ourself in developing
ourself.

00:32:17.929 --> 00:32:27.190
And by 2005, we spent four years developing
our own internal equanimity.

00:32:27.190 --> 00:32:32.139
Tim Ferriss: Well, let's talk about this equanimity
for a second because this is super important

00:32:32.139 --> 00:32:41.110
and it's the enabler or the handicapper, the
lack of it being a handicapper in so many

00:32:41.110 --> 00:32:42.110
different capacities.

00:32:42.110 --> 00:32:47.130
And I would say a lot of the people listening
to this are going to be from the US, and fortunately

00:32:47.130 --> 00:32:51.610
for them, they've never had the experience
of being under a political regime where people

00:32:51.610 --> 00:32:52.610
disappear.

00:32:52.610 --> 00:32:54.399
But I've spent a lot of time in South America
—

00:32:54.399 --> 00:32:55.399
Daniil Liberman: Or poisoned.

00:32:55.399 --> 00:32:57.769
Tim Ferriss: Or poisoned, or fill in the blank.

00:32:57.769 --> 00:32:58.769
Yeah, "disappears."

00:32:58.769 --> 00:32:59.769
Daniil Liberman: Yes.

00:32:59.769 --> 00:33:04.039
Tim Ferriss: But what you realize is there
are political regimes all around the world,

00:33:04.039 --> 00:33:09.950
very common, where people routinely are killed,
poisoned, or otherwise made to disappear.

00:33:09.950 --> 00:33:13.070
So the risks were very real for you.

00:33:13.070 --> 00:33:16.669
And these are not conspiracy theories.

00:33:16.669 --> 00:33:18.049
They were actual threats.

00:33:18.049 --> 00:33:25.790
So was the lack of fear a lack of fear of
death, or was it a belief that you could extricate

00:33:25.790 --> 00:33:31.410
yourself or avoid any type of really significant
consequence, or something else?

00:33:31.410 --> 00:33:33.049
Daniil Liberman: There are three answers to
that.

00:33:33.049 --> 00:33:34.049
A, yes.

00:33:34.049 --> 00:33:35.960
We don't afraid to die, somehow don't.

00:33:35.960 --> 00:33:42.779
I mean this is like you die, that's it, whatever.

00:33:42.779 --> 00:33:48.960
B, somehow — and it actually really helped
with the bandits in 2009, when they came to

00:33:48.960 --> 00:33:53.970
ask for all of their money invested in the
game back.

00:33:53.970 --> 00:34:01.049
We knew that it's their way of threatening
you, and they want you to be afraid.

00:34:01.049 --> 00:34:02.870
They want you to fear.

00:34:02.870 --> 00:34:05.179
And that's how you are on the hook.

00:34:05.179 --> 00:34:07.794
As soon as you show the fear —
Tim Ferriss: That's how you are controlled.

00:34:07.794 --> 00:34:10.710
Daniil Liberman: — that's when they hook
you and you owe them forever.

00:34:10.710 --> 00:34:14.240
And we showed them that like, "Okay, kill
us, whatever, you will never get your money

00:34:14.240 --> 00:34:15.240
back.

00:34:15.240 --> 00:34:17.649
In the other case, we can probably work it
out, somehow."

00:34:17.649 --> 00:34:21.840
David Liberman: And Lawrence has this experience,
that actually if you don't show the fear,

00:34:21.840 --> 00:34:24.550
it's much safer than if you show.

00:34:24.550 --> 00:34:25.550
It's a little bit like with lions.

00:34:25.550 --> 00:34:26.550
Tim Ferriss: Like blood in the water.

00:34:26.550 --> 00:34:27.550
David Liberman: Yes.

00:34:27.550 --> 00:34:28.550
Daniil Liberman: Blood in the water.

00:34:28.550 --> 00:34:29.550
Yeah.

00:34:29.550 --> 00:34:36.210
And then the third one is when it comes to
the political systems, let's say particularly

00:34:36.210 --> 00:34:40.960
Russian political system, for the KGB people
who were actually running the country at that

00:34:40.960 --> 00:34:43.290
time, Putin was ex-head of KGB.

00:34:43.290 --> 00:34:45.800
That's the only thing which we all should
understand.

00:34:45.800 --> 00:34:48.740
That's a country run by KGB people.

00:34:48.740 --> 00:34:52.889
Yes, they call themselves FSB, but who's to
give a shit?

00:34:52.889 --> 00:34:53.889
It's KGB.

00:34:53.889 --> 00:34:59.140
It's the same guys who [inaudible] who were
just basically killing people 60, 50 years

00:34:59.140 --> 00:35:04.500
before that in Russia, after the war, before
the war.

00:35:04.500 --> 00:35:12.200
So when it comes to them, they see two type
of people who are playing against them.

00:35:12.200 --> 00:35:13.880
It's either enemies or idiots, fools.

00:35:13.880 --> 00:35:15.970
Tim Ferriss: Enemies or idiots.

00:35:15.970 --> 00:35:18.060
Daniil Liberman: Enemies or fools.

00:35:18.060 --> 00:35:19.380
David Liberman: Enemies, like spies.

00:35:19.380 --> 00:35:20.380
Daniil Liberman: Spies.

00:35:20.380 --> 00:35:21.590
David Liberman: Your either work for CIA —
Daniil Liberman: You either work for CIA —

00:35:21.590 --> 00:35:23.530
David Liberman: — or you're an idiot.

00:35:23.530 --> 00:35:28.280
Daniil Liberman: — or you're an idiot who
was convinced by CIA to fight against them

00:35:28.280 --> 00:35:30.839
and you just fool who —
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, you're a pawn.

00:35:30.839 --> 00:35:32.670
Daniil Liberman: A pawn.

00:35:32.670 --> 00:35:34.550
So we played the pawn card.

00:35:34.550 --> 00:35:38.850
We're like, "Let's show ourself as if we're
stupid."

00:35:38.850 --> 00:35:43.910
And we were completely transparent about all
of our ideas from the very beginning, saying

00:35:43.910 --> 00:35:47.780
like, "They will just see us as fools."

00:35:47.780 --> 00:35:48.780
Tim Ferriss: Like town lunatics.

00:35:48.780 --> 00:35:49.780
Daniil Liberman: Town lunatics.

00:35:49.780 --> 00:35:51.480
David Liberman: And this strategy —
Daniil Liberman: Worked.

00:35:51.480 --> 00:35:52.910
David Liberman: It worked.

00:35:52.910 --> 00:35:54.579
This point, it doesn't work anymore.

00:35:54.579 --> 00:35:56.690
Daniil Liberman: Officially, look at what’s
happening —

00:35:56.690 --> 00:35:59.849
David Liberman: We know people like that who
already were in jail.

00:35:59.849 --> 00:36:03.950
But at that particular time, at the time —
Tim Ferriss: It was just so rare that they're

00:36:03.950 --> 00:36:05.280
like, "Oh, these guys are just crazy."

00:36:05.280 --> 00:36:06.920
David Liberman: Because we made it transparently.

00:36:06.920 --> 00:36:09.410
If you are a CIA agent, you will try to hide.

00:36:09.410 --> 00:36:15.690
But if you transfer money to the biggest opposition
here transparently, write an article —

00:36:15.690 --> 00:36:18.290
Daniil Liberman: Writing an article in the
largest newspaper, Financial Times —

00:36:18.290 --> 00:36:19.682
David Liberman: — about that, you probably
need —

00:36:19.682 --> 00:36:20.682
Tim Ferriss: Hiding in plain sight.

00:36:20.682 --> 00:36:22.109
Daniil Liberman: Hiding in plain sight.

00:36:22.109 --> 00:36:23.410
And they're like, "Oh, idiots.

00:36:23.410 --> 00:36:24.410
They spend their money.

00:36:24.410 --> 00:36:25.410
Okay, whatever.

00:36:25.410 --> 00:36:27.980
We'll just steal this money from the opposition
here anyway, so fools."

00:36:27.980 --> 00:36:29.820
That was hesitation.

00:36:29.820 --> 00:36:34.380
So for us, the shield for us all those years
was like they believe we're just idiots.

00:36:34.380 --> 00:36:35.790
Tim Ferriss: Incredible.

00:36:35.790 --> 00:36:36.790
All right.

00:36:36.790 --> 00:36:40.680
We may come back to this, but I want to make
sure I don't lose the thread.

00:36:40.680 --> 00:36:47.349
So the graphics for this political satire,
it seems to be working pretty well.

00:36:47.349 --> 00:36:52.080
You returned to your ideas from prior, which
is how we got here.

00:36:52.080 --> 00:36:55.490
So thinking about anti-corruption and things
like that.

00:36:55.490 --> 00:36:57.700
And then where do you —
David Liberman: And also, we started our move

00:36:57.700 --> 00:36:59.400
to United States in 2010.

00:36:59.400 --> 00:37:02.119
Tim Ferriss: You started thinking about —
David Liberman: Immediately established our

00:37:02.119 --> 00:37:10.200
presence in Los Angeles, because of animation
in 2010, and started to move our whole attention

00:37:10.200 --> 00:37:11.200
to United States.

00:37:11.200 --> 00:37:14.030
Daniil Liberman: So we were like, "Nothing
good going to happen in the country.

00:37:14.030 --> 00:37:20.810
It's run by the KGB and they're not going
to release their grasp over the country, unless

00:37:20.810 --> 00:37:26.130
it's going to be like a revolution type of
situation," which we saw that even the opposition,

00:37:26.130 --> 00:37:33.570
they were trying to play the political game,
and it was a lost game from the very beginning.

00:37:33.570 --> 00:37:39.579
So we knew that the show political satire
on largest TV network in Russia is not going

00:37:39.579 --> 00:37:45.060
to last more than the term of puppet president
Medvedev.

00:37:45.060 --> 00:37:46.500
That's exactly what happened.

00:37:46.500 --> 00:37:52.310
As soon as Putin got back to his position,
the show was shut down immediately.

00:37:52.310 --> 00:37:57.160
So we knew it's going to end, and we knew
that we needed to spend all of the money which

00:37:57.160 --> 00:38:00.530
we will make from this in order to transit
to United States.

00:38:00.530 --> 00:38:04.020
Tim Ferriss: So two questions, and it might
seem obvious to you guys, but I think for

00:38:04.020 --> 00:38:08.359
a lot of people listening, they might wonder,
well, why not Europe?

00:38:08.359 --> 00:38:09.590
Why not somewhere else?

00:38:09.590 --> 00:38:10.590
Daniil Liberman: We tried.

00:38:10.590 --> 00:38:11.590
Tim Ferriss: Why the US?

00:38:11.590 --> 00:38:17.069
And then how hard was it or how easy to start
to move your lives to the US?

00:38:17.069 --> 00:38:23.090
David Liberman: In Europe, especially animation
industry is —

00:38:23.090 --> 00:38:24.099
Daniil Liberman: It's mostly subsidized.

00:38:24.099 --> 00:38:25.619
David Liberman: — mostly subsidized by government.

00:38:25.619 --> 00:38:32.580
So you actually need to deal with bureaucrats
and you need to get any fighting.

00:38:32.580 --> 00:38:35.970
The private sector almost doesn't exist.

00:38:35.970 --> 00:38:38.560
Tim Ferriss: I would say that makes it very
hard for immigrants.

00:38:38.560 --> 00:38:39.560
Daniil Liberman: Yes, exactly.

00:38:39.560 --> 00:38:40.560
David Liberman: Yes, yes, yes.

00:38:40.560 --> 00:38:42.640
Daniil Liberman: And it was always like if
you want to get —

00:38:42.640 --> 00:38:46.470
David Liberman: If you're local, you need
to work with local studios, and they should

00:38:46.470 --> 00:38:47.590
get this funding, and all that stuff.

00:38:47.590 --> 00:38:51.589
Daniil Liberman: We started developing the
relationship, but even faster we realized

00:38:51.589 --> 00:38:55.130
that in Hollywood we have all the chances.

00:38:55.130 --> 00:38:59.359
And pretty quickly we were introduced to people
who then introduced us to the agencies, and

00:38:59.359 --> 00:39:04.020
the William Morris Endeavor signed up with
us January, 2010.

00:39:04.020 --> 00:39:07.540
Tim Ferriss: Early, on the trajectory.

00:39:07.540 --> 00:39:11.930
Daniil Liberman: Half a year after we started
the company.

00:39:11.930 --> 00:39:14.220
David Liberman: It's still a challenging process.

00:39:14.220 --> 00:39:17.400
Any immigration is a challenging process.

00:39:17.400 --> 00:39:22.630
It took us six years for people to really
take us seriously and to get to the same type

00:39:22.630 --> 00:39:30.200
of level of professional acceptance than we
had back in Moscow.

00:39:30.200 --> 00:39:32.340
Daniil Liberman: But we found our ways.

00:39:32.340 --> 00:39:38.420
David Liberman: But at least it is possible
here, like in airport, in UK you would never

00:39:38.420 --> 00:39:41.790
become —
Daniil Liberman: You would never able to spend

00:39:41.790 --> 00:39:46.099
only five, six years and get into the major
Hollywood studios to —

00:39:46.099 --> 00:39:47.990
David Liberman: To be the same, like equal
in terms of market —

00:39:47.990 --> 00:39:50.220
Tim Ferriss: And California has a lot of downsides.

00:39:50.220 --> 00:39:57.329
But one of the upsides is — this is true
in a lot of the US, no one is discriminating

00:39:57.329 --> 00:40:00.141
against green, and people like opportunity.

00:40:00.141 --> 00:40:03.451
And also California, a lot of people don't
realize part of the reason Silicon Valley

00:40:03.451 --> 00:40:10.369
worked is that California also makes it very
hard to enforce non-compete contracts.

00:40:10.369 --> 00:40:14.740
So there are opportunities everywhere of all
different types.

00:40:14.740 --> 00:40:15.740
Daniil Liberman: Yeah.

00:40:15.740 --> 00:40:16.740
Yeah.

00:40:16.740 --> 00:40:17.740
We agree with you completely.

00:40:17.740 --> 00:40:19.109
This is the cornerstone —
David Liberman: This was our observations.

00:40:19.109 --> 00:40:24.670
It's all about non-competes, that they are
not enforceable, that this allow you to actually

00:40:24.670 --> 00:40:29.569
work at Google, write your own search engine
at night, and then launch your own search

00:40:29.569 --> 00:40:30.569
engine.

00:40:30.569 --> 00:40:32.062
And Google cannot do really anything that
you can —

00:40:32.062 --> 00:40:33.300
Tim Ferriss: There's a lot of free flow of
talent.

00:40:33.300 --> 00:40:34.300
David Liberman: Free flow.

00:40:34.300 --> 00:40:35.300
Yes.

00:40:35.300 --> 00:40:38.130
Tim Ferriss: Which makes it hard sometimes
to retain talent in Silicon Valley, but that's

00:40:38.130 --> 00:40:39.600
a whole separate story.

00:40:39.600 --> 00:40:47.930
So you land in L.A., you have introductions
to people at, say, William Morris Endeavor

00:40:47.930 --> 00:40:51.500
and other places.

00:40:51.500 --> 00:40:53.400
What happens as that momentum starts building?

00:40:53.400 --> 00:40:54.400
David Liberman: Yeah.

00:40:54.400 --> 00:41:00.099
We actually had a pretty good success working
with different studios and different big names

00:41:00.099 --> 00:41:03.510
like Jimmy Fallon, we worked on the show with
him in —

00:41:03.510 --> 00:41:09.780
Daniil Liberman: At some point we met with
the guy who used to be Jon Stewart's head

00:41:09.780 --> 00:41:18.060
writer, and he just moved back from New York
to Los Angeles, and was available-ish.

00:41:18.060 --> 00:41:20.930
He wasn't for a year, but then a year later
he was available.

00:41:20.930 --> 00:41:24.619
We took our chance to become friends over
the year.

00:41:24.619 --> 00:41:29.580
And then when he become available, like, "Josh,
why don't you work with us?"

00:41:29.580 --> 00:41:34.630
And we managed to convince him because we
showed him how fast we actually can produce

00:41:34.630 --> 00:41:35.630
animation.

00:41:35.630 --> 00:41:36.630
David Liberman: Yeah.

00:41:36.630 --> 00:41:39.170
We realized that he —
Daniil Liberman: What we realized, that people

00:41:39.170 --> 00:41:45.280
even like William Morris Endeavor or Josh,
even being the friend for us for a year, they

00:41:45.280 --> 00:41:49.420
never actually believed that we can produce
animation in one week.

00:41:49.420 --> 00:41:54.730
And we had to prove it literally by saying
like, "Josh, are you serious?

00:41:54.730 --> 00:41:57.930
Okay, let's write a script right now, a one-pager.

00:41:57.930 --> 00:41:59.450
You will write a one-pager joke.

00:41:59.450 --> 00:42:02.550
In a week you will have it in animation."

00:42:02.550 --> 00:42:03.550
And that's what we did.

00:42:03.550 --> 00:42:05.811
And he was like, "Whoa, you guys were saying
true."

00:42:05.811 --> 00:42:06.811
Tim Ferriss: You guys can actually do it.

00:42:06.811 --> 00:42:07.811
Daniil Liberman: You guys can actually do
it.

00:42:07.811 --> 00:42:12.230
David Liberman: Before that he worked at Simpsons,
before Jon Stewart, and at Simpsons like 12

00:42:12.230 --> 00:42:13.230
months —
Daniil Liberman: Like a year.

00:42:13.230 --> 00:42:14.250
He's like, "You're making a joke?"

00:42:14.250 --> 00:42:17.920
And it's all really old, a year-old joke.

00:42:17.920 --> 00:42:22.480
And with us there was an opportunity for him
to make jokes, which are super hard.

00:42:22.480 --> 00:42:23.890
David Liberman: So it was cool.

00:42:23.890 --> 00:42:29.970
But on the other hand, really wanted to focus
more on the technologies which can transform

00:42:29.970 --> 00:42:30.970
society.

00:42:30.970 --> 00:42:34.670
And plus, Hollywood wasn't really our thing.

00:42:34.670 --> 00:42:36.820
Tim Ferriss: There are some trade-offs.

00:42:36.820 --> 00:42:37.820
David Liberman: Yes.

00:42:37.820 --> 00:42:38.820
Daniil Liberman: There are lots of trade-offs.

00:42:38.820 --> 00:42:41.550
David Liberman: No one cares about technologists,
especially that time.

00:42:41.550 --> 00:42:43.319
It's before Netflix and before all this stuff.

00:42:43.319 --> 00:42:44.720
No one cared about technologists.

00:42:44.720 --> 00:42:47.170
No one cares that you are faster and cheaper.

00:42:47.170 --> 00:42:51.510
No one cared about like who is your producers
and all —

00:42:51.510 --> 00:42:52.530
Daniil Liberman: Divorce lawyers.

00:42:52.530 --> 00:42:53.960
David Liberman: — divorce lawyers and all
this stuff.

00:42:53.960 --> 00:42:54.960
Tim Ferriss: Wait, wait, wait.

00:42:54.960 --> 00:42:56.640
How does the divorce lawyer fit in?

00:42:56.640 --> 00:42:59.260
Daniil Liberman: We don't know, but people
in Hollywood say so.

00:42:59.260 --> 00:43:01.470
Tim Ferriss: Oh, wait, you need to know divorce
lawyers?

00:43:01.470 --> 00:43:02.680
Daniil Liberman: Yes, because they know people.

00:43:02.680 --> 00:43:03.930
David Liberman: They know people, they know
the power dynamics.

00:43:03.930 --> 00:43:04.930
Tim Ferriss: Oh, this is good for me to know.

00:43:04.930 --> 00:43:11.150
Daniil Liberman: There's a power dynamic of
the divorce lawyers who control —

00:43:11.150 --> 00:43:12.829
Tim Ferriss: I picked up a new stratagem today.

00:43:12.829 --> 00:43:13.829
Thank you.

00:43:13.829 --> 00:43:14.829
Daniil Liberman: Exactly.

00:43:14.829 --> 00:43:15.829
Tim Ferriss: Okay, divorce lawyers.

00:43:15.829 --> 00:43:19.400
Daniil Liberman: So, in addition, we knew
all the lawyers and we started to know the

00:43:19.400 --> 00:43:25.780
people, and we started to meet the people,
and eventually we deconstructed what the show

00:43:25.780 --> 00:43:30.230
needed to be in order to come to a meeting
in Fox, at Fox.

00:43:30.230 --> 00:43:31.230
Tim Ferriss: Okay.

00:43:31.230 --> 00:43:33.150
So you were at that point then you're pitching
your own show?

00:43:33.150 --> 00:43:34.150
Daniil Liberman: Yes.

00:43:34.150 --> 00:43:37.410
So we get to the point understanding that
we cannot sell our technology, we have to

00:43:37.410 --> 00:43:38.410
sell the entire show.

00:43:38.410 --> 00:43:39.410
David Liberman: Package, yeah.

00:43:39.410 --> 00:43:40.410
Daniil Liberman: We package it.

00:43:40.410 --> 00:43:43.000
We should package voice actors —
Tim Ferriss: The Trojan horse with the technology

00:43:43.000 --> 00:43:44.000
inside.

00:43:44.000 --> 00:43:45.000
Daniil Liberman: Exactly.

00:43:45.000 --> 00:43:46.000
We took it all.

00:43:46.000 --> 00:43:48.450
That's why we needed Josh, because we needed
a head writer.

00:43:48.450 --> 00:43:57.079
That, we took the voice actors, we took the
best concept artists to combine this all and

00:43:57.079 --> 00:44:03.390
produce a pilot, like a literal final quality
pilot, and brought it to Fox.

00:44:03.390 --> 00:44:04.420
And who was it?

00:44:04.420 --> 00:44:09.089
Suzanna Makkos at the time, she was like,
"Wow, I love it," because we actually knew

00:44:09.089 --> 00:44:12.859
what she likes, who she likes —
David Liberman: What type of artist.

00:44:12.859 --> 00:44:13.930
Daniil Liberman: What type of artist.

00:44:13.930 --> 00:44:17.470
We engineered data, data, data.

00:44:17.470 --> 00:44:20.280
And then we —
Tim Ferriss: Went to a custom tailor to put

00:44:20.280 --> 00:44:21.359
together a pilot for her?

00:44:21.359 --> 00:44:22.359
Daniil Liberman: Yes, exactly.

00:44:22.359 --> 00:44:29.809
David Liberman: But it was also some misfortuned
time, because still we didn't get back to

00:44:29.809 --> 00:44:30.809
power.

00:44:30.809 --> 00:44:36.130
So our show was canceled back in Russia in
2013.

00:44:36.130 --> 00:44:42.439
The banking crisis in Cyprus, all our money
were nationalized.

00:44:42.439 --> 00:44:45.370
So [inaudible] —
Tim Ferriss: Wait, hold on a second.

00:44:45.370 --> 00:44:50.850
So Cyprus, you had — does that mean that
you had moved your finances from Russia to

00:44:50.850 --> 00:44:51.850
Cyprus?

00:44:51.850 --> 00:44:52.850
David Liberman: Yes.

00:44:52.850 --> 00:44:55.559
Tim Ferriss: And then it was out of the pan
and into the fire.

00:44:55.559 --> 00:44:56.559
David Liberman: Yes.

00:44:56.559 --> 00:44:57.670
Tim Ferriss: And then Cyprus got nationalized.

00:44:57.670 --> 00:45:00.180
It was like uh-oh, it's the same story all
over again.

00:45:00.180 --> 00:45:01.180
David Liberman: Yes.

00:45:01.180 --> 00:45:02.589
Daniil Liberman: Same story all over again.

00:45:02.589 --> 00:45:05.640
David Liberman: It was like $6 million earned.

00:45:05.640 --> 00:45:12.339
Daniil Liberman: And all of a sudden we couldn't
pay anyone in the United States.

00:45:12.339 --> 00:45:16.609
No voice actors, no screenwriters, we couldn't
pay anyone.

00:45:16.609 --> 00:45:18.410
Immediately it all fell apart.

00:45:18.410 --> 00:45:22.540
And we're sitting there and like, all right,
are we going to do it again here?

00:45:22.540 --> 00:45:25.070
We know everything but —
Tim Ferriss: So did you get the green light

00:45:25.070 --> 00:45:26.070
for the pilot?

00:45:26.070 --> 00:45:27.070
Daniil Liberman: Yes.

00:45:27.070 --> 00:45:28.760
Tim Ferriss: And then Cyprus, and then bye-bye
fuel —

00:45:28.760 --> 00:45:30.230
Daniil Liberman: Bye-bye fuel.

00:45:30.230 --> 00:45:31.230
Exactly.

00:45:31.230 --> 00:45:35.670
And we tried to think what we can do in Hollywood
more for half a year, a year.

00:45:35.670 --> 00:45:40.390
But then we realize that it will take us another
four or five years to recover, because there's

00:45:40.390 --> 00:45:42.480
no money, there's nothing in there.

00:45:42.480 --> 00:45:45.000
We're like, "Screw it, we're not going to
stay in Hollywood.

00:45:45.000 --> 00:45:46.280
Let's move to Silicon Valley."

00:45:46.280 --> 00:45:49.470
David Liberman: And we moved to Menlo Park
at that point.

00:45:49.470 --> 00:45:51.150
Tim Ferriss: Right in the middle.

00:45:51.150 --> 00:45:52.830
Daniil Liberman: Right in the middle.

00:45:52.830 --> 00:45:58.550
David Liberman: And also, we really already
wanted to return to our real ideas.

00:45:58.550 --> 00:46:02.559
We started with transparency again.

00:46:02.559 --> 00:46:08.100
We decided that we can do a trick, that we
can actually not target governments, become

00:46:08.100 --> 00:46:11.970
transparent, but target nonprofits.

00:46:11.970 --> 00:46:16.430
Like, why we don't see how nonprofits actually
spend our money?

00:46:16.430 --> 00:46:17.430
It's kind of obvious.

00:46:17.430 --> 00:46:19.000
It's not your personal money.

00:46:19.000 --> 00:46:25.380
It's like we all together gather these funds
to make the world a better place.

00:46:25.380 --> 00:46:30.570
So we created a transparent banking platform
for nonprofits.

00:46:30.570 --> 00:46:33.980
Daniil Liberman: For many reasons.

00:46:33.980 --> 00:46:40.099
We were wrong about the —
David Liberman: Ability and readiness on —

00:46:40.099 --> 00:46:41.099
Tim Ferriss: The appetite.

00:46:41.099 --> 00:46:42.099
Daniil Liberman: The appetite, exactly.

00:46:42.099 --> 00:46:43.099
The appetite for the nonprofits.

00:46:43.099 --> 00:46:50.460
David Liberman: But this was also the times
when we realized that some of the challenges,

00:46:50.460 --> 00:46:53.490
fundamental challenges which we saw in —
Daniil Liberman: United States.

00:46:53.490 --> 00:47:00.040
David Liberman: — the economy, actually
they were true in the United States as well.

00:47:00.040 --> 00:47:05.670
Not only in Europe, not only in Russia, not
only in Israel, but in the United States as

00:47:05.670 --> 00:47:07.059
well.

00:47:07.059 --> 00:47:15.990
And we realized that even with the nonprofit
transparency, if you will look at research

00:47:15.990 --> 00:47:23.990
of what actually donors want, you will see
it's the previous generations of donors, they

00:47:23.990 --> 00:47:26.869
don't really want to see where is the money
spent.

00:47:26.869 --> 00:47:28.010
Tim Ferriss: Don't ask, don't tell.

00:47:28.010 --> 00:47:29.010
David Liberman: Yes.

00:47:29.010 --> 00:47:34.230
It's almost the last item in the list of their
preferences, their requirements.

00:47:34.230 --> 00:47:39.339
But our generation is younger, it's actually
on the first place.

00:47:39.339 --> 00:47:43.210
We want to see what happens with the —
Daniil Liberman: The dynamic of thinking behind

00:47:43.210 --> 00:47:49.349
this as for the older generation, they donate
money because it's a giveback for them.

00:47:49.349 --> 00:47:50.880
They're like society —
David Liberman: Society gives them a lot.

00:47:50.880 --> 00:47:52.750
Tim Ferriss: It's feeling good versus doing
good.

00:47:52.750 --> 00:47:53.750
David Liberman: Yes.

00:47:53.750 --> 00:47:55.960
Daniil Liberman: And for the youngest it's
like giving forward.

00:47:55.960 --> 00:47:58.630
David Liberman: They don't —
Daniil Liberman: We want to change what's

00:47:58.630 --> 00:48:02.150
bad right now, and we want to see it's changing,
now.

00:48:02.150 --> 00:48:08.750
David Liberman: And also, actually, previous
generations got a lot from society, in terms

00:48:08.750 --> 00:48:14.990
of form of free education, almost free college
education, like 30 years ago, and things like

00:48:14.990 --> 00:48:15.990
that.

00:48:15.990 --> 00:48:18.190
New generation being —
Daniil Liberman: Hammered.

00:48:18.190 --> 00:48:23.220
David Liberman: — hammered with student
debts, et cetera, they don't really feel that

00:48:23.220 --> 00:48:25.920
society gives them much.

00:48:25.920 --> 00:48:28.550
But they really still donate the same portion.

00:48:28.550 --> 00:48:29.550
Daniil Liberman: Share.

00:48:29.550 --> 00:48:33.619
David Liberman: The same share as the older
generation.

00:48:33.619 --> 00:48:35.270
But it's just different reasons to do that.

00:48:35.270 --> 00:48:38.990
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, I feel like some of the
younger generations here, younger certainly

00:48:38.990 --> 00:48:45.340
than me, get a bad reputation for misunderstood
reasons, or I should say they're misunderstood

00:48:45.340 --> 00:48:49.910
in the sense that — and I know you guys
have spoken about this before, but if you

00:48:49.910 --> 00:48:56.119
look at, say, previous generations' ability
to get a higher education, put a down payment

00:48:56.119 --> 00:48:59.760
on a house and pay for a mortgage, compared
to some of the more recent generations, it's

00:48:59.760 --> 00:49:00.760
night and day.

00:49:00.760 --> 00:49:01.760
David Liberman: Yes.

00:49:01.760 --> 00:49:02.760
Tim Ferriss: It's night and day.

00:49:02.760 --> 00:49:06.100
So it's important to factor that into how
you evaluate them.

00:49:06.100 --> 00:49:13.260
So question for you guys, looking at the nonprofit
and this and the platform, you're very good

00:49:13.260 --> 00:49:21.730
at, it seems like, letting go of ideas, having
a light grasp on ideas if things are not working,

00:49:21.730 --> 00:49:24.829
so you can move to something else that you
think might work better.

00:49:24.829 --> 00:49:25.829
David Liberman: Yes.

00:49:25.829 --> 00:49:27.530
Tim Ferriss: That's not true for everyone.

00:49:27.530 --> 00:49:33.670
Some people get very attached to one thing
and they want to ride it until they die, and

00:49:33.670 --> 00:49:36.720
they get attached to things that may never
work.

00:49:36.720 --> 00:49:45.829
And I'm wondering how you think about changing
direction, maybe "failure."

00:49:45.829 --> 00:49:47.619
What allows you to do that so readily?

00:49:47.619 --> 00:49:48.760
David Liberman: Data.

00:49:48.760 --> 00:49:49.910
Daniil Liberman: Data.

00:49:49.910 --> 00:49:50.910
Yes.

00:49:50.910 --> 00:49:52.859
David Liberman: They will learn the hard way.

00:49:52.859 --> 00:49:58.390
Daniil Liberman: So the first, the game studio,
we grasp, and we tried —

00:49:58.390 --> 00:50:01.910
David Liberman: So you know that if you don't
stop at the right moment, you can actually

00:50:01.910 --> 00:50:03.290
get to deep shit.

00:50:03.290 --> 00:50:06.170
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, you can get into a hole.

00:50:06.170 --> 00:50:07.170
David Liberman: Yes.

00:50:07.170 --> 00:50:12.309
But also what we saw in the data is that — and
what we realized also through the experience

00:50:12.309 --> 00:50:18.000
of game development, failure, and then animation
success, that we realized that —

00:50:18.000 --> 00:50:21.619
Daniil Liberman: It is not always about your
abilities.

00:50:21.619 --> 00:50:22.869
David Liberman: — your abilities.

00:50:22.869 --> 00:50:26.850
So you can fail because of different reasons,
like financial crises —

00:50:26.850 --> 00:50:27.850
Tim Ferriss: Like poker, right?

00:50:27.850 --> 00:50:28.990
David Liberman: — and the times are not
right.

00:50:28.990 --> 00:50:32.160
Tim Ferriss: You have to study the decision-making,
not just the outcomes.

00:50:32.160 --> 00:50:40.720
David Liberman: Yes, and the statistics of
venture startups, is that like two thirds

00:50:40.720 --> 00:50:42.430
will fail, completely fail.

00:50:42.430 --> 00:50:44.780
Daniil Liberman: Completely fail, losing all
the money, at zero.

00:50:44.780 --> 00:50:50.319
David Liberman: And if you will look at all
these failures, there are rare exceptions,

00:50:50.319 --> 00:50:55.520
but in most cases what you should see is that
if your startup is growing, you should continue,

00:50:55.520 --> 00:50:58.660
you should double down with your all attention
there, et cetera.

00:50:58.660 --> 00:50:59.660
Daniil Liberman: If it's not, think twice.

00:50:59.660 --> 00:51:00.960
David Liberman: But if it's not, it could
stop growing.

00:51:00.960 --> 00:51:02.790
Daniil Liberman: And probably just quit.

00:51:02.790 --> 00:51:07.290
David Liberman: And quit and start another
one.

00:51:07.290 --> 00:51:10.349
It's almost like in most of the startups you
will observe —

00:51:10.349 --> 00:51:15.380
Daniil Liberman: If we look at this purely
from the perspective of statistics, if it's

00:51:15.380 --> 00:51:22.760
not doubled the growth, if whatever numbers
behind your understanding of your business

00:51:22.760 --> 00:51:23.760
isn't doubled, it didn't double, isn't doubled
—

00:51:23.760 --> 00:51:26.290
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, didn't double —
Daniil Liberman: Didn't double —

00:51:26.290 --> 00:51:27.490
Tim Ferriss: Over what period of time?

00:51:27.490 --> 00:51:28.490
David Liberman: 14 months.

00:51:28.490 --> 00:51:29.490
Daniil Liberman: Over 14 months.

00:51:29.490 --> 00:51:30.490
Tim Ferriss: 14 months?

00:51:30.490 --> 00:51:31.490
David Liberman: Yes.

00:51:31.490 --> 00:51:32.490
Tim Ferriss: Why 14 months?

00:51:32.490 --> 00:51:33.490
I have to ask.

00:51:33.490 --> 00:51:34.490
Daniil Liberman: It's pure statistics.

00:51:34.490 --> 00:51:35.490
David Liberman: If you look at the statistics
of just —

00:51:35.490 --> 00:51:37.710
Daniil Liberman: If you look at all the failures
and successes, it's like a pattern.

00:51:37.710 --> 00:51:38.710
They should basically take —
Tim Ferriss: Oh, I see, I see.

00:51:38.710 --> 00:51:39.710
That's the pattern, is over that period — doubling
over 14 months.

00:51:39.710 --> 00:51:43.369
Daniil Liberman: If you take all of them and
divide, then just 14 months —

00:51:43.369 --> 00:51:47.630
David Liberman: Because if you're also not
growing within this space, venture capital

00:51:47.630 --> 00:51:52.730
will not be accessible to you, and you won't
be able to actually raise money, use these

00:51:52.730 --> 00:51:55.390
funds to grow further, and things like that.

00:51:55.390 --> 00:52:03.040
So this market is actually pretty special
in terms of these probabilities, like just

00:52:03.040 --> 00:52:04.040
one out of hundreds.

00:52:04.040 --> 00:52:05.760
Daniil Liberman: It is going to be a unicorn.

00:52:05.760 --> 00:52:06.760
David Liberman: — success.

00:52:06.760 --> 00:52:10.950
And if you don't see trajectory to get there,
probably —

00:52:10.950 --> 00:52:13.049
Daniil Liberman: Probably you're not getting
there.

00:52:13.049 --> 00:52:16.480
David Liberman: — the right thing is just
to stop and start something new.

00:52:16.480 --> 00:52:19.980
Daniil Liberman: And knowing that your chance
is a one in a hundred, you should try as many

00:52:19.980 --> 00:52:22.000
attempts as possible, take more shots overall.

00:52:22.000 --> 00:52:25.369
David Liberman: Moreover, we usually show
to the young —

00:52:25.369 --> 00:52:28.480
Daniil Liberman: One of the entrepreneurs.

00:52:28.480 --> 00:52:32.260
David Liberman: — people who want to be
an entrepreneurs, we show this picture of

00:52:32.260 --> 00:52:38.520
Mark Zuckerberg or Evan Spiegel who made into
success from the first attempt.

00:52:38.520 --> 00:52:41.069
Daniil Liberman: Dropping out from the college
and look, they're billionaires.

00:52:41.069 --> 00:52:43.400
David Liberman: Most of the billion dollar
companies —

00:52:43.400 --> 00:52:44.400
Daniil Liberman: It's not like that.

00:52:44.400 --> 00:52:45.400
David Liberman: — created not like that.

00:52:45.400 --> 00:52:47.350
It's created by people who failed multiple
times —

00:52:47.350 --> 00:52:50.390
Daniil Liberman: Multiple times, and then
got to success.

00:52:50.390 --> 00:52:55.069
In terms of statistics, this is what we should
observe with these things.

00:52:55.069 --> 00:52:59.079
Definitely something depends on your abilities
to execute, et cetera.

00:52:59.079 --> 00:53:01.390
But there are things which is independent
from you.

00:53:01.390 --> 00:53:02.390
Tim Ferriss: Totally.

00:53:02.390 --> 00:53:11.349
I mean I remember angel investing starting
in 2008 and seeing business models, companies

00:53:11.349 --> 00:53:16.270
that couldn't execute in a period of time
because the technology just wasn't there and

00:53:16.270 --> 00:53:17.520
the adoption wasn't there.

00:53:17.520 --> 00:53:23.490
And then five, six years later, basically
the exact same company, but a few things have

00:53:23.490 --> 00:53:24.490
changed.

00:53:24.490 --> 00:53:25.490
Mobile phones have become more powerful.

00:53:25.490 --> 00:53:28.920
A handful of protocols have been developed
and then boom, it works.

00:53:28.920 --> 00:53:32.220
David Liberman: Uber is mobile phone with
GPS.

00:53:32.220 --> 00:53:35.630
Yes, with wide enough adoption.

00:53:35.630 --> 00:53:41.569
And it was tried, these ideas were tried —
Daniil Liberman: Ordering taxi online, yes,

00:53:41.569 --> 00:53:43.549
for sure has been there.

00:53:43.549 --> 00:53:51.460
David Liberman: And because of that, especially
because we really balance ourself between

00:53:51.460 --> 00:53:56.900
sometimes when we work on some forward-looking
ideas, like not —

00:53:56.900 --> 00:54:01.490
Daniil Liberman: Flying cars, rockets to Mars.

00:54:01.490 --> 00:54:02.910
David Liberman: — something which is not
happening yet.

00:54:02.910 --> 00:54:07.490
And also we work on some projects which down
to Earth, which already something which is

00:54:07.490 --> 00:54:08.750
happening.

00:54:08.750 --> 00:54:16.500
We know that with these projects which are
not there yet, yes there is a chance to win

00:54:16.500 --> 00:54:21.710
big, but this chance is small and there is
a lot of timing issues —

00:54:21.710 --> 00:54:22.710
Tim Ferriss: Very binary.

00:54:22.710 --> 00:54:28.690
David Liberman: And you can lose just because
timing is not right.

00:54:28.690 --> 00:54:34.049
And we, as Daniil mentioned, we learned it
hard way with game development studio.

00:54:34.049 --> 00:54:37.840
It was really hard for us to stop, even though
it was obvious that probably —

00:54:37.840 --> 00:54:40.530
Daniil Liberman: We were lucky with the animation
studio.

00:54:40.530 --> 00:54:46.140
And then another unfortunate event with losing
all of the money just in banking crisis in

00:54:46.140 --> 00:54:47.230
Cyprus.

00:54:47.230 --> 00:54:49.750
And we stopped pretty fast.

00:54:49.750 --> 00:54:53.609
I mean we're like, "Okay, are we going to
spend time recovering, or are we just starting

00:54:53.609 --> 00:54:54.609
something new?"

00:54:54.609 --> 00:54:55.609
David Liberman: Yeah.

00:54:55.609 --> 00:55:03.930
When Silicon Valley Bank crisis happened,
we were among really small group of our friends,

00:55:03.930 --> 00:55:09.800
founders who all this time we were obsessed
—

00:55:09.800 --> 00:55:10.800
Daniil Liberman: With diversifying.

00:55:10.800 --> 00:55:13.980
David Liberman: — diversifying the money
on different banks.

00:55:13.980 --> 00:55:18.960
And everyone would tell us that in the United
States, you shouldn't think about that stuff.

00:55:18.960 --> 00:55:20.720
Don't worry about the science.

00:55:20.720 --> 00:55:22.339
Daniil Liberman: Can't happen.

00:55:22.339 --> 00:55:23.339
Cannot.

00:55:23.339 --> 00:55:24.339
Cannot.

00:55:24.339 --> 00:55:25.339
Just because cannot.

00:55:25.339 --> 00:55:26.339
And we're like, "Right."

00:55:26.339 --> 00:55:27.339
Tim Ferriss: Surprise.

00:55:27.339 --> 00:55:30.039
We are not immune from all the problems we've
seen other places.

00:55:30.039 --> 00:55:31.039
All right.

00:55:31.039 --> 00:55:36.380
So I'm going to zoom out for a second because
I like to think about sort of meta decisions

00:55:36.380 --> 00:55:42.599
that span across a lot of other decisions
and projects and so on.

00:55:42.599 --> 00:55:47.400
So the two of you living one life, we have
to talk about this.

00:55:47.400 --> 00:55:50.300
I have never seen an example quite like this.

00:55:50.300 --> 00:55:55.660
I mean there are couples who are say power
couples, but it's a different dynamic.

00:55:55.660 --> 00:56:00.600
I mean you guys have been seemingly sort of
hive mind from a very early age.

00:56:00.600 --> 00:56:02.250
So when did this start, right?

00:56:02.250 --> 00:56:06.799
So you have one email address, one Instagram
account, one LinkedIn.

00:56:06.799 --> 00:56:08.790
I think the phone is maybe the same.

00:56:08.790 --> 00:56:11.280
David Liberman: It's connected so that —
Tim Ferriss: Right.

00:56:11.280 --> 00:56:12.920
So I'm looking at some notes here.

00:56:12.920 --> 00:56:21.330
So since you were born, this year was the
first time that you guys spent 10 days apart

00:56:21.330 --> 00:56:28.799
or, put another way, not being together in
one place when you went to a Vipassana retreat.

00:56:28.799 --> 00:56:29.799
David Liberman: Yes.

00:56:29.799 --> 00:56:30.799
Tim Ferriss: All right.

00:56:30.799 --> 00:56:35.930
So could you explain for people who understandably
will find this interesting and kind of alien,

00:56:35.930 --> 00:56:41.849
how did this come about and how is it helpful
versus confusing?

00:56:41.849 --> 00:56:42.849
How do you guys do it?

00:56:42.849 --> 00:56:49.549
Daniil Liberman: It is confusing, but we should
understand that the six of us grew up in one

00:56:49.549 --> 00:56:52.890
room of a size smaller than this studio.

00:56:52.890 --> 00:56:58.349
Tim Ferriss: And for those who can't see the
full studio, it's not huge.

00:56:58.349 --> 00:57:02.751
This isn't an airplane hangar, it's not a
basketball court.

00:57:02.751 --> 00:57:03.751
It's like a bedroom.

00:57:03.751 --> 00:57:06.299
Daniil Liberman: Three bunk beds.

00:57:06.299 --> 00:57:10.079
That's how the six kids could fit in the room
like this.

00:57:10.079 --> 00:57:18.340
And then even later, so we grew up this up
until school already started and I think until

00:57:18.340 --> 00:57:23.710
13, 14 years old it was a situation more or
less.

00:57:23.710 --> 00:57:29.270
And then at this moment, and then parents
also send us to school by pairs.

00:57:29.270 --> 00:57:31.539
It was like a logistical innovation.

00:57:31.539 --> 00:57:35.260
Tim Ferriss: It's like buddy diving when you're
scuba diving.

00:57:35.260 --> 00:57:36.260
Daniil Liberman: Exactly.

00:57:36.260 --> 00:57:37.260
Buddy diving.

00:57:37.260 --> 00:57:40.700
David Liberman: It was dangerous to walk —
Daniil Liberman: On the streets in the '90s,

00:57:40.700 --> 00:57:41.700
alone, in Moscow.

00:57:41.700 --> 00:57:45.560
David Liberman: So put together, they don't
need to walk us to school so we can do it

00:57:45.560 --> 00:57:47.349
by ourselves and all the stuff.

00:57:47.349 --> 00:57:48.430
Tim Ferriss: High degree of confidence.

00:57:48.430 --> 00:57:49.430
David Liberman: Exactly.

00:57:49.430 --> 00:57:53.400
Daniil Liberman: So they were sending us by
pairs to school, not only the two of us but

00:57:53.400 --> 00:57:55.750
the other kids as well.

00:57:55.750 --> 00:58:02.470
So we were a pair, Dave was five years old,
I was six years old when we went to the school

00:58:02.470 --> 00:58:04.670
and the rest of the kids were seven.

00:58:04.670 --> 00:58:05.690
So we were beaten..

00:58:05.690 --> 00:58:09.760
David Liberman: But because it's two of us,
we learn how to —

00:58:09.760 --> 00:58:13.120
Daniil Liberman: How to avoid —
David Liberman: To protect ourselves being

00:58:13.120 --> 00:58:15.240
two rather than one.

00:58:15.240 --> 00:58:18.829
Daniil Liberman: And then so had to survive.

00:58:18.829 --> 00:58:20.660
It's like bonding experience.

00:58:20.660 --> 00:58:22.520
Tim Ferriss: It's very helpful for the bonding.

00:58:22.520 --> 00:58:23.520
Yeah.

00:58:23.520 --> 00:58:26.850
Daniil Liberman: It's very helpful for the
bonding, helping each other through the education,

00:58:26.850 --> 00:58:28.520
doing homework together, all the stuff together
—

00:58:28.520 --> 00:58:33.050
David Liberman: And through this process —
Daniil Liberman: By 13, 14 years old, that's

00:58:33.050 --> 00:58:38.819
when we started forming our own ideas of what
could be cool for us to do in our life.

00:58:38.819 --> 00:58:44.010
And that was already a sort of social justice
type of thinking.

00:58:44.010 --> 00:58:48.099
How can we make the world a better place for
everyone?

00:58:48.099 --> 00:58:54.180
And starting from there we saw how no one
literally was supporting this idea except

00:58:54.180 --> 00:59:00.640
two of us basically continued the conversation
in between the two of us and it never faded

00:59:00.640 --> 00:59:01.640
away.

00:59:01.640 --> 00:59:02.640
Tim Ferriss: Which idea is this?

00:59:02.640 --> 00:59:07.030
This is the combining —
David Liberman: Transparency in government.

00:59:07.030 --> 00:59:09.980
It was just one branch of this idea that technologies
—

00:59:09.980 --> 00:59:12.000
Tim Ferriss: So the two of you are very aligned.

00:59:12.000 --> 00:59:13.000
David Liberman: Yes.

00:59:13.000 --> 00:59:20.810
The technologies can actually lead us to a
better governance and that's what we thought.

00:59:20.810 --> 00:59:28.630
And through this experience of being kids,
first we realized that when you go to school,

00:59:28.630 --> 00:59:31.850
all kids, they are alone.

00:59:31.850 --> 00:59:34.490
But we weren't, it gave us a lot of advantage.

00:59:34.490 --> 00:59:35.569
Daniil Liberman: We're always two of us.

00:59:35.569 --> 00:59:42.960
David Liberman: We don't need to struggle
to company gather around us because it's already

00:59:42.960 --> 00:59:43.960
two people.

00:59:43.960 --> 00:59:48.680
It's much easier to gather together a company
like group of friends around.

00:59:48.680 --> 01:00:00.360
Also, we realized that if I know mathematics
better, we can do it the way so that no one

01:00:00.360 --> 01:00:04.750
would know that Daniil don't know mathematics
as I do.

01:00:04.750 --> 01:00:06.230
Daniil Liberman: As good as he does.

01:00:06.230 --> 01:00:09.809
David Liberman: So you can actually copy —
Tim Ferriss: Like Voltron.

01:00:09.809 --> 01:00:10.990
David Liberman: Yes.

01:00:10.990 --> 01:00:11.990
Voltron-type presentation.

01:00:11.990 --> 01:00:14.780
And we learned that that's what we can do
that.

01:00:14.780 --> 01:00:22.990
And if we weren't allowed outside world to
actually distinguish who has which qualities

01:00:22.990 --> 01:00:24.220
—
Tim Ferriss: You can cover the bases.

01:00:24.220 --> 01:00:31.790
David Liberman: Yes, we will be able to seem
to be a superhuman because we have both of

01:00:31.790 --> 01:00:32.790
the qualities.

01:00:32.790 --> 01:00:33.790
Tim Ferriss: Okay.

01:00:33.790 --> 01:00:35.819
So I know you guys as an example are fans
of the Collison Brothers, right?

01:00:35.819 --> 01:00:36.819
David Liberman: Yes.

01:00:36.819 --> 01:00:37.819
Tim Ferriss: Amazing.

01:00:37.819 --> 01:00:40.460
And Patrick's been on the podcast.

01:00:40.460 --> 01:00:44.230
My understanding is they're very powerful
together, but they have maintained, they're

01:00:44.230 --> 01:00:47.060
like, I don't want to say Batman and Robin,
that's not quite the same.

01:00:47.060 --> 01:00:48.680
They're like two of the Avengers, right?

01:00:48.680 --> 01:00:54.289
I mean they're superheroes, but they have
clearly delineated separate points of contact

01:00:54.289 --> 01:00:56.770
and so on.

01:00:56.770 --> 01:01:01.520
What have been some of the other advantages
or drawbacks of doing it the way that you

01:01:01.520 --> 01:01:03.109
guys have approached it?

01:01:03.109 --> 01:01:09.049
David Liberman: Even though they kind of a
little bit definitely more separate than we

01:01:09.049 --> 01:01:11.220
do, but they did a lot of really great stuff.

01:01:11.220 --> 01:01:16.800
For example, they were CEO and president,
they switched who is CEO and who is president

01:01:16.800 --> 01:01:17.900
and they switched roles.

01:01:17.900 --> 01:01:23.630
So they actually showed to the world that
they are interchangeable —

01:01:23.630 --> 01:01:24.630
Daniil Liberman: A team.

01:01:24.630 --> 01:01:25.630
Tim Ferriss: Tag team.

01:01:25.630 --> 01:01:31.950
David Liberman: The team and they're great
in this regard, but in our case we went to

01:01:31.950 --> 01:01:38.890
just in an extreme version of that, having
one line of communication.

01:01:38.890 --> 01:01:43.869
So if you text me, you actually text both
of us and you don't know who answered this

01:01:43.869 --> 01:01:44.869
text.

01:01:44.869 --> 01:01:45.869
Daniil Liberman: But we can answer faster.

01:01:45.869 --> 01:01:52.970
David Liberman: We can answer faster, we can
seems to be this superhuman.

01:01:52.970 --> 01:01:57.530
And that worked pretty well during negotiations
of the contract.

01:01:57.530 --> 01:02:01.569
Daniil Liberman: David honestly hated speaking
to people as he was young.

01:02:01.569 --> 01:02:07.349
He was super not into talking to people and
I was super easy to talk to people.

01:02:07.349 --> 01:02:12.120
So it was my role in the couple to talk all
the time.

01:02:12.120 --> 01:02:14.250
David Liberman: And I still never pick up
the phone —

01:02:14.250 --> 01:02:15.710
Daniil Liberman: He was never picking up the
phone —

01:02:15.710 --> 01:02:16.740
David Liberman: — if someone called.

01:02:16.740 --> 01:02:18.299
Tim Ferriss: I don't pick up the phone either.

01:02:18.299 --> 01:02:19.760
For me, it's just too much.

01:02:19.760 --> 01:02:23.900
I don't know what I'm signing up for unless
it's my brother or a family.

01:02:23.900 --> 01:02:24.900
Okay, fine.

01:02:24.900 --> 01:02:25.900
There are a few exceptions.

01:02:25.900 --> 01:02:28.480
David Liberman: It mentions that you can outsource
that.

01:02:28.480 --> 01:02:31.170
That's nice.

01:02:31.170 --> 01:02:33.960
So that worked in business principle.

01:02:33.960 --> 01:02:38.830
Even though our first business partners, they
were like, why you are in the same meeting,

01:02:38.830 --> 01:02:42.619
you can be in two places at the same time
and think like that.

01:02:42.619 --> 01:02:48.920
But we even at some moment gave up on trying
to explain them that it's allow us to — in

01:02:48.920 --> 01:02:52.800
business especially, it's like win or lose.

01:02:52.800 --> 01:02:55.880
You won this contract or lost the contract.

01:02:55.880 --> 01:02:58.140
And all these meetings are important and if
—

01:02:58.140 --> 01:03:02.210
Daniil Liberman: If you're playing two contracts
and losing both of them, it doesn't matter

01:03:02.210 --> 01:03:03.900
that it was two of them.

01:03:03.900 --> 01:03:11.130
If you're playing one, but you're playing
hardcore game of us being a team in this conversation,

01:03:11.130 --> 01:03:13.089
which can win the contract.

01:03:13.089 --> 01:03:14.089
It works much better.

01:03:14.089 --> 01:03:17.250
Tim Ferriss: Bird in the hand versus two in
the bush.

01:03:17.250 --> 01:03:22.770
What are your respective roles generally then
in terms of responsibilities or strengths?

01:03:22.770 --> 01:03:23.770
How do you think about them?

01:03:23.770 --> 01:03:29.079
Daniil Liberman: I would put this way, we
have two different intuitions and they're

01:03:29.079 --> 01:03:31.460
completely different.

01:03:31.460 --> 01:03:38.980
David's intuition is he absorbs data, he remembers
everything and then at some point he can just

01:03:38.980 --> 01:03:45.110
like, "The answer is this, like, 42," just
out of nowhere, and you're like, "Why?"

01:03:45.110 --> 01:03:48.359
He's like, "I don't know, but I'm pretty sure
that it's 42."

01:03:48.359 --> 01:03:49.359
Tim Ferriss: 42.

01:03:49.359 --> 01:03:50.800
Yeah, it's like The Hitchhiker's Guide to
the Galaxy.

01:03:50.800 --> 01:03:53.380
Daniil Liberman: And then the next morning
he can wake up.

01:03:53.380 --> 01:03:54.380
He's like, yeah.

01:03:54.380 --> 01:03:55.380
And now I know why.

01:03:55.380 --> 01:03:56.640
So I figured.

01:03:56.640 --> 01:04:02.809
I cleared my path to this place in my mind,
which actually made the calculation and tell

01:04:02.809 --> 01:04:03.970
me it's 42, so here's why.

01:04:03.970 --> 01:04:05.369
My intuition is completely different.

01:04:05.369 --> 01:04:10.579
My intuition is like, "We need to fly to London
tomorrow."

01:04:10.579 --> 01:04:12.680
He's like, "Why?"

01:04:12.680 --> 01:04:16.710
"I have a good feeling about this."

01:04:16.710 --> 01:04:20.619
And then we fly to London and we got the investors.

01:04:20.619 --> 01:04:23.960
It's like this and [I'm] like, "We need to
offer these people this."

01:04:23.960 --> 01:04:25.849
And then he's like, "No, no, no, no, no."

01:04:25.849 --> 01:04:26.900
I'm like, "Yes, yes, yes.

01:04:26.900 --> 01:04:27.970
We need to offer them this."

01:04:27.970 --> 01:04:31.100
I offer them this, and they're like, "Yes,
agree," handshaking.

01:04:31.100 --> 01:04:33.039
And they're like, "How did you know?"

01:04:33.039 --> 01:04:34.520
I'm just like, "I felt it."

01:04:34.520 --> 01:04:38.859
David Liberman: So Daniil, much better in
the reading people, Daniil much better in

01:04:38.859 --> 01:04:42.980
reading situations in general.

01:04:42.980 --> 01:04:48.430
I'm much better in processing data, processing
knowledge.

01:04:48.430 --> 01:04:52.930
I have these strange qualities that I remember
—

01:04:52.930 --> 01:04:54.180
Daniil Liberman: Everything.

01:04:54.180 --> 01:04:55.430
David Liberman: — everything.

01:04:55.430 --> 01:04:57.490
All the events, all the emails —
Daniil Liberman: Give him some time and he

01:04:57.490 --> 01:04:58.490
will.

01:04:58.490 --> 01:04:59.490
Tim Ferriss: Is that hard for you?

01:04:59.490 --> 01:05:04.140
I'm just wondering because I have family members
like this and sometimes it's a huge advantage,

01:05:04.140 --> 01:05:06.910
but are there things that you wish you could
forget or is it —

01:05:06.910 --> 01:05:10.030
David Liberman: I learned to live with that.

01:05:10.030 --> 01:05:16.911
I think that because we have both of it and
I see what is the advantage of a different

01:05:16.911 --> 01:05:23.910
type of thinking, et cetera, and I learned
how to live with that and now I embrace it.

01:05:23.910 --> 01:05:28.520
I understand the limitations, but I cannot
read dissertation properly.

01:05:28.520 --> 01:05:37.400
I understand the limitation of this tool,
but because it's two of us and we have both,

01:05:37.400 --> 01:05:42.650
I actually doubled down into these qualities
and developed these qualities further and

01:05:42.650 --> 01:05:43.650
further and further.

01:05:43.650 --> 01:05:50.579
Tim Ferriss: Do you guys get physical or psychological
anxiety when you are separate?

01:05:50.579 --> 01:05:54.240
David Liberman: This was the first time when
we were separate.

01:05:54.240 --> 01:05:59.540
Daniil Liberman: April was the first time
when we're separate, not even being able to

01:05:59.540 --> 01:06:02.510
text each other because the phone is off during
Vipassana.

01:06:02.510 --> 01:06:04.020
For 10 days long in our life.

01:06:04.020 --> 01:06:05.470
Tim Ferriss: What was that like?

01:06:05.470 --> 01:06:07.130
Well, see you had something to do.

01:06:07.130 --> 01:06:10.890
I mean you're in your own mind, but you had
a task.

01:06:10.890 --> 01:06:12.241
So what was it like for you David?

01:06:12.241 --> 01:06:17.720
David Liberman: It was unexpected, but for
me it was okay because I knew that Daniil

01:06:17.720 --> 01:06:20.800
is working on something which is important.

01:06:20.800 --> 01:06:27.720
I knew that if he will extract some new experiences
out of it, I also will do that.

01:06:27.720 --> 01:06:31.550
So it's kind of I outsourced this —
Tim Ferriss: He was the Guinea pig.

01:06:31.550 --> 01:06:32.550
Daniil Liberman: I was the Guinea pig.

01:06:32.550 --> 01:06:38.289
And I actually felt like I had this intuition,
I got to do this and I just went and it was

01:06:38.289 --> 01:06:39.289
so quick.

01:06:39.289 --> 01:06:40.510
So David had to wait for three months.

01:06:40.510 --> 01:06:43.500
David Liberman: So everyone expected —
Daniil Liberman: I was accepted four days

01:06:43.500 --> 01:06:45.670
before the course started.

01:06:45.670 --> 01:06:52.220
David Liberman: So everyone expected around
that I will be devastated by this experience.

01:06:52.220 --> 01:06:58.510
But somehow I was super calm because I probably
knew that Daniil is working on something important

01:06:58.510 --> 01:07:06.620
for us and that didn't feel like —
Tim Ferriss: There was a purpose behind it.

01:07:06.620 --> 01:07:07.620
David Liberman: Yes.

01:07:07.620 --> 01:07:10.960
Daniil Liberman: And then the second time
was the same situation but reversed.

01:07:10.960 --> 01:07:13.350
He was working on his mind and I was back
home.

01:07:13.350 --> 01:07:20.930
For me it was a little bit more challenging,
meaning that I had this sort of a mind trick,

01:07:20.930 --> 01:07:25.140
which was: "It's your fault that he's there
struggling there."

01:07:25.140 --> 01:07:27.020
I'm like, "What am I talking about?

01:07:27.020 --> 01:07:28.390
For you it was a great experience.

01:07:28.390 --> 01:07:30.809
Yeah, but for David, maybe it's not as great.

01:07:30.809 --> 01:07:32.220
Maybe it's challenging for him.

01:07:32.220 --> 01:07:33.849
Come on, seriously, stop it."

01:07:33.849 --> 01:07:35.130
Tim Ferriss: All right.

01:07:35.130 --> 01:07:37.170
So you had the internal voice keeping you
company.

01:07:37.170 --> 01:07:38.170
Daniil Liberman: Yes.

01:07:38.170 --> 01:07:41.460
Tim Ferriss: Part of the reason I ask, and
I don't know if this is true, this is from

01:07:41.460 --> 01:07:45.359
the New Yorker piece, is “Selling shares
in yourself is the way of the future,” which

01:07:45.359 --> 01:07:50.349
I would love to hear what they got, if there's
anything you wish were different in that piece

01:07:50.349 --> 01:07:52.020
for those people who might check it out.

01:07:52.020 --> 01:07:55.950
But I was curious because at one point it
says at home the brothers share a single king-sized

01:07:55.950 --> 01:07:56.950
bed.

01:07:56.950 --> 01:07:57.950
Is that still the case?

01:07:57.950 --> 01:08:00.349
David Liberman: We recently changed it.

01:08:00.349 --> 01:08:05.390
Daniil Liberman: In April, we moved to a new
house and we basically decided to have more

01:08:05.390 --> 01:08:08.700
bedrooms and we have separate bedrooms now.

01:08:08.700 --> 01:08:13.569
David Liberman: Because this behavior creates
some limitations, especially around relationships.

01:08:13.569 --> 01:08:15.130
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, I would imagine so.

01:08:15.130 --> 01:08:20.299
David Liberman: And so we decided that especially
now when it's published, would become an additional

01:08:20.299 --> 01:08:21.299
problem.

01:08:21.299 --> 01:08:24.279
Tim Ferriss: It was already right in The New
Yorker, right?

01:08:24.279 --> 01:08:28.830
I imagine I'm not the first person to ask
you about this.

01:08:28.830 --> 01:08:33.390
David Liberman: So in general our bond is
quite challenging in terms of relationship.

01:08:33.390 --> 01:08:37.410
That's why we decided that we need to make
it easier —

01:08:37.410 --> 01:08:38.410
Daniil Liberman: Like romantic relationship,
he means.

01:08:38.410 --> 01:08:39.410
David Liberman: Yes.

01:08:39.410 --> 01:08:45.830
We decided that we need to explicitly make
it easier for potential romantic partners.

01:08:45.830 --> 01:08:48.440
And splitting bedrooms is one of the first
that is —

01:08:48.440 --> 01:08:49.940
Tim Ferriss: Maybe a key.

01:08:49.940 --> 01:08:51.049
Daniil Liberman: Different floors.

01:08:51.049 --> 01:08:54.929
Like Daniil has his own floor and David has
his own floor in the house, so.

01:08:54.929 --> 01:08:59.901
Tim Ferriss: Oh, I can imagine those in my
mind as I'm painting a picture, I just imagine

01:08:59.901 --> 01:09:05.299
the floors looking very different if the respective
brother floors must be very different.

01:09:05.299 --> 01:09:09.429
David Liberman: But also when we were in the
same room, it was for purpose.

01:09:09.429 --> 01:09:15.700
We usually would stop talking only before
a minute we'll go to sleep and —

01:09:15.700 --> 01:09:17.009
Daniil Liberman: Start talking as we wake
up.

01:09:17.009 --> 01:09:18.679
"So what are we doing today?

01:09:18.679 --> 01:09:20.440
This is a wake-up call."

01:09:20.440 --> 01:09:22.650
David Liberman: So now we're traveling to
New York, we're in one —

01:09:22.650 --> 01:09:24.150
Daniil Liberman: In one part of the room.

01:09:24.150 --> 01:09:25.350
David Liberman: We constantly talk.

01:09:25.350 --> 01:09:27.030
Daniil Liberman: And we constantly talking
nonstop.

01:09:27.030 --> 01:09:34.520
Tim Ferriss: I mean what an incredible blessing
and gift it is that you guys have each other.

01:09:34.520 --> 01:09:38.569
Daniil Liberman: Yeah, I mean we're absolutely
enjoying this way of life.

01:09:38.569 --> 01:09:42.700
Yes, as David said, challenges in the romantic
relationship.

01:09:42.700 --> 01:09:43.700
We will work it out.

01:09:43.700 --> 01:09:46.779
Tim Ferriss: So do you think when you have
romantic partner, let's just say you have

01:09:46.779 --> 01:09:48.739
romantic partners, maybe you have families,
who knows.

01:09:48.739 --> 01:09:50.109
Do you think you guys will still live together?

01:09:50.109 --> 01:09:51.109
David Liberman: Yes.

01:09:51.109 --> 01:09:52.109
Daniil Liberman: Yes.

01:09:52.109 --> 01:09:56.360
David Liberman: We maybe we'll do some is
in our vision.

01:09:56.360 --> 01:10:04.020
We maybe can create special house when you
have sections of private and not private in

01:10:04.020 --> 01:10:06.060
something like that.

01:10:06.060 --> 01:10:11.220
Because we definitely need to hear also what
our partners —

01:10:11.220 --> 01:10:13.120
Tim Ferriss: Partners have to say about things.

01:10:13.120 --> 01:10:14.540
David Liberman: Have to say yes.

01:10:14.540 --> 01:10:19.550
But we really want to still have a place for
the two of us when we can spend a lot of time

01:10:19.550 --> 01:10:20.550
together.

01:10:20.550 --> 01:10:25.969
Tim Ferriss: So I have heard, people need
to fact check this because I think a friend

01:10:25.969 --> 01:10:31.489
of mine told me just as a model, that Tim
Burton and his wife, I think it's Helena Bonham

01:10:31.489 --> 01:10:34.670
Carter, if they're still together, I don't
know, but that in their house they actually

01:10:34.670 --> 01:10:38.550
lived in separate wings and then they would
come together for shared meals and things

01:10:38.550 --> 01:10:39.550
like that.

01:10:39.550 --> 01:10:40.550
So they had a very unorthodox arrangement.

01:10:40.550 --> 01:10:44.679
David Liberman: We have friends who build
houses when they have joint bedroom and —

01:10:44.679 --> 01:10:45.679
Tim Ferriss: Separate bedrooms.

01:10:45.679 --> 01:10:46.679
David Liberman: — separate bedrooms.

01:10:46.679 --> 01:10:47.679
Daniil Liberman: And a wing for kids.

01:10:47.679 --> 01:10:50.360
David Liberman: A wing for kids —
Daniil Liberman: A wing for him, wing for

01:10:50.360 --> 01:10:53.800
her, wing for kids and wing for them together.

01:10:53.800 --> 01:10:55.800
Tim Ferriss: It's like a spaceship.

01:10:55.800 --> 01:10:56.800
I like it.

01:10:56.800 --> 01:10:57.800
I like it.

01:10:57.800 --> 01:10:58.800
I'm very interested in all.

01:10:58.800 --> 01:11:02.720
That's why maybe the divorce lawyers can give
you some advice from Hollywood.

01:11:02.720 --> 01:11:04.090
What has worked?

01:11:04.090 --> 01:11:06.760
What has not worked?

01:11:06.760 --> 01:11:12.190
So you mentioned Evan earlier, not everyone's
going to recognize that name.

01:11:12.190 --> 01:11:14.610
How did you guys end up at Snap?

01:11:14.610 --> 01:11:16.140
Daniil Liberman: How did we end up?

01:11:16.140 --> 01:11:21.310
So our point of view, we always tell this
story from the perspective of us meeting Dalai

01:11:21.310 --> 01:11:22.750
Lama in 2010.

01:11:22.750 --> 01:11:26.270
Tim Ferriss: When in doubt, go with the Dalai
Lama story.

01:11:26.270 --> 01:11:33.489
Daniil Liberman: So it was back in 2009, right
in between us starting the animation studio

01:11:33.489 --> 01:11:37.610
and bankrupting the game studio, there was
a three months of us —

01:11:37.610 --> 01:11:39.910
Tim Ferriss: Took a Dalai Lama sabbatical.

01:11:39.910 --> 01:11:45.239
Daniil Liberman: Yeah, there was three months
of us clearly not knowing what to do and then

01:11:45.239 --> 01:11:47.240
the bandits are threatening our lives.

01:11:47.240 --> 01:11:49.680
We don't don't know how to return the money.

01:11:49.680 --> 01:11:57.580
And then we just decided to go for a five,
six days meditation retreat of Rinpoche Tibetan

01:11:57.580 --> 01:12:01.350
lama who just arrived to Moscow and a friend
invited us.

01:12:01.350 --> 01:12:06.300
So we came there with no expectations whatsoever.

01:12:06.300 --> 01:12:10.320
But the gentleman was very interesting.

01:12:10.320 --> 01:12:15.240
He was young, he was super smart, speaking
like 11 languages.

01:12:15.240 --> 01:12:21.150
And from time to time he would switch from
this Tibetan stories about the enlightened

01:12:21.150 --> 01:12:29.340
kingdoms to something more practical, even
claiming that a single person can actually

01:12:29.340 --> 01:12:30.870
change the world if they want.

01:12:30.870 --> 01:12:34.219
And for us it was like, wait, what?

01:12:34.219 --> 01:12:36.310
Are you talking to us?

01:12:36.310 --> 01:12:42.719
And we then asked if we can have an audience
with the Rinpoche.

01:12:42.719 --> 01:12:46.630
And we were told that everything is booked
already.

01:12:46.630 --> 01:12:48.770
But five minutes later he called us.

01:12:48.770 --> 01:12:51.110
Tim Ferriss: Now, just so I'm clear.

01:12:51.110 --> 01:12:53.699
So he is which teacher?

01:12:53.699 --> 01:12:57.929
Daniil Liberman: Rinpoche is like a Tibetan
—

01:12:57.929 --> 01:12:58.929
Tim Ferriss: Spiritual.

01:12:58.929 --> 01:12:59.929
Daniil Liberman: — spiritual, yes.

01:12:59.929 --> 01:13:05.320
David Liberman: It's like a title which you
can get only from your childhood if you recognize

01:13:05.320 --> 01:13:07.489
—
Daniil Liberman: He's recognized as the —

01:13:07.489 --> 01:13:08.890
Tim Ferriss: Reincarnation of someone else.

01:13:08.890 --> 01:13:09.890
Daniil Liberman: Yes.

01:13:09.890 --> 01:13:16.150
So this gentleman is in Moscow and Rinpoche
is asking us to come see him.

01:13:16.150 --> 01:13:17.650
And we're like, wait, okay, cool.

01:13:17.650 --> 01:13:21.360
So we —
David Liberman: Shared our ideas about transparency,

01:13:21.360 --> 01:13:30.110
about how in the future we can leave within
governments without physical borders and for

01:13:30.110 --> 01:13:35.060
Tibetan peoples it's really important subjects
knowing that they don't have their land.

01:13:35.060 --> 01:13:36.060
Daniil Liberman: So it resonated with him.

01:13:36.060 --> 01:13:40.720
And when we finished our story by saying,
"And now it's just two of us," he immediately

01:13:40.720 --> 01:13:48.199
abrupted us and is like the three of us and
it was touching and unexpected and he finished

01:13:48.199 --> 01:13:54.620
his phrase with, "Fly, come visit me in India
in…" what was it?

01:13:54.620 --> 01:14:01.260
"...January next year, I am hosting an integration
of a monastery.

01:14:01.260 --> 01:14:05.260
Dalai Lama going to be there and I will introduce
you and we'll introduce your ideas together

01:14:05.260 --> 01:14:08.110
and we'll see what we can do together."

01:14:08.110 --> 01:14:11.820
That meeting actually happened and not happened
at the same time.

01:14:11.820 --> 01:14:17.470
So we flew to India, we spent this week in
the monastery in India, Tibetan monastery,

01:14:17.470 --> 01:14:19.989
Dalai Lama actually arrived.

01:14:19.989 --> 01:14:27.320
We had a non-private meeting with him in a
group of 10 European donors of the monastery.

01:14:27.320 --> 01:14:32.650
But then we had to have a one-on-one meeting
with him next morning, which never happened

01:14:32.650 --> 01:14:35.550
because the storm came.

01:14:35.550 --> 01:14:37.440
I know this is like a movie story.

01:14:37.440 --> 01:14:40.880
The storm came and Dalai Lama immediately
took off —

01:14:40.880 --> 01:14:43.440
David Liberman: Was packed in to helicopter
—

01:14:43.440 --> 01:14:47.310
Daniil Liberman: To the helicopter, the military
helicopter and just disappeared.

01:14:47.310 --> 01:14:53.190
And then we're standing there and in half
hour the storm just rushed and flooded the

01:14:53.190 --> 01:14:54.380
entire valley —
David Liberman: For the next two days —

01:14:54.380 --> 01:14:57.620
Daniil Liberman: For the next two days we
weren't able to leave the place because it

01:14:57.620 --> 01:14:59.239
was flooded.

01:14:59.239 --> 01:15:07.719
But then a month later we received an email
from a gentleman whose name is Jerry Murdock,

01:15:07.719 --> 01:15:12.130
he's the founder of Insight —
David Liberman: Partners, one of the biggest

01:15:12.130 --> 01:15:13.949
VC firms in New York.

01:15:13.949 --> 01:15:16.850
Daniil Liberman: And the email was like —
David Liberman: "I'm at the dinner…"

01:15:16.850 --> 01:15:22.620
Daniil Liberman: "I'm at the dinner with Dalai
Lama and this other monk and they told me

01:15:22.620 --> 01:15:24.699
I have to email you and they give me your
email.

01:15:24.699 --> 01:15:26.570
So that's what I do."

01:15:26.570 --> 01:15:28.060
Tim Ferriss: "That's why I'm emailing."

01:15:28.060 --> 01:15:29.800
Daniil Liberman: "That's why I'm emailing
you.

01:15:29.800 --> 01:15:33.110
We met this team few months and we become
close friends."

01:15:33.110 --> 01:15:38.520
David Liberman: And eventually through the
many different stories, he invited us to a

01:15:38.520 --> 01:15:39.520
dinner.

01:15:39.520 --> 01:15:42.270
The dinner wasn't really for us.

01:15:42.270 --> 01:15:45.670
He thought that other people will come, but
it didn't work.

01:15:45.670 --> 01:15:51.850
So he invited us instead and —
Tim Ferriss: Whatever gets the job done.

01:15:51.850 --> 01:15:53.200
David Liberman: Yes.

01:15:53.200 --> 01:16:03.750
But since this introduction, we always were
quite cautious about everything which —

01:16:03.750 --> 01:16:05.750
Daniil Liberman: Was related to him.

01:16:05.750 --> 01:16:07.401
I mean Dalai Lama introduced you to someone
—

01:16:07.401 --> 01:16:08.590
David Liberman: Could be the reason.

01:16:08.590 --> 01:16:10.580
Daniil Liberman: So there should be a reason.

01:16:10.580 --> 01:16:16.580
And we always try to help him and be around
and just see where it'll come, where it'll

01:16:16.580 --> 01:16:17.580
go.

01:16:17.580 --> 01:16:18.580
Tim Ferriss: You're talking about Jerry?

01:16:18.580 --> 01:16:19.580
Daniil Liberman: Jerry.

01:16:19.580 --> 01:16:20.580
Yes.

01:16:20.580 --> 01:16:23.150
And at some point eventually he introduced
us to Evan at his house and then Evan gave

01:16:23.150 --> 01:16:25.540
me his phone number just right away at the
meeting.

01:16:25.540 --> 01:16:27.440
David Liberman: And it was the right moment
—

01:16:27.440 --> 01:16:31.800
Daniil Liberman: Which wasn't expected for
everyone around because that's not what he's

01:16:31.800 --> 01:16:32.800
doing.

01:16:32.800 --> 01:16:37.250
David Liberman: And we were developing technologies
around 3D avatars —

01:16:37.250 --> 01:16:42.469
Daniil Liberman: Computer vision —
David Liberman: — at that moment.

01:16:42.469 --> 01:16:46.380
And that's what was exactly what he wanted
—

01:16:46.380 --> 01:16:47.610
Tim Ferriss: On his mind.

01:16:47.610 --> 01:16:54.760
Daniil Liberman: On his mind was he wanted
to combine a bitmoji, which were the 2D avatars

01:16:54.760 --> 01:17:04.030
he acquired from the Canadian company and
3D engine of AR technology of Luxury, the

01:17:04.030 --> 01:17:05.840
Ukrainian company he acquired.

01:17:05.840 --> 01:17:13.780
He wanted to merge the two technologies, and
our experience in Hollywood was merging technologies.

01:17:13.780 --> 01:17:19.050
We literally were transferring the 2D styles
of existing animated shows into 3D and back

01:17:19.050 --> 01:17:20.380
and force and back and forth.

01:17:20.380 --> 01:17:24.370
David Liberman: In that moment, we're also
focused on 3D avatars and how technology can

01:17:24.370 --> 01:17:28.070
actually not a show, not something which require
—

01:17:28.070 --> 01:17:30.180
Daniil Liberman: Game type of thinking.

01:17:30.180 --> 01:17:35.960
Because we decided right after the financial
transparency for nonprofits didn't work, we

01:17:35.960 --> 01:17:41.150
decided that we looked around and realized
that the technology advanced like a computer

01:17:41.150 --> 01:17:49.429
vision and style transfers and all this stuff
advanced a lot since we left the CGA graphics

01:17:49.429 --> 01:17:53.820
and we realized that we know everything and
more than probably everyone in the field.

01:17:53.820 --> 01:17:59.650
So we can try to just start something and
probably going to be going to build a successful

01:17:59.650 --> 01:18:00.650
company.

01:18:00.650 --> 01:18:05.469
Half a year later, Evan offered us, not offered,
but already acquired us.

01:18:05.469 --> 01:18:11.159
Tim Ferriss: And so just for people who might
not have the acronym, so AR, augmented reality.

01:18:11.159 --> 01:18:16.800
So people may recognize some of the current
or recent iterations on Snap where you can,

01:18:16.800 --> 01:18:23.500
say, put any number of different sort of overlays
of characters.

01:18:23.500 --> 01:18:29.199
I mean they do partnerships with all sorts
of companies and so on so that you can add

01:18:29.199 --> 01:18:35.280
something to your face that sort of tracks
your eye movement and augments the kind of

01:18:35.280 --> 01:18:36.280
visual experience.

01:18:36.280 --> 01:18:39.150
So is that what you guys were working on in
some capacity?

01:18:39.150 --> 01:18:44.530
David Liberman: This technology, the face-tracking
technology, it was Ukrainian company Luxury,

01:18:44.530 --> 01:18:48.170
which they recently acquired before —
Tim Ferriss: Snapchat acquired.

01:18:48.170 --> 01:18:49.170
David Liberman: — us.

01:18:49.170 --> 01:18:55.340
And we worked how to combine the avatars into
this technology.So right now, 3D version of

01:18:55.340 --> 01:19:00.520
bitmojis is the most used avatars daily in
Snapchat.

01:19:00.520 --> 01:19:05.770
It's already everyone in your profile and
games in lenses in all places.

01:19:05.770 --> 01:19:14.449
Tim Ferriss: And so you guys get acquired
and, if I'm missing a chapter, let me know,

01:19:14.449 --> 01:19:23.320
but can you describe what you did, what your
analysis looked like when Snapchat suddenly

01:19:23.320 --> 01:19:24.370
started losing users?

01:19:24.370 --> 01:19:25.370
I think it was in 2017.

01:19:25.370 --> 01:19:26.370
David Liberman: 2018.

01:19:26.370 --> 01:19:27.370
Tim Ferriss: 18.

01:19:27.370 --> 01:19:30.100
David Liberman: So it was early 2018.

01:19:30.100 --> 01:19:32.560
Tim Ferriss: Are you right?

01:19:32.560 --> 01:19:33.560
Yes.

01:19:33.560 --> 01:19:39.341
David Liberman: 2017, Instagram released their
stories and in the beginning of 2018, new

01:19:39.341 --> 01:19:43.920
version, new update version of Snapchat was
the list.

01:19:43.920 --> 01:19:50.151
Kim Kardashian tweeted or Kylie Jenner tweets
that no one would use Snapchat anymore.

01:19:50.151 --> 01:19:54.080
All the stuff and audience started to decline.

01:19:54.080 --> 01:19:58.020
And at that moment most of the people believed
—

01:19:58.020 --> 01:20:02.210
Daniil Liberman: Internal idea in the company
was that it's due to the —

01:20:02.210 --> 01:20:03.210
David Liberman: Competition.

01:20:03.210 --> 01:20:04.210
Tim Ferriss: Public sentiment, competition.

01:20:04.210 --> 01:20:07.969
Daniil Liberman: Public sentiment of the bad
new version or competition of —

01:20:07.969 --> 01:20:08.989
David Liberman: Instagram.

01:20:08.989 --> 01:20:12.940
Daniil Liberman: — Instagram and TikTok
or altogether.

01:20:12.940 --> 01:20:19.520
While our analysis of data showed us that
it's not true.

01:20:19.520 --> 01:20:27.370
And then the major reason for the decline
of the audience was related to the fact that

01:20:27.370 --> 01:20:35.740
Android version of Snapchat after the update
became so slow that people over time developed,

01:20:35.740 --> 01:20:38.370
not developed, but gave up.

01:20:38.370 --> 01:20:39.980
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, they just abandoned.

01:20:39.980 --> 01:20:40.980
Daniil Liberman: They just abandoned.

01:20:40.980 --> 01:20:41.980
Started abandoning.

01:20:41.980 --> 01:20:43.970
David Liberman: Yes —
Daniil Liberman: 10 percent of the slowest

01:20:43.970 --> 01:20:51.370
devices of Android users could have experienced
up to 40 seconds before the feed with stories

01:20:51.370 --> 01:20:52.370
would load.

01:20:52.370 --> 01:20:55.199
David Liberman: Next time when you will open
some feed like count to 40.

01:20:55.199 --> 01:20:56.449
Tim Ferriss: 40 seconds.

01:20:56.449 --> 01:20:57.449
No way.

01:20:57.449 --> 01:20:58.610
I'll barely wait for four seconds.

01:20:58.610 --> 01:20:59.610
Daniil Liberman: Exactly.

01:20:59.610 --> 01:21:03.699
So it was so obvious that this was the reason,
but it took some time to —

01:21:03.699 --> 01:21:11.140
David Liberman: We realized that we didn't
have any specific position related to that

01:21:11.140 --> 01:21:17.130
in the company and we realized that what we
can do is actually to just bring all the insights

01:21:17.130 --> 01:21:21.830
which we were able to extract to Evan —
Daniil Liberman: The engineering team to Evan

01:21:21.830 --> 01:21:24.940
—
David Liberman: — and to just give him all

01:21:24.940 --> 01:21:36.540
the evidences so that more focus can be added
to the problem and to making the app faster.

01:21:36.540 --> 01:21:40.380
Tim Ferriss: Now why do you think you guys
spotted this, right?

01:21:40.380 --> 01:21:46.050
Because it doesn't sound like it's immediately
your department or responsibilities?

01:21:46.050 --> 01:21:54.860
Was the narrative that it was the Jenners
and the Kim Kardashians and then the competition,

01:21:54.860 --> 01:21:59.739
was that so accepted at face value that people
weren't looking at the data or what happened?

01:21:59.739 --> 01:22:02.820
David Liberman: So there were several reasons
that first of all, it's Android.

01:22:02.820 --> 01:22:03.820
Daniil Liberman: Android.

01:22:03.820 --> 01:22:10.219
David Liberman: We're Android users and among
executives it was a really rare things to

01:22:10.219 --> 01:22:12.260
—
Daniil Liberman: To actually use Android date.

01:22:12.260 --> 01:22:14.340
David Liberman: To use Android day to day.

01:22:14.340 --> 01:22:22.600
Even he pushed himself to use Android to experience
this all what his users usually experience

01:22:22.600 --> 01:22:23.690
when they use Android.

01:22:23.690 --> 01:22:26.590
But we use Androids as a day-to-day devices.

01:22:26.590 --> 01:22:30.010
We hackers, we don't like limitations of Apple
App stores.

01:22:30.010 --> 01:22:31.300
Daniil Liberman: Closed systems.

01:22:31.300 --> 01:22:33.239
David Liberman: And closed up ecosystems.

01:22:33.239 --> 01:22:35.030
That's why we always were there.

01:22:35.030 --> 01:22:39.540
So when we developed our 3D avatars, it was
the first —

01:22:39.540 --> 01:22:42.610
Daniil Liberman: It worked, it worked further
faster on Android than on iOS.

01:22:42.610 --> 01:22:46.330
David Liberman: Android than iOS, and it was
quite unique to compared to other products.

01:22:46.330 --> 01:22:50.770
And through this experience we learned a lot
about some limitations and we already at that

01:22:50.770 --> 01:22:54.409
moment looked at some data just for our products
to be launched properly.

01:22:54.409 --> 01:22:59.420
Daniil Liberman: We wanted to fix Snapchat's
Android app earlier just for our product to

01:22:59.420 --> 01:23:00.420
work better.

01:23:00.420 --> 01:23:05.739
David Liberman: And so we had already some
predispositions and beliefs related to that.

01:23:05.739 --> 01:23:10.060
Daniil Liberman: Then, when the product was
launched, we decided to research everything

01:23:10.060 --> 01:23:14.820
about the user behavior in order to understand
how we can better position our product inside

01:23:14.820 --> 01:23:16.210
of the Snapchat ecosystem.

01:23:16.210 --> 01:23:18.040
David Liberman: And with such problems.

01:23:18.040 --> 01:23:21.610
The challenge, there are different methodologies.

01:23:21.610 --> 01:23:24.210
For example, there is a methodology to A/B
tests.

01:23:24.210 --> 01:23:28.960
When you launch, half of the users will see
one version and half of users will see —

01:23:28.960 --> 01:23:30.639
Tim Ferriss: Different one.

01:23:30.639 --> 01:23:34.120
David Liberman: A different version, and then
you will compare the results.

01:23:34.120 --> 01:23:42.010
But with performance degradation, the result
is delayed.

01:23:42.010 --> 01:23:43.350
It's not immediate.

01:23:43.350 --> 01:23:45.639
You cannot really, it's really hard to —
Tim Ferriss: It's not apples to apples.

01:23:45.639 --> 01:23:46.639
David Liberman: Yes.

01:23:46.639 --> 01:23:48.120
So you need to wait for some time.

01:23:48.120 --> 01:23:51.690
You need data for longer period of time —
Daniil Liberman: And more or less you need

01:23:51.690 --> 01:23:58.520
to break it, give your users a bad version,
wait for them to use it for a long enough

01:23:58.520 --> 01:24:00.900
period for you to see the difference in behavior.

01:24:00.900 --> 01:24:02.510
Tim Ferriss: Sounds like a tough assignment.

01:24:02.510 --> 01:24:03.510
David Liberman: Yes.

01:24:03.510 --> 01:24:06.150
Evan's decision, moral decision, was never
do that.

01:24:06.150 --> 01:24:12.280
So you shouldn't really make the experience
of users worse intentionally.

01:24:12.280 --> 01:24:18.950
Daniil Liberman: And as hackers, for us it
was like, okay, can we find a way how to get

01:24:18.950 --> 01:24:20.239
this data anyway?

01:24:20.239 --> 01:24:22.880
So we started to look at all the previous
bugs.

01:24:22.880 --> 01:24:24.110
David Liberman: Accidental, accidental.

01:24:24.110 --> 01:24:25.110
Tim Ferriss: Mistakes.

01:24:25.110 --> 01:24:26.110
David Liberman: Degradations.

01:24:26.110 --> 01:24:31.270
Daniil Liberman: Which led to and then was
discovered, but lasted for long enough so

01:24:31.270 --> 01:24:34.270
that we can actually extract the change in
behavior data.

01:24:34.270 --> 01:24:41.410
David Liberman: Still it's really important
to note in the article in The New Yorker,

01:24:41.410 --> 01:24:42.550
the words not from us.

01:24:42.550 --> 01:24:46.250
That's why they are not as clear.

01:24:46.250 --> 01:24:52.530
There were people inside of Snap who also
believe in this idea, not only us.

01:24:52.530 --> 01:24:55.381
Daniil Liberman: It was basically their initiative,
we just —

01:24:55.381 --> 01:24:57.450
David Liberman: There were not enough momentum.

01:24:57.450 --> 01:24:59.300
Daniil Liberman: And arguments.

01:24:59.300 --> 01:25:03.590
David Liberman: Certainly not enough people
who would work on this problem.

01:25:03.590 --> 01:25:09.350
They were really brilliant engineers who already
before that started to rewrite that Android

01:25:09.350 --> 01:25:10.350
application.

01:25:10.350 --> 01:25:13.170
Daniil Liberman: And we had our daily meeting
with Evan, so we actually could have —

01:25:13.170 --> 01:25:14.420
Tim Ferriss: You could be good ambassadors.

01:25:14.420 --> 01:25:15.420
Daniil Liberman: Yes.

01:25:15.420 --> 01:25:18.630
David Liberman: So we used our previously
earned —

01:25:18.630 --> 01:25:19.920
Tim Ferriss: Like social capital.

01:25:19.920 --> 01:25:20.960
David Liberman: Social capital.

01:25:20.960 --> 01:25:23.300
Daniil Liberman: Social capital of building
this avatar product.

01:25:23.300 --> 01:25:27.050
David Liberman: We need to just spend all
the social capital on this problem, otherwise

01:25:27.050 --> 01:25:34.180
its shares dropped to five dollars per share
from 20 in the entire computation of ours.

01:25:34.180 --> 01:25:35.280
Tim Ferriss: In the equity, of course.

01:25:35.280 --> 01:25:36.280
David Liberman: In equity of course.

01:25:36.280 --> 01:25:39.540
And also we saw the opportunity.

01:25:39.540 --> 01:25:47.179
For us, we got through two major crises in
our life, not counting the childhood crisis,

01:25:47.179 --> 01:25:48.360
financial crisis.

01:25:48.360 --> 01:25:53.300
That's why somehow for us, when it is a crisis
for us, it's a great times.

01:25:53.300 --> 01:25:56.600
So it’s like —
Daniil Liberman: Now we can show how we can

01:25:56.600 --> 01:25:59.139
—
Tim Ferriss: Never let a good crisis go to

01:25:59.139 --> 01:26:00.139
waste.

01:26:00.139 --> 01:26:01.369
Daniil Liberman: Yes, exactly.

01:26:01.369 --> 01:26:06.300
And at some point we just had to show that
we are good engineers as well.

01:26:06.300 --> 01:26:15.020
So David, just in order to challenge the status
quo, David had to sit down and fix camera.

01:26:15.020 --> 01:26:21.159
I mean camera, Snapchat is camera and David
made it load, what was it?

01:26:21.159 --> 01:26:22.520
David Liberman: Twice as fast.

01:26:22.520 --> 01:26:23.880
Daniil Liberman: Twice as fast.

01:26:23.880 --> 01:26:25.340
David Liberman: Like not load.

01:26:25.340 --> 01:26:28.130
When you press the button, how fast the picture
would be ready.

01:26:28.130 --> 01:26:31.620
I showed that it can be —
Daniil Liberman: The speed can be doubled,

01:26:31.620 --> 01:26:33.929
and it was like, "Oh, wow.

01:26:33.929 --> 01:26:37.010
So yes, your guys also are great engineers
as well.

01:26:37.010 --> 01:26:39.350
So how did you do that?"

01:26:39.350 --> 01:26:45.000
And we showed some of the principles of how
you can find inefficiencies in the code.

01:26:45.000 --> 01:26:49.100
David Liberman: But still it's really important
in the sense that it was a really team effort

01:26:49.100 --> 01:26:53.239
of great engineers actually was part of this
process.

01:26:53.239 --> 01:26:56.369
What we see is our contribution, this kind
of promotion, this idea.

01:26:56.369 --> 01:26:58.520
Daniil Liberman: Their voice was heard.

01:26:58.520 --> 01:27:05.090
David Liberman: And bringing this voice to
the decision makers so that they also will

01:27:05.090 --> 01:27:09.700
be empowered with data to then thread it to
the entire organization.

01:27:09.700 --> 01:27:14.760
And that was definitely really great times
and it worked.

01:27:14.760 --> 01:27:19.489
And even though Snapchat has some challenges
right now with —

01:27:19.489 --> 01:27:20.970
Daniil Liberman: Revenue.

01:27:20.970 --> 01:27:25.540
David Liberman: Financial side of things,
but with audience since then it's growing.

01:27:25.540 --> 01:27:26.540
Daniil Liberman: The audience is growing.

01:27:26.540 --> 01:27:28.020
David Liberman: They recently reached like
800 million.

01:27:28.020 --> 01:27:31.630
Daniil Liberman: The audience is growing in
the countries where the Androids are even

01:27:31.630 --> 01:27:32.630
slower.

01:27:32.630 --> 01:27:35.520
So the app actually is pretty significant
still.

01:27:35.520 --> 01:27:39.250
David Liberman: It's still right now in terms
of Android apps, it's still one of the fastest

01:27:39.250 --> 01:27:42.239
apps to load compared to the major apps outside.

01:27:42.239 --> 01:27:48.980
Daniil Liberman: What was interesting as well,
so launching a new updated version only happened

01:27:48.980 --> 01:27:52.739
when in March-ish of 2019.

01:27:52.739 --> 01:27:59.850
But it was also important and we put lots
of efforts into this program more than the

01:27:59.850 --> 01:28:02.650
other one to fix the existing version.

01:28:02.650 --> 01:28:10.300
Before the new was released, the old had to
be fixed in order to stop bleeding.

01:28:10.300 --> 01:28:12.179
And by December the bleeding stopped.

01:28:12.179 --> 01:28:13.760
David Liberman: You can see it's from data.

01:28:13.760 --> 01:28:16.420
Daniil Liberman: And in January the audience
started to grow.

01:28:16.420 --> 01:28:17.820
David Liberman: Even before the new version
—

01:28:17.820 --> 01:28:20.190
Daniil Liberman: And in March, the new version
was released.

01:28:20.190 --> 01:28:22.150
It never actually slowed down since then.

01:28:22.150 --> 01:28:27.800
Tim Ferriss: So I have a question about this
type of data-driven analysis.

01:28:27.800 --> 01:28:35.280
Do you attempt to do this type of analysis
with goals or challenges that you have in

01:28:35.280 --> 01:28:37.180
your personal lives?

01:28:37.180 --> 01:28:38.730
Do you try to apply it everywhere?

01:28:38.730 --> 01:28:41.650
Are there examples that you could give outside
of the business context?

01:28:41.650 --> 01:28:46.340
David Liberman: First of all, we should know
that we, as Daniil mentioned about intuition,

01:28:46.340 --> 01:28:47.350
we believe in both.

01:28:47.350 --> 01:28:49.610
We believe in intuition, we believe in data
approach.

01:28:49.610 --> 01:29:00.659
But yes, so for example, one was a major principle
is that we know what is compound growth.

01:29:00.659 --> 01:29:09.550
And for example, when we have some crazy goals,
crazy for some people, we will set some goals

01:29:09.550 --> 01:29:12.929
and these goals seems to be impossible.

01:29:12.929 --> 01:29:21.020
What we show again and again that actually
if you have KPI, for example, number of followers.

01:29:21.020 --> 01:29:23.830
Daniil Liberman: If you have —
David Liberman: Goals, for example, number

01:29:23.830 --> 01:29:27.760
of followers or a number of dollars in your
pocket.

01:29:27.760 --> 01:29:28.760
Daniil Liberman: Quantify.

01:29:28.760 --> 01:29:29.760
David Liberman: Quantifiable things.

01:29:29.760 --> 01:29:30.760
Tim Ferriss: Quantifiable key performance.

01:29:30.760 --> 01:29:38.719
David Liberman: What you can do is that you
can evaluate what you have right now and then

01:29:38.719 --> 01:29:40.739
if you can multiply this number two.

01:29:40.739 --> 01:29:43.150
Daniil Liberman: If you can double.

01:29:43.150 --> 01:29:45.260
David Liberman: Two times a year actually.

01:29:45.260 --> 01:29:48.380
Daniil Liberman: Every year, if you can double
your results.

01:29:48.380 --> 01:29:50.520
David Liberman: It is a greatness.

01:29:50.520 --> 01:29:54.610
You have the entire money on Earth, the entire
—

01:29:54.610 --> 01:29:55.610
Daniil Liberman: Population on Earth.

01:29:55.610 --> 01:29:57.610
David Liberman: Population of following, et
cetera.

01:29:57.610 --> 01:30:02.330
So it's really important then when you have
this big goals, you can have any big goal

01:30:02.330 --> 01:30:04.160
with this approach.

01:30:04.160 --> 01:30:06.040
You just —
Daniil Liberman: You need to run faster than

01:30:06.040 --> 01:30:07.040
—
David Liberman: Doubling.

01:30:07.040 --> 01:30:08.550
Daniil Liberman: Two times every year.

01:30:08.550 --> 01:30:11.500
David Liberman: Actually what we realized
that there is this corridor.

01:30:11.500 --> 01:30:17.460
Daniil Liberman: There’s probably a physical
limitation at the top, which is around 2.4.

01:30:17.460 --> 01:30:25.090
You cannot run faster than increasing your
results 2.4 times a year in a —

01:30:25.090 --> 01:30:26.090
David Liberman: Long.

01:30:26.090 --> 01:30:27.090
Daniil Liberman: Long run.

01:30:27.090 --> 01:30:30.480
David Liberman: But what we realized that
actually it's really tiny corridor.

01:30:30.480 --> 01:30:36.300
If you increase this number 1.6 times, you're
successful.

01:30:36.300 --> 01:30:41.219
If you increase this number 2.4 times, you
are —

01:30:41.219 --> 01:30:42.219
Daniil Liberman: You're superhuman.

01:30:42.219 --> 01:30:43.219
David Liberman: Superhuman.

01:30:43.219 --> 01:30:44.219
Daniil Liberman: You're like, godlike.

01:30:44.219 --> 01:30:46.239
David Liberman: So this is only as a small
corridor, you don't need to think about something

01:30:46.239 --> 01:30:47.239
faster.

01:30:47.239 --> 01:30:50.719
If it's —
Daniil Liberman: Anything slower, it just

01:30:50.719 --> 01:30:51.719
like a normal life.

01:30:51.719 --> 01:30:52.719
David Liberman: Slower, it will not be success.

01:30:52.719 --> 01:30:54.159
Tim Ferriss: Now this is multiplying your
KPI by 1.7 times every year.

01:30:54.159 --> 01:30:55.159
Daniil Liberman: Every year.

01:30:55.159 --> 01:30:56.159
David Liberman: Every year.

01:30:56.159 --> 01:30:57.159
Tim Ferriss: Every year.

01:30:57.159 --> 01:30:58.159
Once per year.

01:30:58.159 --> 01:30:59.159
David Liberman: Once per year.

01:30:59.159 --> 01:31:00.159
Daniil Liberman: Once per year.

01:31:00.159 --> 01:31:02.989
David Liberman: And we applied it a lot in
our life, in our business life, in the life

01:31:02.989 --> 01:31:05.520
of our companies, in our personal life.

01:31:05.520 --> 01:31:07.219
So we use this methodology.

01:31:07.219 --> 01:31:08.219
Tim Ferriss: How do you apply it?

01:31:08.219 --> 01:31:10.360
What would be an example from the personal
life?

01:31:10.360 --> 01:31:13.870
Maybe it doesn't have to follow that doubling
heuristic.

01:31:13.870 --> 01:31:16.450
It could, but what might be an example?

01:31:16.450 --> 01:31:22.070
David Liberman: But for example, amount of
hours you spent with as a romantic partner,

01:31:22.070 --> 01:31:24.250
you inspired by.

01:31:24.250 --> 01:31:32.750
Tim Ferriss: And so you would track the hours
and then look to multiply that over time?

01:31:32.750 --> 01:31:33.860
David Liberman: Yes.

01:31:33.860 --> 01:31:36.130
So you shouldn't expect that immediately.

01:31:36.130 --> 01:31:40.330
You would start a relationship which would
be perfect and you will spend all the time

01:31:40.330 --> 01:31:43.409
and all this time would be perfect and great,
et cetera.

01:31:43.409 --> 01:31:50.949
But if you have one hour of this experience
a year, the next year it can be two hours

01:31:50.949 --> 01:31:52.121
as the next year it can be four hours.

01:31:52.121 --> 01:31:53.800
Daniil Liberman: For some it might sound like
—

01:31:53.800 --> 01:31:56.780
David Liberman: In 10 years you have the entire
life like that.

01:31:56.780 --> 01:31:58.420
Tim Ferriss: Slow build.

01:31:58.420 --> 01:32:00.060
David Liberman: Slow build.

01:32:00.060 --> 01:32:05.220
And we just realized that when you try to
run faster, you can actually break yourself.

01:32:05.220 --> 01:32:06.770
Tim Ferriss: I see what you're saying.

01:32:06.770 --> 01:32:10.300
So you can make a mistake by saying, "Okay,
I'm going to spend half of my time this year

01:32:10.300 --> 01:32:16.940
with this person," because you're sort of
biting off more than you can chew and maybe

01:32:16.940 --> 01:32:19.969
shooting yourself in the foot for the long
term.

01:32:19.969 --> 01:32:21.480
Daniil Liberman: Yes.

01:32:21.480 --> 01:32:22.570
Tim Ferriss: Okay.

01:32:22.570 --> 01:32:27.070
So basically having an eye towards growth
but not being excessively ambitious in the

01:32:27.070 --> 01:32:29.210
beginning because you'll break things.

01:32:29.210 --> 01:32:30.210
Daniil Liberman: Yes.

01:32:30.210 --> 01:32:31.429
I know on personal experience, yes.

01:32:31.429 --> 01:32:36.429
This is like when you run too much right away,
you break stuff.

01:32:36.429 --> 01:32:42.340
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, I can see that as someone
who's suffered a lot of physical breakage

01:32:42.340 --> 01:32:43.340
also.

01:32:43.340 --> 01:32:47.489
David Liberman: But even with the acceleration,
when you're in the rockets flying into space,

01:32:47.489 --> 01:32:50.380
there is this limitation of three —
Daniil Liberman: 3G.

01:32:50.380 --> 01:32:57.030
David Liberman: G, three times G, which then
actually start breaking stuff in yourself.

01:32:57.030 --> 01:32:59.969
So it seems that it's kind of natural number.

01:32:59.969 --> 01:33:03.909
This is what we can sustain like 2.4 like
to 1.6.

01:33:03.909 --> 01:33:09.420
This is something which we can sustain physically
and mentally and you can achieve everything.

01:33:09.420 --> 01:33:11.573
Daniil Liberman: And you can honestly you
can find it anywhere.

01:33:11.573 --> 01:33:19.231
If you want to play music, just start from
something achievable right now today, double

01:33:19.231 --> 01:33:20.480
your result every year.

01:33:20.480 --> 01:33:22.390
David Liberman: And you will become like Beyoncé.

01:33:22.390 --> 01:33:24.060
Daniil Liberman: You will soon become superstar.

01:33:24.060 --> 01:33:25.060
Tim Ferriss: Superstars.

01:33:25.060 --> 01:33:26.060
Daniil Liberman: Superstar.

01:33:26.060 --> 01:33:27.720
Tim Ferriss: Certainly true with physical
activity too.

01:33:27.720 --> 01:33:32.440
If you think about say weightlifting or whatever,
you don't need to 10X a number every year.

01:33:32.440 --> 01:33:33.670
And if you try —
Daniil Liberman: You want 10X.

01:33:33.670 --> 01:33:34.670
You can try.

01:33:34.670 --> 01:33:37.159
Tim Ferriss: If you try it like I've tried,
then you start damaging your connective tissue

01:33:37.159 --> 01:33:38.159
or needing surgeries.

01:33:38.159 --> 01:33:42.611
David Liberman: I remember that was one of
the Olympic teams, was it in UK or something

01:33:42.611 --> 01:33:43.611
like that?

01:33:43.611 --> 01:33:48.630
I don't remember exactly, but they got a new
tech trainer and the new trainer was —

01:33:48.630 --> 01:33:49.630
Daniil Liberman: A coach.

01:33:49.630 --> 01:33:54.310
David Liberman: Coach was with this approach,
but the approach was like we need to improve

01:33:54.310 --> 01:33:57.320
our KPIs one percent a week.

01:33:57.320 --> 01:33:58.320
It's almost the same.

01:33:58.320 --> 01:34:00.219
And they become champion from nowhere.

01:34:00.219 --> 01:34:03.870
Daniil Liberman: Years later they become champion
from nowhere.

01:34:03.870 --> 01:34:06.020
The last position on the list they become
champions.

01:34:06.020 --> 01:34:10.139
David Liberman: So but physical activity is
the same and business activity, money, finance.

01:34:10.139 --> 01:34:12.460
Daniil Liberman: Writing books, music.

01:34:12.460 --> 01:34:15.410
David Liberman: But really important is that
you can achieve anything.

01:34:15.410 --> 01:34:23.040
You can have any type of mission or big goals,
but then you draw this course for yourself

01:34:23.040 --> 01:34:25.280
—
Tim Ferriss: As long as you're consistent.

01:34:25.280 --> 01:34:26.910
David Liberman: And be consistent.

01:34:26.910 --> 01:34:36.550
Tim Ferriss: So what are some of the maybe
logistics challenges and how have you addressed

01:34:36.550 --> 01:34:41.880
them in running your businesses, how you run
them or organizing your lives, the way that

01:34:41.880 --> 01:34:43.560
you guys organize them?

01:34:43.560 --> 01:34:46.730
Daniil Liberman: We have a very messed up
calendar.

01:34:46.730 --> 01:34:48.980
It's like multidimensional.

01:34:48.980 --> 01:34:54.270
Tim Ferriss: Does that mean that you're just
traveling all over the place?

01:34:54.270 --> 01:34:57.060
Do you have —
David Liberman: We travel a lot for sure.

01:34:57.060 --> 01:34:58.370
Daniil Liberman: We travel a lot.

01:34:58.370 --> 01:35:05.800
We probably this year we're in the States
for three months all over the year.

01:35:05.800 --> 01:35:12.370
But at home in L.A., even less maybe than
that, maybe one week a month, not more than

01:35:12.370 --> 01:35:13.370
that.

01:35:13.370 --> 01:35:18.001
David Liberman: But what helped a lot, it
started with two of us, but quite fast, two

01:35:18.001 --> 01:35:23.290
of our sisters joined and they usually —
Daniil Liberman: And they don't travel that

01:35:23.290 --> 01:35:24.290
much.

01:35:24.290 --> 01:35:28.550
David Liberman: And they usually run operations
and scaling of their projects.

01:35:28.550 --> 01:35:32.030
Tim Ferriss: Do any of your other siblings
have the pair bonding that you guys have?

01:35:32.030 --> 01:35:33.030
David Liberman: No.

01:35:33.030 --> 01:35:34.030
It didn't work.

01:35:34.030 --> 01:35:36.830
But five of us, we are quite close and we
work together.

01:35:36.830 --> 01:35:40.719
Daniil Liberman: We work together, we live
together in L.A. Our houses is literally like

01:35:40.719 --> 01:35:42.080
two minutes away from each other.

01:35:42.080 --> 01:35:47.449
David Liberman: Yes, and they start to run
operations scenes, game development.

01:35:47.449 --> 01:35:51.301
And then they come —
Daniil Liberman: Became our equal partners.

01:35:51.301 --> 01:35:54.120
David Liberman: The equal partners in animation
studio.

01:35:54.120 --> 01:36:03.219
And in since then it's much easier for us
to dream more and to act.

01:36:03.219 --> 01:36:07.370
Daniil Liberman: No, now we discuss everything
together.

01:36:07.370 --> 01:36:14.050
Philosophy, the vision for the future, the
morality.

01:36:14.050 --> 01:36:17.280
It's a constant discussion among all of us.

01:36:17.280 --> 01:36:21.880
Tim Ferriss: So I can imagine, I'm just putting
myself in the place of people listening to

01:36:21.880 --> 01:36:26.880
this that I'm sure there are people out there
who work with family and then there are other

01:36:26.880 --> 01:36:28.790
people who don't work with family.

01:36:28.790 --> 01:36:34.210
But I imagine working with my family and I
love my family, but I'm like, "Wow, if I hired

01:36:34.210 --> 01:36:40.120
two people from my family to run my operations,"
what do you do if it's not working?

01:36:40.120 --> 01:36:44.390
Would you ever fire them or do you just have
complete confidence that you can resolve anything

01:36:44.390 --> 01:36:45.390
that might come up?

01:36:45.390 --> 01:36:48.400
How do you think about —
David Liberman: You definitely can fire, but

01:36:48.400 --> 01:36:49.400
for sure.

01:36:49.400 --> 01:36:52.231
Daniil Liberman: I mean what we learned —
David Liberman: What we learned.

01:36:52.231 --> 01:36:57.570
So we went through really harsh experience
with game development to the bankruptcy and

01:36:57.570 --> 01:37:00.230
they were the only ones who stayed with us.

01:37:00.230 --> 01:37:01.230
Daniil Liberman: Stayed with us.

01:37:01.230 --> 01:37:02.920
David Liberman: Through it was a challenging
period.

01:37:02.920 --> 01:37:10.390
And when you actually went through such experience,
it's much easier to trust.

01:37:10.390 --> 01:37:14.650
Not trust just because it's family, but because
you know what you can expect.

01:37:14.650 --> 01:37:17.030
Tim Ferriss: You saw how they behaved.

01:37:17.030 --> 01:37:22.250
David Liberman: Especially in venture business
when as we mentioned most of the attempts.

01:37:22.250 --> 01:37:23.489
Daniil Liberman: Would fail.

01:37:23.489 --> 01:37:24.719
David Liberman: Would fail.

01:37:24.719 --> 01:37:31.050
It is really important quality to have someone
who you can trust that they are with you long

01:37:31.050 --> 01:37:35.320
term, not just for this project.

01:37:35.320 --> 01:37:38.590
And I would say this worked pretty well.

01:37:38.590 --> 01:37:49.280
Maybe this was the results of this experience,
which we all had, but maybe just because our

01:37:49.280 --> 01:37:51.300
parents worked together.

01:37:51.300 --> 01:37:56.030
For us, we didn't have this concept [inaudible]
that it's not right.

01:37:56.030 --> 01:37:57.030
Tim Ferriss: You saw that modeled.

01:37:57.030 --> 01:37:58.030
Daniil Liberman: Never work with your family?

01:37:58.030 --> 01:37:59.030
No!

01:37:59.030 --> 01:38:00.030
David Liberman: And we went through challenges.

01:38:00.030 --> 01:38:01.570
Definitely we argue with each other, et cetera.

01:38:01.570 --> 01:38:06.030
So we went through a lot of challenges but
it worth it.

01:38:06.030 --> 01:38:12.060
Tim Ferriss: Well, I was thinking also, as
you saw your sisters, I suppose, doing the

01:38:12.060 --> 01:38:18.080
challenges of the game development company
has reminded me of a quote, I don't know the

01:38:18.080 --> 01:38:21.650
attribution, but that's in English is a common
expression.

01:38:21.650 --> 01:38:22.909
"Adversity builds character."

01:38:22.909 --> 01:38:30.320
But the alternative, which I think might be
just as true or maybe truer, is "Adversity

01:38:30.320 --> 01:38:31.320
doesn't build character.

01:38:31.320 --> 01:38:32.320
It reveals character."

01:38:32.320 --> 01:38:33.320
Daniil Liberman: Reveals character.

01:38:33.320 --> 01:38:39.449
Tim Ferriss: And so you got to see how they
responded to a lot of challenge, which gave

01:38:39.449 --> 01:38:40.730
you the confidence then.

01:38:40.730 --> 01:38:42.929
David Liberman: And there were a lot of challenges.

01:38:42.929 --> 01:38:46.469
Daniil Liberman: We are lucky in many ways.

01:38:46.469 --> 01:38:55.890
Tim Ferriss: So I want to ask you about, so
this is the same question applied to two different

01:38:55.890 --> 01:38:56.890
groups.

01:38:56.890 --> 01:39:00.760
So the first is going to be your parents and
I wanted just ask about what ideas of theirs

01:39:00.760 --> 01:39:03.040
you find most interesting.

01:39:03.040 --> 01:39:06.730
Maybe that's a place to talk about [inaudible],
maybe not.

01:39:06.730 --> 01:39:13.200
But then the second is what ideas you guys
are most excited about right now.

01:39:13.200 --> 01:39:17.890
But I wanted to start with —
Daniil Liberman: Do we have a couple more

01:39:17.890 --> 01:39:18.890
hours for each?

01:39:18.890 --> 01:39:19.890
David Liberman: Yeah, exactly.

01:39:19.890 --> 01:39:28.050
So our parents, so our dad, he was from physics,
but he was by government, pushed out from

01:39:28.050 --> 01:39:33.860
physics because he was Jew and —
Daniil Liberman: And switched to biology.

01:39:33.860 --> 01:39:37.670
David Liberman: Biology because biophysics
was an open field.

01:39:37.670 --> 01:39:43.040
Tim Ferriss: Physics was more protected and
Jews were more discriminated against in physics?

01:39:43.040 --> 01:39:44.040
Daniil Liberman: Yes.

01:39:44.040 --> 01:39:50.820
David Liberman: So he found this niche, it
was a new niche, it was an emerging new science.

01:39:50.820 --> 01:39:59.650
He came there with knowledge of physics and
that gave him a different perspective than

01:39:59.650 --> 01:40:00.650
most —
Daniil Liberman: An advantage.

01:40:00.650 --> 01:40:01.650
David Liberman: Biologists.

01:40:01.650 --> 01:40:02.650
Tim Ferriss: A huge advantage.

01:40:02.650 --> 01:40:06.429
David Liberman: Who would see everything through
prism of chemistry or through a prism of biology.

01:40:06.429 --> 01:40:08.719
Daniil Liberman: He looked at it through the
prism of physics.

01:40:08.719 --> 01:40:10.440
David Liberman: And he started discovering
things —

01:40:10.440 --> 01:40:11.440
Daniil Liberman: Faster.

01:40:11.440 --> 01:40:16.171
David Liberman: Faster in this field —
Daniil Liberman: Starting with the first thing

01:40:16.171 --> 01:40:26.010
which he discovered was that the color which
received by an eye is encoded not in the frequency,

01:40:26.010 --> 01:40:33.781
which is then translated as a signal inside
of the eye nerve, but as a pattern of signal

01:40:33.781 --> 01:40:34.781
—
David Liberman: Pauses in between spikes.

01:40:34.781 --> 01:40:35.781
Daniil Liberman: Pauses in between spikes.

01:40:35.781 --> 01:40:36.781
David Liberman: So this pattern is encoded.

01:40:36.781 --> 01:40:37.781
Daniil Liberman: It's almost like Morse, de-de-de-de.

01:40:37.781 --> 01:40:39.489
Tim Ferriss: Morse code.

01:40:39.489 --> 01:40:41.760
Daniil Liberman: And this is sort of like
—

01:40:41.760 --> 01:40:45.260
David Liberman: That's how he realized that
it should be a computer is there, that should

01:40:45.260 --> 01:40:46.980
be a computation because the encoding happened.

01:40:46.980 --> 01:40:52.420
Daniil Liberman: He came to this idea, there
was a really interesting weird obsession of

01:40:52.420 --> 01:40:59.650
his on the idea that if evolution was working
properly for millions of years, if not billions

01:40:59.650 --> 01:41:02.940
of years, then it should be optimal.

01:41:02.940 --> 01:41:11.030
That the most optimal mechanisms should have
survived the evolution process and therefore

01:41:11.030 --> 01:41:15.380
what's the most optimal from the physics perspective
in terms of measuring —

01:41:15.380 --> 01:41:18.030
David Liberman: Amount of energy spent on
the operation.

01:41:18.030 --> 01:41:26.580
Daniil Liberman: On operation and data is
like time in between the quantum events.

01:41:26.580 --> 01:41:28.990
This is probably the most optimal one.

01:41:28.990 --> 01:41:32.270
And from this perspective, from the perspective
—

01:41:32.270 --> 01:41:33.790
David Liberman: He was able to find —
Daniil Liberman: Optimality, he was trying

01:41:33.790 --> 01:41:38.900
to find how the eye works and he was the one
to discover exactly how it works.

01:41:38.900 --> 01:41:40.040
And he's like, "Wow.

01:41:40.040 --> 01:41:45.860
So I was right about optimality," and then
used optimality all the way down, exploring

01:41:45.860 --> 01:41:54.790
into the nerve, into the neuron and into the
core of the cell, into the DNA and then across

01:41:54.790 --> 01:41:55.790
the entire cell.

01:41:55.790 --> 01:41:59.699
David Liberman: So Chematic was the abbreviation
of chemistry, chemistry.

01:41:59.699 --> 01:42:00.699
Daniil Liberman: Chemistry.

01:42:00.699 --> 01:42:02.170
David Liberman: Optics and mathematics.

01:42:02.170 --> 01:42:08.901
So he always believes that the real discoveries
are in combination of different fields, that

01:42:08.901 --> 01:42:13.560
actually the fact that we learn this subject
separately —

01:42:13.560 --> 01:42:14.679
Daniil Liberman: Limiting us.

01:42:14.679 --> 01:42:20.140
David Liberman: Limiting us in terms of us
being able to discover some things, which

01:42:20.140 --> 01:42:23.040
requires you to look at some —
Tim Ferriss: Have some interdisciplinary synthesis.

01:42:23.040 --> 01:42:24.659
Daniil Liberman: He was pushing this idea
of interdisciplinary synthesis.

01:42:24.659 --> 01:42:31.560
David Liberman: And their success, both of
our dad and mom, their success was because

01:42:31.560 --> 01:42:32.560
of that.

01:42:32.560 --> 01:42:35.170
They would combine things which others wouldn't
combine.

01:42:35.170 --> 01:42:37.170
For example, he brought the quantum physics
—

01:42:37.170 --> 01:42:38.980
Daniil Liberman: Into cells.

01:42:38.980 --> 01:42:41.630
David Liberman: Describing cells processes.

01:42:41.630 --> 01:42:50.300
So even he, maybe 2008, 2010, it was really
emerging idea in science.

01:42:50.300 --> 01:42:53.400
Scientists, they were trying to avoid —
Daniil Liberman: Avoid as much as —

01:42:53.400 --> 01:42:57.469
David Liberman: As much as possible some complicated
processes in biology because —

01:42:57.469 --> 01:42:58.469
Daniil Liberman: Not just in chemistry.

01:42:58.469 --> 01:43:01.739
David Liberman: It will challenge the idea
that it happened randomly and that they was

01:43:01.739 --> 01:43:03.090
trying to avoid that.

01:43:03.090 --> 01:43:09.989
I can only recently more and more discoveries
around photosynthesis, which probably requires

01:43:09.989 --> 01:43:12.489
quantum tunneling.

01:43:12.489 --> 01:43:17.610
And then there's the smell and the fact that
the birds can see the direction of —

01:43:17.610 --> 01:43:18.690
Daniil Liberman: Polarization of light.

01:43:18.690 --> 01:43:23.090
David Liberman: Polarization of light and
where is North Pole.

01:43:23.090 --> 01:43:28.580
This all can be described only through the
quantum properties of some processes.

01:43:28.580 --> 01:43:29.580
Daniil Liberman: Processes.

01:43:29.580 --> 01:43:32.100
David Liberman: Which happened inside —
Daniil Liberman: In life.

01:43:32.100 --> 01:43:35.980
David Liberman: So biggest discovery, which
we personally, it's the last discovery.

01:43:35.980 --> 01:43:36.980
It's always like that.

01:43:36.980 --> 01:43:38.760
Daniil Liberman: It’s not just discovery.

01:43:38.760 --> 01:43:47.710
It's like the entire concept that our consciousness
work through the quantum compute inside every

01:43:47.710 --> 01:43:49.080
single neuron.

01:43:49.080 --> 01:43:51.960
So every single neuron is sort of a qubit.

01:43:51.960 --> 01:43:58.150
It's like a quantum state machine itself,
which then combined in the network produce

01:43:58.150 --> 01:43:59.150
all the results.

01:43:59.150 --> 01:44:01.330
But every neuron is quantum state machine.

01:44:01.330 --> 01:44:05.369
David Liberman: This can actually explain
a lot about our psychology, about how we think,

01:44:05.369 --> 01:44:12.541
how our consciousness work, but also it can
