Daniil Liberman: Sometimes like all this stuff.

01:58:03.320 --> 01:58:07.400
David Liberman: Yes, people usually when it's
about derivative in credit default swaps,

01:58:07.400 --> 01:58:08.670
blame the bankers.

01:58:08.670 --> 01:58:10.800
Daniil Liberman: Everyone blames bankers.

01:58:10.800 --> 01:58:15.270
David Liberman: Oh, bankers, they made this
deregulation, they had the problem.

01:58:15.270 --> 01:58:17.750
And that's how it all happens.

01:58:17.750 --> 01:58:25.370
But every time when we have a statement, we
check it with data and we decide to [inaudible]

01:58:25.370 --> 01:58:28.650
who was the actual beneficiary.

01:58:28.650 --> 01:58:31.130
Beneficiary of credit default swaps.

01:58:31.130 --> 01:58:37.000
Daniil Liberman: Not of their fault, but of
them being created and popularized that way.

01:58:37.000 --> 01:58:40.361
David Liberman: And then we realized that
most of the credit default swaps through some

01:58:40.361 --> 01:58:44.449
vehicles, they were owned by pension funds.

01:58:44.449 --> 01:58:46.270
We're like, what?

01:58:46.270 --> 01:58:55.051
Because we are from a region where pension
funds, really small pensioners are really

01:58:55.051 --> 01:59:00.619
poor people and you would never think that
they all sell multi-trillion dollar —

01:59:00.619 --> 01:59:02.159
Tim Ferriss: Huge.

01:59:02.159 --> 01:59:03.159
Huge.

01:59:03.159 --> 01:59:09.270
Daniil Liberman: The United States are like,
what is it?

01:59:09.270 --> 01:59:10.270
David Liberman: 40 trillion dollars in assets.

01:59:10.270 --> 01:59:13.659
It's more than the entire publicly traded
market combined.

01:59:13.659 --> 01:59:20.159
You will combine all Apple, Google, Exxon,
Walmart, you will combine all these companies.

01:59:20.159 --> 01:59:22.530
It's smaller than the assets of pension funds.

01:59:22.530 --> 01:59:24.850
Daniil Liberman: Altogether less than the
assets of pension funds.

01:59:24.850 --> 01:59:28.349
David Liberman: So through this reason, we
started to learn more and more like how the

01:59:28.349 --> 01:59:31.560
hell it happened —
Daniil Liberman: And it was green invention.

01:59:31.560 --> 01:59:36.270
I mean the fact that United States is the
greatest economy in the world is because of

01:59:36.270 --> 01:59:37.270
this.

01:59:37.270 --> 01:59:39.179
David Liberman: So we realize it's not because
of the army, not military.

01:59:39.179 --> 01:59:41.210
Daniil Liberman: It's because of people actually
were saving some big chunk of their money,

01:59:41.210 --> 01:59:46.949
and the money was not just saved but was invested
into the market and that's how market got

01:59:46.949 --> 01:59:49.889
all the capital to grow, et cetera, et cetera,
et cetera.

01:59:49.889 --> 02:00:02.540
David Liberman: But then realized that unfortunately
as soon as this population not only grew to

02:00:02.540 --> 02:00:08.139
the amounts that are the majority of voters,
but also they have this enormous financial

02:00:08.139 --> 02:00:14.810
power, the financial power, which bigger than
the banks, the bigger than any other organization.

02:00:14.810 --> 02:00:19.060
So this financial power in this voting power
—

02:00:19.060 --> 02:00:25.679
Daniil Liberman: Together combined led to
lots of popular political decisions.

02:00:25.679 --> 02:00:26.679
David Liberman: And regulations.

02:00:26.679 --> 02:00:29.180
Daniil Liberman: And regulations which was
completely false.

02:00:29.180 --> 02:00:34.190
David Liberman: Which led to —
Daniil Liberman: What types of things?

02:00:34.190 --> 02:00:36.090
We can give you some taste of it.

02:00:36.090 --> 02:00:37.090
Yeah.

02:00:37.090 --> 02:00:41.929
David Liberman: For example, when we first
came to United States, we learned about student

02:00:41.929 --> 02:00:42.929
debt.

02:00:42.929 --> 02:00:43.929
Daniil Liberman: Student loan.

02:00:43.929 --> 02:00:47.520
David Liberman: And for you it's obvious it's
United States, everything is commercial.

02:00:47.520 --> 02:00:51.079
That's why college is not free.

02:00:51.079 --> 02:00:53.440
That's how we got to the student debt.

02:00:53.440 --> 02:01:01.179
But the student debt, if you will look at
the size, before 2000, it was really a small

02:01:01.179 --> 02:01:02.179
problem.

02:01:02.179 --> 02:01:03.179
It's now like $2 trillion.

02:01:03.179 --> 02:01:07.880
But before that it was a much, much, much,
much smaller problems.

02:01:07.880 --> 02:01:16.570
And what we realized that previously in the
'80s, your tuition when you go to state college

02:01:16.570 --> 02:01:18.860
would be mostly subsidized by government.

02:01:18.860 --> 02:01:22.570
90 percent of your tuition would be subsidized
by government.

02:01:22.570 --> 02:01:29.139
That's how your parents were able to actually
pay for their tuition during the summer job,

02:01:29.139 --> 02:01:30.820
there's a summer job.

02:01:30.820 --> 02:01:36.320
And that's how the debts never accumulated
as much.

02:01:36.320 --> 02:01:47.139
But in the late '80s, beginning of '90s, because
of almost bankruptcy, state of —

02:01:47.139 --> 02:01:48.139
Daniil Liberman: Public pension funds.

02:01:48.139 --> 02:01:49.139
David Liberman: Public pension funds.

02:01:49.139 --> 02:01:51.080
Daniil Liberman: In many states.

02:01:51.080 --> 02:01:55.590
David Liberman: The governments in different
states and in federal level as well.

02:01:55.590 --> 02:01:56.599
Daniil Liberman: Bailed out.

02:01:56.599 --> 02:02:02.989
David Liberman: Made a decision to not only
bail out, but also for the future, protect

02:02:02.989 --> 02:02:04.400
these pension funds.

02:02:04.400 --> 02:02:10.590
For example, in California, the growth of
public pension funds, including teachers funds

02:02:10.590 --> 02:02:15.720
guaranteed on the level of 7.8 percent a year.

02:02:15.720 --> 02:02:18.570
Imagine that your deficit would be guaranteed
as this level.

02:02:18.570 --> 02:02:19.570
Daniil Liberman: A state budget.

02:02:19.570 --> 02:02:21.070
David Liberman: And it's not even average.

02:02:21.070 --> 02:02:22.070
Tim Ferriss: State budget.

02:02:22.070 --> 02:02:24.889
So the subsidies to education went down as
a result.

02:02:24.889 --> 02:02:26.030
David Liberman: Yes, exactly.

02:02:26.030 --> 02:02:27.949
So it's —
Daniil Liberman: You can see because during

02:02:27.949 --> 02:02:29.540
[inaudible] —
David Liberman: The money is still there.

02:02:29.540 --> 02:02:33.011
Daniil Liberman: Tons of information become
publicly available online.

02:02:33.011 --> 02:02:34.011
David Liberman: You can trace it.

02:02:34.011 --> 02:02:35.011
Daniil Liberman: You can trace it.

02:02:35.011 --> 02:02:36.011
It's all there.

02:02:36.011 --> 02:02:37.699
David Liberman: So this is from —
Daniil Liberman: Tons of data, but if you

02:02:37.699 --> 02:02:40.420
like to dig this data, you can find it.

02:02:40.420 --> 02:02:42.110
It's just there.

02:02:42.110 --> 02:02:43.110
You can see it.

02:02:43.110 --> 02:02:45.579
You can like, "Oh, right, the money goes into
the education."

02:02:45.579 --> 02:02:47.369
Then —
Tim Ferriss: There's a pension fund.

02:02:47.369 --> 02:02:50.480
David Liberman: There's a pension fund like
CalPERS in California.

02:02:50.480 --> 02:02:52.030
Tim Ferriss: In California.

02:02:52.030 --> 02:02:53.190
David Liberman: Yes.

02:02:53.190 --> 02:02:59.260
It's public pension funds pension fund, the
public workers pension fund of teachers of

02:02:59.260 --> 02:03:00.260
California.

02:03:00.260 --> 02:03:03.300
No one really realized the size of these funds.

02:03:03.300 --> 02:03:06.330
Daniil Liberman: And no one really realized
the gap in between —

02:03:06.330 --> 02:03:10.790
David Liberman: Assets and liabilities of
this fund.

02:03:10.790 --> 02:03:12.300
Daniil Liberman: Assets and liabilities of
this fund.

02:03:12.300 --> 02:03:16.510
So when everyone discusses that pension funds
are underfunded —

02:03:16.510 --> 02:03:19.081
David Liberman: It's only public pension funds.

02:03:19.081 --> 02:03:20.579
Daniil Liberman: It's only public pensions
funds which are underfunded.

02:03:20.579 --> 02:03:25.590
David Liberman: It's $5 trillion of a gap,
$5 trillion.

02:03:25.590 --> 02:03:29.900
And this is accounting the money which is
constantly transferred.

02:03:29.900 --> 02:03:31.510
Daniil Liberman: Someone might think that
—

02:03:31.510 --> 02:03:33.099
David Liberman: From subsidies to the pension
funds instead of subsidies.

02:03:33.099 --> 02:03:36.699
Daniil Liberman: Someone might think that
this is just public workers.

02:03:36.699 --> 02:03:38.320
They're probably what?

02:03:38.320 --> 02:03:42.159
15 percent of workforce in United States.

02:03:42.159 --> 02:03:46.710
And yet the size of their pension funds is
half of all pension funds.

02:03:46.710 --> 02:03:49.540
David Liberman: Even though it's only 15 percent
of workforce.

02:03:49.540 --> 02:03:51.270
Daniil Liberman: Why?

02:03:51.270 --> 02:03:55.530
Because of a guaranteed 7.8 percent analog
growth.

02:03:55.530 --> 02:03:58.309
Because when the rest of the market goes down,
up, down, up.

02:03:58.309 --> 02:04:01.550
David Liberman: And this problem is not only
indication.

02:04:01.550 --> 02:04:05.810
If you look at the police, there was this
big movement of underfunding police.

02:04:05.810 --> 02:04:16.530
And we looked at the budget and per person
in L.A. for example, per officer in L.A.,

02:04:16.530 --> 02:04:19.090
it's almost like 250,000 a year.

02:04:19.090 --> 02:04:24.969
Daniil Liberman: And people were very angry
about that.

02:04:24.969 --> 02:04:28.790
"What the hell, we pay you a lot and you're
not doing your job."

02:04:28.790 --> 02:04:31.679
But then what —
David Liberman: The real work is right now,

02:04:31.679 --> 02:04:34.400
and the real officers right now —
Daniil Liberman: Are not paid as much.

02:04:34.400 --> 02:04:35.400
David Liberman: — are not paid as much.

02:04:35.400 --> 02:04:41.150
And they get only a tiny portion of it and
most of it goes as a transfer for pension

02:04:41.150 --> 02:04:44.070
funds for officers who worked 30 years ago.

02:04:44.070 --> 02:04:45.960
Daniil Liberman: Now again —
Tim Ferriss: So where does this lead?

02:04:45.960 --> 02:04:52.309
You guys are looking at the data and I suppose
a good set of data ideally allows you to have

02:04:52.309 --> 02:04:57.230
some predictive ability or at least speculation
ability.

02:04:57.230 --> 02:04:59.250
So what do you do with this after identifying.

02:04:59.250 --> 02:05:03.150
David Liberman: So we realized that every
next generation because of that —

02:05:03.150 --> 02:05:10.110
Daniil Liberman: Zooming out to a larger picture,
what it led to is that through the generations

02:05:10.110 --> 02:05:17.050
starting saying from Boomers, I know this
is like a false signs about dividing people

02:05:17.050 --> 02:05:20.679
into these generations, but we can batch —
David Liberman: People who was born around

02:05:20.679 --> 02:05:21.679
the '50s, yes, let's say.

02:05:21.679 --> 02:05:27.340
Daniil Liberman: The Boomers, as they were
getting into 30 years old, on average as a

02:05:27.340 --> 02:05:31.130
generation, own something around 21 —
David Liberman: 23 percent.

02:05:31.130 --> 02:05:34.989
Daniil Liberman: 23 percent of national wealth
in the United States.

02:05:34.989 --> 02:05:45.980
The following generation Gen Xes at the same
age, average age owned around seven.

02:05:45.980 --> 02:05:55.330
Us, the Millennials, by the same age, owned
only less than three percent, like 2.8-ish.

02:05:55.330 --> 02:06:00.620
And then the trajectory shows us that together
with the crisis which happened during the

02:06:00.620 --> 02:06:07.261
COVID, we can expect that the next generation
after are the Gen Z are going to be in debt.

02:06:07.261 --> 02:06:10.280
David Liberman: By the same average —
Daniil Liberman: By the average age.

02:06:10.280 --> 02:06:12.159
So they still —
David Liberman: So its trajectory —

02:06:12.159 --> 02:06:14.710
Daniil Liberman: — on average as a generation,
they're still going to be in debt.

02:06:14.710 --> 02:06:16.300
David Liberman: So trajectory really bad.

02:06:16.300 --> 02:06:17.300
Tim Ferriss: Sounds bad.

02:06:17.300 --> 02:06:24.860
David Liberman: And also what this created
that if these young people, not if, when they

02:06:24.860 --> 02:06:32.380
realize that the social agreement is not working,
social structures will start to fall apart.

02:06:32.380 --> 02:06:36.929
Tim Ferriss: What do you think are some likely
ways that that will show up?

02:06:36.929 --> 02:06:43.809
Daniil Liberman: We'll see the new wave of
unions, protests, people not working, economies

02:06:43.809 --> 02:06:44.809
slowing down.

02:06:44.809 --> 02:06:49.309
This stuff, new businesses not created.

02:06:49.309 --> 02:06:54.780
We can see from the statistics that over the
last 10 years, the share of the new businesses

02:06:54.780 --> 02:06:56.000
created in the United States.

02:06:56.000 --> 02:06:58.850
And in the United States, lots of new businesses
created every year.

02:06:58.850 --> 02:07:00.960
Lots of them never survive the year.

02:07:00.960 --> 02:07:02.559
But that's the case.

02:07:02.559 --> 02:07:06.510
You create many, many, many businesses and
lots of them die and some of them survive.

02:07:06.510 --> 02:07:07.650
And that's the economy.

02:07:07.650 --> 02:07:14.000
So the share of the new businesses created
annually in the United States, by the people

02:07:14.000 --> 02:07:21.480
under 30 years old used to be 40 percent or
45, let's say 40.

02:07:21.480 --> 02:07:25.790
And now 10 years later, now it's a 30, so
it's down 10 percent.

02:07:25.790 --> 02:07:31.110
Tim Ferriss: Is the same number of companies
being built just by younger and older?

02:07:31.110 --> 02:07:34.480
Or is the total number —
Daniil Liberman: It's just more people, more

02:07:34.480 --> 02:07:35.480
companies.

02:07:35.480 --> 02:07:36.949
The total number is sort of the same.

02:07:36.949 --> 02:07:42.909
It's just not young people creating them,
it's people 55 plus creating them.

02:07:42.909 --> 02:07:43.909
Tim Ferriss: That's surprising.

02:07:43.909 --> 02:07:44.909
Daniil Liberman: That's surprising.

02:07:44.909 --> 02:07:46.400
And not what I would expect.

02:07:46.400 --> 02:07:53.070
I never checked the data, but my intuition
tells me all those businesses are just real

02:07:53.070 --> 02:07:54.590
estate.

02:07:54.590 --> 02:07:57.469
People buying houses and renting them out.

02:07:57.469 --> 02:07:59.170
So nothing new is created.

02:07:59.170 --> 02:08:03.820
Just the spike in the price in real estate
is just because they're buying and renting

02:08:03.820 --> 02:08:05.809
out and those are the new businesses.

02:08:05.809 --> 02:08:07.239
David Liberman: So there are a lot of problems.

02:08:07.239 --> 02:08:12.071
But still when you talk about the problems,
it's really important also, at least to propose

02:08:12.071 --> 02:08:13.510
some solutions.

02:08:13.510 --> 02:08:20.320
What we saw, because we realized that this
shift is based on regulations, it's not natural

02:08:20.320 --> 02:08:22.260
that this shift is happening.

02:08:22.260 --> 02:08:25.179
So regulations which were —
Tim Ferriss: Based on regulations?

02:08:25.179 --> 02:08:28.739
David Liberman: Regulations, that's the rules
which was set by the previous generations,

02:08:28.739 --> 02:08:31.900
which making this constant transfer.

02:08:31.900 --> 02:08:40.489
We realized that if you would decipher the
regulations, what they did, that they made

02:08:40.489 --> 02:08:47.929
a preference to equity, to shares of the companies,
et cetera, compared to the income.

02:08:47.929 --> 02:08:49.190
Daniil Liberman: Individual income.

02:08:49.190 --> 02:08:51.610
David Liberman: So capital gain is taxed less.

02:08:51.610 --> 02:08:59.099
There are a lot of things which actually made
the huge difference between equity, how equity

02:08:59.099 --> 02:09:02.679
treated by the regulation and by the market
and how personally.

02:09:02.679 --> 02:09:03.730
Tim Ferriss: Got it, right.

02:09:03.730 --> 02:09:05.660
The taxation —
David Liberman: Everything.

02:09:05.660 --> 02:09:06.810
Tim Ferriss: — incentives.

02:09:06.810 --> 02:09:11.250
"The expansion funds can invest in that, but
cannot invest in that," and things like that.

02:09:11.250 --> 02:09:13.559
So there are limitations set, not naturally.

02:09:13.559 --> 02:09:18.929
Daniil Liberman: The majority of the capital
is pension capital.

02:09:18.929 --> 02:09:23.099
Then where it can be invested matters a lot.

02:09:23.099 --> 02:09:24.099
Tim Ferriss: Sure.

02:09:24.099 --> 02:09:31.360
David Liberman: So in this term we realize
that the solution which we can test and propose

02:09:31.360 --> 02:09:32.360
—
Daniil Liberman: At least try.

02:09:32.360 --> 02:09:36.110
David Liberman: — is to try to convert income
—

02:09:36.110 --> 02:09:37.450
Daniil Liberman: Individual Income.

02:09:37.450 --> 02:09:39.059
David Liberman: — into securities.

02:09:39.059 --> 02:09:40.059
Securities.

02:09:40.059 --> 02:09:41.059
Tim Ferriss: Okay.

02:09:41.059 --> 02:09:43.040
So this is where the Libermans Company comes.

02:09:43.040 --> 02:09:44.040
Daniil Liberman: Yes, the Libermans Company
comes.

02:09:44.040 --> 02:09:45.040
David Liberman: Yes.

02:09:45.040 --> 02:09:47.070
First time we did this to ourselves.

02:09:47.070 --> 02:09:51.750
As a scientist, you need to make the experiments
and the experiments first have to —

02:09:51.750 --> 02:09:53.620
Tim Ferriss: I wish more scientists did this.

02:09:53.620 --> 02:09:57.050
Yeah, you've got to be the first monkey shot
into space.

02:09:57.050 --> 02:09:58.050
David Liberman: Yes.

02:09:58.050 --> 02:09:59.740
Tim Ferriss: At less than three Gs?

02:09:59.740 --> 02:10:00.740
Just to be clear.

02:10:00.740 --> 02:10:01.740
David Liberman: So we realize, yeah, okay
—

02:10:01.740 --> 02:10:03.239
Daniil Liberman: If you want to survive this.

02:10:03.239 --> 02:10:08.829
David Liberman: So even though our income
is not stable, but if you will build a model

02:10:08.829 --> 02:10:14.360
around all the founders, all tech founders,
and the probabilities of successes during

02:10:14.360 --> 02:10:21.280
the period of time, we can build an income
model like you would do with a company, kind

02:10:21.280 --> 02:10:22.719
of cashflow projections.

02:10:22.719 --> 02:10:28.120
Daniil Liberman: Risk, weighted risk adjusted
cashflow, projection of an entrepreneur.

02:10:28.120 --> 02:10:32.300
David Liberman: And then based on these —
Tim Ferriss: Like a value stock.

02:10:32.300 --> 02:10:33.300
David Liberman: — projections.

02:10:33.300 --> 02:10:38.639
Like yes, but based on these projections you
can actually set a price for a company which

02:10:38.639 --> 02:10:42.260
would have the same type of income.

02:10:42.260 --> 02:10:48.469
In this case, if you can form the company,
then you can attract investment the same way

02:10:48.469 --> 02:10:49.820
companies do.

02:10:49.820 --> 02:10:59.790
And in this case he will eliminate this bias
and vice versa will be supercharged by this

02:10:59.790 --> 02:11:01.960
—
Daniil Liberman: The capital floating into

02:11:01.960 --> 02:11:02.960
personal income.

02:11:02.960 --> 02:11:06.260
David Liberman: — improving your future.

02:11:06.260 --> 02:11:09.130
Like personal income —
Tim Ferriss: So supercharged, just to try

02:11:09.130 --> 02:11:11.219
to ensure I'm hearing you correctly.

02:11:11.219 --> 02:11:16.530
So supercharged, meaning if you're showing
a decent rate of growth, even though you're

02:11:16.530 --> 02:11:22.660
capital constrained, if you can bring in equity
investors who invest in you as if you were

02:11:22.660 --> 02:11:26.980
a company, you can accelerate your growth
and potential.

02:11:26.980 --> 02:11:28.400
Daniil Liberman: Yes.

02:11:28.400 --> 02:11:37.800
And then we can average as an index across
some cohorts of specific professions or specific

02:11:37.800 --> 02:11:39.989
cohorts like students for example.

02:11:39.989 --> 02:11:43.809
You have higher education or you don't have
higher education.

02:11:43.809 --> 02:11:45.710
How does it change your average income?

02:11:45.710 --> 02:11:48.639
David Liberman: Should we invest in you having
good education?

02:11:48.639 --> 02:11:53.300
Daniil Liberman: Should we invest in every
kid to have the good high education, higher

02:11:53.300 --> 02:11:56.520
education for them to increase their individual
income?

02:11:56.520 --> 02:11:59.489
And that's where investors get their money
back.

02:11:59.489 --> 02:12:03.630
Or should we just leave it to the way it is
right now, with the loan, which then suppresses

02:12:03.630 --> 02:12:06.040
the wheel to go and risk?

02:12:06.040 --> 02:12:10.340
And that's how young people don't create new
businesses and just go find a shitty job.

02:12:10.340 --> 02:12:17.650
Tim Ferriss: So how do you think you incentivize
this type of wider adoption and data sharing,

02:12:17.650 --> 02:12:18.650
right?

02:12:18.650 --> 02:12:23.809
Because I can imagine if let's just say we
flash forward and this has been widely adopted.

02:12:23.809 --> 02:12:27.530
Now I can choose right, do I want to invest
in people who are going getting accounting

02:12:27.530 --> 02:12:32.380
degrees where maybe I have a more reliable
return on my investment year-on-year, or do

02:12:32.380 --> 02:12:36.340
I want to invest in the growth stocks that
have much higher volatility.

02:12:36.340 --> 02:12:42.540
Like the entrepreneurs of a certain class
or maybe age bracket where it's going to be

02:12:42.540 --> 02:12:49.469
a much riskier, by volatility measurements,
investment, but who knows the gains could

02:12:49.469 --> 02:12:50.510
be higher.

02:12:50.510 --> 02:12:54.470
How do you go from a single proof of concept?

02:12:54.470 --> 02:12:56.240
I have also questions about taxation.

02:12:56.240 --> 02:13:00.960
Just if you're being taxed at an ordinary
income level, how you translate that effectively

02:13:00.960 --> 02:13:05.750
to equity holders who might be your investors,
let's just say.

02:13:05.750 --> 02:13:08.610
But well, we could tackle any one of those.

02:13:08.610 --> 02:13:14.219
So it could be like how do you contend with
maybe the handicap of having ordinary income

02:13:14.219 --> 02:13:19.360
taxed, even though that's presumably what
people are investing in on some level, like

02:13:19.360 --> 02:13:21.969
the discounted cashflow or whatever.

02:13:21.969 --> 02:13:28.790
And then how do you take this and encourage
other people to experiment with it?

02:13:28.790 --> 02:13:32.699
David Liberman: Some regulations need to be
changed to make it widely adopted.

02:13:32.699 --> 02:13:39.440
Daniil Liberman: But now we start with ourself
as tech entrepreneurs, because most of our

02:13:39.440 --> 02:13:44.329
income comes from building companies and selling
companies or the making companies [inaudible].

02:13:44.329 --> 02:13:50.060
David Liberman: Even today there is no difference
whether you registered this company under

02:13:50.060 --> 02:13:54.989
your name or you registered this company under
a holding company name.

02:13:54.989 --> 02:13:55.989
So there is no difference.

02:13:55.989 --> 02:13:56.989
Tim Ferriss: That's a great point.

02:13:56.989 --> 02:13:57.989
That's a great point.

02:13:57.989 --> 02:14:03.199
You guys are actually operating in an easier
case, straightforward case, because most of

02:14:03.199 --> 02:14:08.849
your gains would be from companies therefore
likely long-term capital gains.

02:14:08.849 --> 02:14:12.960
Maybe you have USBS exclusions, et cetera.

02:14:12.960 --> 02:14:16.440
Versus if you guys were highly paid lawyers
or something, it could get a lot trickier.

02:14:16.440 --> 02:14:22.530
David Liberman: Yes, we went through that,
prove it to regulators, pave the way through,

02:14:22.530 --> 02:14:23.810
show that it works pretty good.

02:14:23.810 --> 02:14:28.840
Daniil Liberman: Thousands of entrepreneurs
can actually show in their example that it

02:14:28.840 --> 02:14:34.860
works for the investors and then if it works
for the investors, then we can introduce new

02:14:34.860 --> 02:14:38.060
regulations which will make it work for the
professionals.

02:14:38.060 --> 02:14:40.780
Let's say software engineers, doctors, lawyers.

02:14:40.780 --> 02:14:45.719
And from there go to even wider groups like
students.

02:14:45.719 --> 02:14:50.699
There need to be even more regulations because
kids need to be protected and stuff like this,

02:14:50.699 --> 02:14:53.179
but it all possible to be done.

02:14:53.179 --> 02:14:59.679
David Liberman: So we're pretty sure that
at least some politicians who will see is

02:14:59.679 --> 02:15:05.840
that their voters can benefit out of it and
that they can actually live better life.

02:15:05.840 --> 02:15:11.670
And the problems like student debts can be
solved in just really short term by the tools

02:15:11.670 --> 02:15:13.719
like that.

02:15:13.719 --> 02:15:16.650
We hope that some rules will change.

02:15:16.650 --> 02:15:22.800
Daniil Liberman: And what's interesting right
now, most of the pension funds are invested

02:15:22.800 --> 02:15:34.389
in really bubbled overpriced assets just because
it's too much money and not that much of instruments,

02:15:34.389 --> 02:15:38.020
like financial instruments, to actually generate
the growth.

02:15:38.020 --> 02:15:41.520
David Liberman: That's why they needed this
accredit for swaps and et cetera because they

02:15:41.520 --> 02:15:47.790
need this derivatives because it was just
too much money to invest.

02:15:47.790 --> 02:15:51.410
So if it would be invested in people —
Daniil Liberman: If we are talking about investing

02:15:51.410 --> 02:15:52.410
in people.

02:15:52.410 --> 02:15:53.410
David Liberman: — everyone will win.

02:15:53.410 --> 02:15:55.570
Pension funds will win, people will win.

02:15:55.570 --> 02:15:58.739
It's just the gross economy would be much
faster than it is today.

02:15:58.739 --> 02:16:04.520
Daniil Liberman: It's likely going to be 10
times larger market to invest in when we start

02:16:04.520 --> 02:16:07.440
investing in individual income as equity.

02:16:07.440 --> 02:16:12.190
Tim Ferriss: So what do you say to people
who think about this and they're like, "Okay,

02:16:12.190 --> 02:16:16.560
I'm sure there are cases where this would
be very attractive, at least initially."

02:16:16.560 --> 02:16:20.710
So you might have somebody like a very promising
YouTuber, they want to build out a studio

02:16:20.710 --> 02:16:21.710
and hire.

02:16:21.710 --> 02:16:24.469
I guess they could go out and raise an equity
round if they're really —

02:16:24.469 --> 02:16:31.110
Daniil Liberman: That's what we did, but with
individual income like in the YouTubers.

02:16:31.110 --> 02:16:32.160
Tim Ferriss: Okay.

02:16:32.160 --> 02:16:38.790
Now do you think if somebody is really successful
that they would try to renegotiate or that

02:16:38.790 --> 02:16:39.790
they would not try to renegotiate?

02:16:39.790 --> 02:16:41.390
David Liberman: There is an example.

02:16:41.390 --> 02:16:44.380
It's not us, it was before us.

02:16:44.380 --> 02:16:53.540
There is a fund which invested in college
athletes and they proposed to every college

02:16:53.540 --> 02:16:54.740
athlete.

02:16:54.740 --> 02:16:58.360
They took the statistics because it's quite
statistical thing.

02:16:58.360 --> 02:17:00.429
Daniil Liberman: It's like a Moneyball type
of a game.

02:17:00.429 --> 02:17:03.300
They calculated their way to understanding
how to predict —

02:17:03.300 --> 02:17:04.300
David Liberman: And they offered.

02:17:04.300 --> 02:17:07.450
Daniil Liberman: — how many students they
should invest in order to basically hit the

02:17:07.450 --> 02:17:08.660
—
Tim Ferriss: Have a couple of LeBron Jameses.

02:17:08.660 --> 02:17:09.660
David Liberman: Exactly, yes.

02:17:09.660 --> 02:17:14.590
And they offered every student era the same
price, 5-10 percent investment.

02:17:14.590 --> 02:17:19.820
Daniil Liberman: We're buying 5-10 percent
of your future income and we pay you the same

02:17:19.820 --> 02:17:20.820
price.

02:17:20.820 --> 02:17:21.820
It's $10 million.

02:17:21.820 --> 02:17:25.620
David Liberman: So half a million, million
for 5-10 percent.

02:17:25.620 --> 02:17:26.860
70 students agreed.

02:17:26.860 --> 02:17:28.099
Daniil Liberman: Athletes.

02:17:28.099 --> 02:17:34.929
David Liberman: One of them already netted
15 years contract for $350 million.

02:17:34.929 --> 02:17:37.060
So they did great with the fund.

02:17:37.060 --> 02:17:38.060
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, they did pretty well.

02:17:38.060 --> 02:17:40.690
David Liberman: There is one athlete —
Daniil Liberman: To sue them.

02:17:40.690 --> 02:17:44.099
David Liberman: — who tried to challenge
this agreement in court.

02:17:44.099 --> 02:17:45.679
Tim Ferriss: This one athlete challenged them?

02:17:45.679 --> 02:17:46.679
David Liberman: One athlete.

02:17:46.679 --> 02:17:47.679
Tim Ferriss: Was it the same athlete?

02:17:47.679 --> 02:17:48.679
David Liberman: No.

02:17:48.679 --> 02:17:53.689
This one, there is a great interview of him
where he explains that this man actually helped

02:17:53.689 --> 02:17:54.689
him a lot.

02:17:54.689 --> 02:17:55.689
Daniil Liberman: Best decision in his life.

02:17:55.689 --> 02:17:57.080
David Liberman: He would never do another
decision.

02:17:57.080 --> 02:18:00.889
Tim Ferriss: I'm just imagining he might be
cool with it, but I'm thinking about the managers

02:18:00.889 --> 02:18:02.920
and the agents who are like, "What the fuck
is this?"

02:18:02.920 --> 02:18:03.920
But it worked out.

02:18:03.920 --> 02:18:06.030
David Liberman: Yes, it worked out for everyone.

02:18:06.030 --> 02:18:13.100
But there is another athlete who netted like
three, $4-million-a-year type of contract.

02:18:13.100 --> 02:18:19.700
He tried to challenge this in the court and
everything in this case is more complicated

02:18:19.700 --> 02:18:20.990
than with our case.

02:18:20.990 --> 02:18:28.599
He was 16 when he signed, English wasn't his
main language, anything like that.

02:18:28.599 --> 02:18:35.540
So he signed in, steel court was on the side
of investors, upheld the ingredient.

02:18:35.540 --> 02:18:40.080
Tim Ferriss: How can people find more about
this particular example if they wanted to

02:18:40.080 --> 02:18:41.080
Google something?

02:18:41.080 --> 02:18:42.090
David Liberman: I will send you a link.

02:18:42.090 --> 02:18:43.760
Tim Ferriss: You can send it to me, we'll
put it in the show notes.

02:18:43.760 --> 02:18:44.760
David Liberman: Yes.

02:18:44.760 --> 02:18:48.030
I think it was in Wall Street Journal, the
description of all this case.

02:18:48.030 --> 02:18:54.160
But also I can send you the links to the court
case because we Googled it.

02:18:54.160 --> 02:18:55.569
All the court cases they opened.

02:18:55.569 --> 02:19:01.010
So you can see all the arguments and arguments
of the court.

02:19:01.010 --> 02:19:05.660
They were about the fact that he did well.

02:19:05.660 --> 02:19:11.030
So it's not about investors taking the glass
screen view, I think like that.

02:19:11.030 --> 02:19:16.150
So he did well and also investors described
all the scenarios.

02:19:16.150 --> 02:19:21.219
There is a scenario in the agreement that
there is scenario that they will do well and

02:19:21.219 --> 02:19:23.570
in this case we'll get this amount of money.

02:19:23.570 --> 02:19:24.880
So they described all the scenarios.

02:19:24.880 --> 02:19:25.880
Daniil Liberman: Precisely described.

02:19:25.880 --> 02:19:26.880
David Liberman: Moreover —
Daniil Liberman: So definitely.

02:19:26.880 --> 02:19:30.800
David Liberman: — I don't remember what
was in their contract, but in our contract,

02:19:30.800 --> 02:19:32.330
when we —
Tim Ferriss: With your investors?

02:19:32.330 --> 02:19:34.469
Daniil Liberman: With our investors, and when
we invest in others —

02:19:34.469 --> 02:19:35.500
David Liberman: We also invest, we're not
the founders.

02:19:35.500 --> 02:19:41.649
Daniil Liberman: We also invested already
in around a dozen people.

02:19:41.649 --> 02:19:45.510
We have this —
David Liberman: Minimal sum, which we don't

02:19:45.510 --> 02:19:46.510
touch a year.

02:19:46.510 --> 02:19:51.221
Daniil Liberman: — minimal sum which we
don't — minimal income of yours before which

02:19:51.221 --> 02:19:52.300
we're not getting anything.

02:19:52.300 --> 02:19:53.300
Tim Ferriss: Okay.

02:19:53.300 --> 02:19:54.300
There's a hurdle, right?

02:19:54.300 --> 02:19:55.300
Daniil Liberman: There's a hurdle.

02:19:55.300 --> 02:19:56.620
David Liberman: It's not about the last opinion.

02:19:56.620 --> 02:20:00.250
Daniil Liberman: It's not about your last
opinions, we actually aim to have the outliers.

02:20:00.250 --> 02:20:01.630
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, it's a power law distribution.

02:20:01.630 --> 02:20:02.630
Daniil Liberman: It's a power law.

02:20:02.630 --> 02:20:05.200
David Liberman: In most of industries there
is this power law.

02:20:05.200 --> 02:20:08.290
It's not only in tech, it's just in art, in
podcasts, in athletes.

02:20:08.290 --> 02:20:12.560
Daniil Liberman: How I feel about this, we
will all win.

02:20:12.560 --> 02:20:16.620
The market will win, society will win.

02:20:16.620 --> 02:20:23.979
Humanity will win from the fact that we will
use this power law for wider groups of people

02:20:23.979 --> 02:20:29.920
to invest in them, provide them capital, and
provide them a chance to get a higher education

02:20:29.920 --> 02:20:31.680
to try to start a business.

02:20:31.680 --> 02:20:37.750
Tim Ferriss: So question about your investments,
and we can look at it at least two different

02:20:37.750 --> 02:20:38.750
ways.

02:20:38.750 --> 02:20:42.891
So one could be your investors and why they
chose to invest in you guys?

02:20:42.891 --> 02:20:47.840
But since you guys made the decisions about
your investments, I'm curious about those

02:20:47.840 --> 02:20:50.399
people.

02:20:50.399 --> 02:20:56.470
To what extent did you choose them based on
their earning potential and so on, versus

02:20:56.470 --> 02:21:02.160
their potential to be a good ambassador or
story for this model?

02:21:02.160 --> 02:21:03.990
Because they both seem to matter, right?

02:21:03.990 --> 02:21:04.990
Daniil Liberman: Yes.

02:21:04.990 --> 02:21:05.990
Now we care about both.

02:21:05.990 --> 02:21:10.120
Tim Ferriss: So a good investment may have
the financial ROI, but there's also showcasing.

02:21:10.120 --> 02:21:14.550
Daniil Liberman: Obviously, because we have
not that much money to invest in every people

02:21:14.550 --> 02:21:15.550
we want.

02:21:15.550 --> 02:21:18.630
We needed to pick those who will be a good
story.

02:21:18.630 --> 02:21:20.790
David Liberman: So definitely we look at both.

02:21:20.790 --> 02:21:21.790
Tim Ferriss: Makes sense.

02:21:21.790 --> 02:21:28.069
David Liberman: So in terms of investors in
us, the first investor who supported us, was

02:21:28.069 --> 02:21:30.630
—
Daniil Liberman: Basically, he did not allow

02:21:30.630 --> 02:21:33.150
us to —
David Liberman: — quit from it.

02:21:33.150 --> 02:21:34.380
Daniil Liberman: — back off.

02:21:34.380 --> 02:21:39.120
How do you say, back up, and we were almost
ready to say, "Oh…" to give up, and say

02:21:39.120 --> 02:21:40.229
like, "Okay, we're not doing this."

02:21:40.229 --> 02:21:42.069
And he's like, "No, no, no, guys.

02:21:42.069 --> 02:21:43.220
You have to do this.

02:21:43.220 --> 02:21:44.220
Please."

02:21:44.220 --> 02:21:45.220
David Liberman: Same lesson.

02:21:45.220 --> 02:21:50.590
He actually had the similar idea when he was
out of high school in 2001.

02:21:50.590 --> 02:21:53.380
He's one who's a general partner of Slow Ventures
—

02:21:53.380 --> 02:21:54.811
Daniil Liberman: Too early.

02:21:54.811 --> 02:21:59.800
David Liberman: — he invested in us, but
then other prominent names like Marc Andreessen,

02:21:59.800 --> 02:22:02.440
or Chris Dixon, or Josh Kushner, they all
—

02:22:02.440 --> 02:22:04.220
Daniil Liberman: Also now own —
David Liberman: — invested —

02:22:04.220 --> 02:22:05.690
Daniil Liberman: — tiny share of Libermans.

02:22:05.690 --> 02:22:09.260
David Liberman: — in Libermans.

02:22:09.260 --> 02:22:10.260
But we earned it.

02:22:10.260 --> 02:22:16.490
So it did work right away, but we made the
first round, then we proved some results,

02:22:16.490 --> 02:22:18.190
and we made the second round.

02:22:18.190 --> 02:22:20.120
So, with the startups.

02:22:20.120 --> 02:22:29.200
So, when we invest for us, definitely it means
a lot that we want to find really great examples

02:22:29.200 --> 02:22:36.649
because all these examples will affect the
future of the idea, and this will help others

02:22:36.649 --> 02:22:39.260
to get access to the same type of resources
—

02:22:39.260 --> 02:22:45.200
Daniil Liberman: But what's more important
for us, the major thing is whether we believe

02:22:45.200 --> 02:22:46.290
in this [inaudible].

02:22:46.290 --> 02:22:51.010
If the person will continue building after
failing.

02:22:51.010 --> 02:22:53.290
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, that's a big one.

02:22:53.290 --> 02:22:56.160
That's a big one because you're not just investing
in one company, or one —

02:22:56.160 --> 02:22:57.160
Daniil Liberman: Yes.

02:22:57.160 --> 02:22:58.160
We don't invest in one company.

02:22:58.160 --> 02:23:02.490
Moreover, we would come, and already did this
through the last year and a half as we investing

02:23:02.490 --> 02:23:06.310
in people, we came to people and said like,
"Quit it.

02:23:06.310 --> 02:23:09.090
Drop it, and that's not worth your time.

02:23:09.090 --> 02:23:10.140
It's not working.

02:23:10.140 --> 02:23:11.140
Switch to something new."

02:23:11.140 --> 02:23:14.120
David Liberman: You'll never hear about —
Daniil Liberman: You will never ever hear

02:23:14.120 --> 02:23:17.340
something like that from the investor who
invested in this particular company.

02:23:17.340 --> 02:23:22.311
If I invested in you personally, I am mostly
interested in your success.

02:23:22.311 --> 02:23:25.560
And if you are locked yourself —
Tim Ferriss: That's refreshing.

02:23:25.560 --> 02:23:28.800
Daniil Liberman: — if you're locked yourself
into something which you're like, "No, no,

02:23:28.800 --> 02:23:30.450
no, I've got to do this because this is failure."

02:23:30.450 --> 02:23:31.600
It's not failure.

02:23:31.600 --> 02:23:33.149
It's an attempt.

02:23:33.149 --> 02:23:39.819
60 percent, 70 percent of those attempts are
going to end up with zero, so just move on.

02:23:39.819 --> 02:23:40.819
Start the next one.

02:23:40.819 --> 02:23:41.819
You'll be successful later.

02:23:41.819 --> 02:23:49.070
David Liberman: So with Sam, it's probably
our best relationship with the investors ever.

02:23:49.070 --> 02:23:55.830
So because it can be really open in —
Daniil Liberman: Conversation about everything.

02:23:55.830 --> 02:24:00.460
David Liberman: — not just showing the good
results, but also showing the things which

02:24:00.460 --> 02:24:05.150
are not working, struggles because now we
in a different type of relationship, and we

02:24:05.150 --> 02:24:06.150
really like it.

02:24:06.150 --> 02:24:10.100
And we actually had challenging experiences
with investors —

02:24:10.100 --> 02:24:11.100
Daniil Liberman: Before.

02:24:11.100 --> 02:24:12.450
Tim Ferriss: I don't think Sam's going to
take you to any basements.

02:24:12.450 --> 02:24:13.450
David Liberman: Yes.

02:24:13.450 --> 02:24:15.609
Tim Ferriss: It seems unlikely.

02:24:15.609 --> 02:24:17.630
Daniil Liberman: Exactly.

02:24:17.630 --> 02:24:20.330
But not even that.

02:24:20.330 --> 02:24:22.360
He's fully supporting us.

02:24:22.360 --> 02:24:25.380
David Liberman: In the same when we invest.

02:24:25.380 --> 02:24:31.120
So we understand some of the founders, who
invest, they already failed with their current

02:24:31.120 --> 02:24:34.550
projects, and they started the new one, and
we were the ones who supported them through

02:24:34.550 --> 02:24:35.550
this process.

02:24:35.550 --> 02:24:41.130
Daniil Liberman: Some of them are going through
hard times, and we would say, "Go for a sabbatical."

02:24:41.130 --> 02:24:44.311
David Liberman: "Go to Vipassana"
Daniil Liberman: "Go to Vipassana.

02:24:44.311 --> 02:24:45.590
Take care of yourself.

02:24:45.590 --> 02:24:48.340
You need to be super efficient, and you need
to be happy with your own life."

02:24:48.340 --> 02:24:49.580
—
David Liberman: Long term, you need to be

02:24:49.580 --> 02:24:50.580
efficient.

02:24:50.580 --> 02:24:51.580
Daniil Liberman: Long term.

02:24:51.580 --> 02:24:56.069
This will score us much more than if you're
like, well destroy yourself right now.

02:24:56.069 --> 02:24:57.500
Tim Ferriss: Running out in the next year.

02:24:57.500 --> 02:24:58.500
Daniil Liberman: Yeah.

02:24:58.500 --> 02:24:59.500
Yeah.

02:24:59.500 --> 02:25:00.500
David Liberman: Yes.

02:25:00.500 --> 02:25:06.859
And we already have really beautiful examples
when founders this freedom were able to act

02:25:06.859 --> 02:25:09.899
the way they wouldn't act —
Daniil Liberman: In the past.

02:25:09.899 --> 02:25:12.680
David Liberman: — it already gave some results,
and —

02:25:12.680 --> 02:25:17.280
Daniil Liberman: We completely enjoin with
the passes of all the people who we invested

02:25:17.280 --> 02:25:23.110
in, and they're all very different, different
stories, different individuals, different

02:25:23.110 --> 02:25:25.460
spheres.

02:25:25.460 --> 02:25:34.590
It's everywhere from YouTuber to reinventing
quantum physics.

02:25:34.590 --> 02:25:36.710
David Liberman: Yes.

02:25:36.710 --> 02:25:41.340
With YouTuber, Marina, she's actually really
also awesome example.

02:25:41.340 --> 02:25:42.500
She's a founder.

02:25:42.500 --> 02:25:47.700
She had the startup, but to create a marketing
vehicle for the startup, she created a YouTube

02:25:47.700 --> 02:25:50.400
channel, and then this YouTube channel —
Daniil Liberman: Media empire.

02:25:50.400 --> 02:25:53.439
David Liberman: Become a much larger things
than —

02:25:53.439 --> 02:25:54.450
Tim Ferriss: Bigger than the startup.

02:25:54.450 --> 02:25:55.770
David Liberman: — the startup.

02:25:55.770 --> 02:25:58.250
Daniil Liberman: Making like a million plus
—

02:25:58.250 --> 02:26:01.030
David Liberman: A year from her network.

02:26:01.030 --> 02:26:08.569
Daniil Liberman: But a mortgage, family, kids,
it's all risk, which you wouldn't bet all

02:26:08.569 --> 02:26:11.050
of your income to the growth.

02:26:11.050 --> 02:26:16.240
Yes, she was growing fast, but as soon as
she got the understanding that she can use

02:26:16.240 --> 02:26:20.899
the capital from her future, literally this
is what we are doing.

02:26:20.899 --> 02:26:22.760
This is capital from your future.

02:26:22.760 --> 02:26:27.040
If you are doing as you are doing right now,
then clearly you're going to have that amount

02:26:27.040 --> 02:26:28.040
of money in the future —
David Liberman: So —

02:26:28.040 --> 02:26:33.370
Daniil Liberman: You can take part of this
money, which already almost exists into the

02:26:33.370 --> 02:26:38.260
present time and use it in order to make your
future even more successful.

02:26:38.260 --> 02:26:43.880
Tim Ferriss: So this brings up something that
I wasn't sure we would get to, but I figure

02:26:43.880 --> 02:26:44.950
I can bring it up.

02:26:44.950 --> 02:26:46.420
Daniil Liberman: Time machine, is it possible?

02:26:46.420 --> 02:26:53.170
Tim Ferriss: Well there's the time machine,
but there's also maybe the — and I go back

02:26:53.170 --> 02:26:58.189
and forth on how I feel about this, but I
guess you could look to the data, if you have

02:26:58.189 --> 02:27:05.030
such data, and there is some it would seem,
but maybe the overfetishizing of suffering,

02:27:05.030 --> 02:27:09.780
and how that contributes to success, or the
belief that the more one struggles, the more

02:27:09.780 --> 02:27:15.029
likely you are to have outside success because
those make for good stories.

02:27:15.029 --> 02:27:16.029
Daniil Liberman: Yes.

02:27:16.029 --> 02:27:19.950
Tim Ferriss: Somebody suffers for their whole
life, and then they show how much they can

02:27:19.950 --> 02:27:21.029
persevere, and then they succeed.

02:27:21.029 --> 02:27:25.200
But if you look at some of the examples that
you mentioned earlier of the dropout and then

02:27:25.200 --> 02:27:32.000
the big company, these are not stories of
abject suffering from the earliest stages

02:27:32.000 --> 02:27:33.960
of childhood, generally.

02:27:33.960 --> 02:27:38.910
So, would you say, and this is tying it to
what you just said, that by relieving some

02:27:38.910 --> 02:27:45.030
of those pressures with the mortgage and the
kids, you're improving the likelihood of a

02:27:45.030 --> 02:27:49.070
bigger success later, or somebody could certainly
look at it and say, "Well, she's not going

02:27:49.070 --> 02:27:50.070
to be as hungry if..."

02:27:50.070 --> 02:27:54.830
Daniil Liberman: So we have lots to say about
this subject.

02:27:54.830 --> 02:27:57.740
Tim Ferriss: That's why I'm asking.

02:27:57.740 --> 02:28:03.800
Daniil Liberman: A, if we'll look at the most
successful experiment of all times for the

02:28:03.800 --> 02:28:11.180
humanity, providing free school education,
free school education for everyone, doesn't

02:28:11.180 --> 02:28:17.609
matter whether they're a smartest kid on the
street or not, every kid, gave us more than

02:28:17.609 --> 02:28:23.229
any other investment, which we as a humanity,
like infrastructures and stuff know.

02:28:23.229 --> 02:28:24.569
Writing free education.

02:28:24.569 --> 02:28:28.680
Is this, a capital early on —
David Liberman: It was a controversial idea

02:28:28.680 --> 02:28:29.680
like a hundred years ago.

02:28:29.680 --> 02:28:37.050
Daniil Liberman: It's an expensive investment
to provide education for every kid.

02:28:37.050 --> 02:28:38.050
Expensive investment —
David Liberman: It gives —

02:28:38.050 --> 02:28:41.860
Daniil Liberman: And we scored a beautiful
world, gave a lot to us.

02:28:41.860 --> 02:28:48.939
So providing a little bit more of the same
can give us unexpected results.

02:28:48.939 --> 02:28:54.370
The different perspective is if we'll look
at majority of the unicorns created in United

02:28:54.370 --> 02:28:58.260
States and look at who were the founders of
those companies —

02:28:58.260 --> 02:29:00.540
Tim Ferriss: And these are companies worth
a billion or more?

02:29:00.540 --> 02:29:05.319
Daniil Liberman: Those are companies worth
a billion or more.

02:29:05.319 --> 02:29:06.990
What —
David Liberman: A big share of them —

02:29:06.990 --> 02:29:08.439
Daniil Liberman: A huge share.

02:29:08.439 --> 02:29:12.040
David Liberman: — most of them are from
upper-middle-class families.

02:29:12.040 --> 02:29:14.240
They never struggled as us.

02:29:14.240 --> 02:29:17.800
They never went through real poverty type
of situation, hunger —

02:29:17.800 --> 02:29:21.890
Daniil Liberman: Losing all of their money
in the bank accounts and stuff like this.

02:29:21.890 --> 02:29:32.399
David Liberman: — all the stories about
dropouts from high school, from college, they

02:29:32.399 --> 02:29:34.480
never had student debt.

02:29:34.480 --> 02:29:37.540
Parents paid for their college, and that's
how they afforded to do —

02:29:37.540 --> 02:29:38.540
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

02:29:38.540 --> 02:29:42.479
The other thing that gets left out of those
stories is when you drop out of a fancy school,

02:29:42.479 --> 02:29:45.149
they always let you come back, generally.

02:29:45.149 --> 02:29:47.279
It's not like you burn the ships behind you.

02:29:47.279 --> 02:29:48.279
Daniil Liberman: Exactly.

02:29:48.279 --> 02:29:49.279
Exactly.

02:29:49.279 --> 02:29:54.080
And then the last one — well, probably not
the last one, but also the last one for me.

02:29:54.080 --> 02:29:56.300
Then there it'll go something.

02:29:56.300 --> 02:29:58.080
The struggle will come.

02:29:58.080 --> 02:30:05.160
It doesn't matter if you have millions of
dollars, you will always be struggling because

02:30:05.160 --> 02:30:13.220
you strive to achieve more, and it's challenging,
and it's not necessary that the struggle is

02:30:13.220 --> 02:30:15.340
needed, but it will be there.

02:30:15.340 --> 02:30:16.340
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

02:30:16.340 --> 02:30:19.850
Daniil Liberman: It doesn't matter if you're
rich or not.

02:30:19.850 --> 02:30:20.850
What was this?

02:30:20.850 --> 02:30:22.260
"Rich people also cry."

02:30:22.260 --> 02:30:29.609
Tim Ferriss: I'm not going to wear that t-shirt
around, but yeah, that's true.

02:30:29.609 --> 02:30:33.399
Daniil Liberman: And the challenge comes especially
when you started the startup.

02:30:33.399 --> 02:30:36.210
You wanted to succeed, but —
Tim Ferriss: Also human nature is human nature.

02:30:36.210 --> 02:30:37.450
Life is suffering.

02:30:37.450 --> 02:30:38.450
It's going to find you.

02:30:38.450 --> 02:30:39.450
Don't worry.

02:30:39.450 --> 02:30:40.450
Daniil Liberman: Sounds scary.

02:30:40.450 --> 02:30:41.450
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

02:30:41.450 --> 02:30:42.450
Daniil Liberman: Still here.

02:30:42.450 --> 02:30:43.450
Tim Ferriss: Exactly.

02:30:43.450 --> 02:30:45.550
David Liberman: But, in general, this argument,
we hear a lot.

02:30:45.550 --> 02:30:48.730
That founders should struggle —
Daniil Liberman: We were spoiled.

02:30:48.730 --> 02:30:53.380
David Liberman: — and we, personally, struggled
a lot through our founder's path.

02:30:53.380 --> 02:31:01.360
We were even no resources in the country where
investments are always considered as a debt.

02:31:01.360 --> 02:31:07.970
You need to put as a collateral your house
and things like that compared to San Francisco

02:31:07.970 --> 02:31:14.010
where there is no collateral, you can have
a salary.

02:31:14.010 --> 02:31:19.430
In our case, we were prohibited to have a
salary as the founders.

02:31:19.430 --> 02:31:20.430
How you should survive.

02:31:20.430 --> 02:31:21.670
You should have the skin in the game, basically.

02:31:21.670 --> 02:31:22.890
Tim Ferriss: No salary.

02:31:22.890 --> 02:31:23.890
That's hardcore.

02:31:23.890 --> 02:31:24.990
David Liberman: So hardcore.

02:31:24.990 --> 02:31:31.423
And if you compare this experience with Silicon
Valley experience, this is a struggle, this

02:31:31.423 --> 02:31:32.720
is not a struggle at all.

02:31:32.720 --> 02:31:38.280
Daniil Liberman: And then we see all the unicorns
were from a [inaudible].

02:31:38.280 --> 02:31:39.480
David Liberman: Yes.

02:31:39.480 --> 02:31:47.212
So most of the successful companies would
never exist without venture capital, without

02:31:47.212 --> 02:31:49.370
venture capital —
Daniil Liberman: Without this reason —

02:31:49.370 --> 02:31:50.970
David Liberman: Venture capital gives the
money in advance.

02:31:50.970 --> 02:31:52.300
It's actually not struggling.

02:31:52.300 --> 02:31:57.950
Struggling is having the cashflow and building
your bootstrap business.

02:31:57.950 --> 02:32:03.160
Most of the companies are not like that because
some levels of achievements —

02:32:03.160 --> 02:32:04.160
Daniil Liberman: Requires.

02:32:04.160 --> 02:32:10.859
David Liberman: — at least in some areas,
they require this freedom for you to actually

02:32:10.859 --> 02:32:18.109
risk and to try things, and that's why we
believe that some struggle is needed.

02:32:18.109 --> 02:32:22.240
But we believe that not on the level we have
right now.

02:32:22.240 --> 02:32:23.310
Tim Ferriss: You don't have to manufacture
it.

02:32:23.310 --> 02:32:24.310
Daniil Liberman: Yes.

02:32:24.310 --> 02:32:25.310
David Liberman: Yes.

02:32:25.310 --> 02:32:27.260
The student loan which stops —
Daniil Liberman: Competition is always struggle,

02:32:27.260 --> 02:32:29.689
and there will always be competition.

02:32:29.689 --> 02:32:35.720
David Liberman: — but student loan, which
just stops most of young people in United

02:32:35.720 --> 02:32:39.540
States from actually taking the risks early
and to achieve more —

02:32:39.540 --> 02:32:40.790
Tim Ferriss: And later.

02:32:40.790 --> 02:32:41.790
And later, too.

02:32:41.790 --> 02:32:45.590
I know people in their thirties who still
haven't paid off their — and I'm not talking

02:32:45.590 --> 02:32:46.590
early thirties.

02:32:46.590 --> 02:32:52.370
Mid, later thirties, especially if they go
to an extended graduate school or professional

02:32:52.370 --> 02:32:53.730
school, they're still paying off student debt.

02:32:53.730 --> 02:32:56.490
Daniil Liberman: None of the individuals —
David Liberman: This is unneeded struggle.

02:32:56.490 --> 02:33:02.170
This is really unoptimal not needed.

02:33:02.170 --> 02:33:10.630
This is artificial struggle because we know
that the subsidized money is paid through

02:33:10.630 --> 02:33:14.110
taxes, by people, but it's just spent on a
different stuff.

02:33:14.110 --> 02:33:15.830
Tim Ferriss: It goes somewhere else.

02:33:15.830 --> 02:33:20.330
David Liberman: So we don't believe in this
idea that the struggle is needed and vice

02:33:20.330 --> 02:33:21.330
versa.

02:33:21.330 --> 02:33:26.859
We believe that different local maximum, which
would be much higher in terms of the size

02:33:26.859 --> 02:33:28.650
of the economy —
Daniil Liberman: Like 10 times higher.

02:33:28.650 --> 02:33:33.279
David Liberman: — is in the place where
we remove some of the struggles, especially

02:33:33.279 --> 02:33:36.311
those which are artificial and —
Daniil Liberman: Just try to imagine what

02:33:36.311 --> 02:33:39.399
it is, 10 times higher.

02:33:39.399 --> 02:33:46.779
More electricity, more power, more roads,
more cars, more education, more whatever,

02:33:46.779 --> 02:33:49.830
more everything, 10 times more everything.

02:33:49.830 --> 02:33:57.649
This is when the abundance is probably likely
to be close to reality.

02:33:57.649 --> 02:34:00.740
And that's what we can do in this new local
maximum.

02:34:00.740 --> 02:34:07.110
If we just change the perspective on whether
what's more worse to invest in —

02:34:07.110 --> 02:34:09.320
David Liberman: Shares or —
Daniil Liberman: — the individual income,

02:34:09.320 --> 02:34:13.109
or the undying companies.

02:34:13.109 --> 02:34:14.520
The companies are dying.

02:34:14.520 --> 02:34:18.232
David Liberman: On average they live actually
less than human beings.

02:34:18.232 --> 02:34:19.319
Daniil Liberman: Less human being.

02:34:19.319 --> 02:34:27.700
David Liberman: All the statistics is for
people in their potential, but all the regulations

02:34:27.700 --> 02:34:30.029
are —
Tim Ferriss: Supporting for companies with

02:34:30.029 --> 02:34:31.569
a shorter life span.

02:34:31.569 --> 02:34:32.729
David Liberman: Yes.

02:34:32.729 --> 02:34:38.649
Daniil Liberman: It's obvious why it's this
way, because in the past we needed this invention

02:34:38.649 --> 02:34:48.500
of limited liability companies in order to
remove the personal ownership of personal

02:34:48.500 --> 02:34:49.500
[inaudible].

02:34:49.500 --> 02:34:50.500
David Liberman: Obligations.

02:34:50.500 --> 02:34:53.030
Daniil Liberman: Obligations, individual obligations
over the business.

02:34:53.030 --> 02:34:59.060
When your ship is sunk in the sea, now you
owe not only all of your money, your house,

02:34:59.060 --> 02:35:03.040
but your family can be taken and enslaved.

02:35:03.040 --> 02:35:07.350
We are far away from this world, hundreds
of years away from this world.

02:35:07.350 --> 02:35:09.490
David Liberman: So now we can —
Daniil Liberman: Through the innovation of

02:35:09.490 --> 02:35:14.950
limited liability, and then the public market,
and this is all innovation in the way how

02:35:14.950 --> 02:35:22.670
we see the market, and the way we look at
the market, but we are ready for the new innovation,

02:35:22.670 --> 02:35:26.101
and this new innovation can uplift the economy.

02:35:26.101 --> 02:35:28.800
Tim Ferriss: So question for you guys.

02:35:28.800 --> 02:35:30.880
I'll answer it first too.

02:35:30.880 --> 02:35:34.399
So what request would you like to make of
my audience?

02:35:34.399 --> 02:35:38.860
So very, very diverse audience across many
countries, many places.

02:35:38.860 --> 02:35:43.320
Most of them are interested, on some level,
in technology, not all of them.

02:35:43.320 --> 02:35:45.819
Well, let's be honest, all humans are interested
in technology.

02:35:45.819 --> 02:35:48.090
They just might not recognize it as technology.

02:35:48.090 --> 02:35:52.170
Daniil Liberman: As I said, limited liabilities
company is a technology.

02:35:52.170 --> 02:35:53.380
Tim Ferriss: Is a technology.

02:35:53.380 --> 02:35:55.300
We all use tools.

02:35:55.300 --> 02:35:59.439
That's the reason that Homo sapiens have done
what they've done, good and bad.

02:35:59.439 --> 02:36:10.310
But my request for my audience would be to
send me links to some of the biological and

02:36:10.310 --> 02:36:15.630
biophysics topics that you both discussed
earlier.

02:36:15.630 --> 02:36:21.390
Very interested in all of that, including
quantum effects and potentially bird vision

02:36:21.390 --> 02:36:24.020
or olfaction.

02:36:24.020 --> 02:36:29.270
All of this is of great interest to me, so
people can shoot those to me on Twitter @tferriss

02:36:29.270 --> 02:36:31.119
or elsewhere.

02:36:31.119 --> 02:36:36.790
But what requests would you like to make of
my audience, or places you'd like to point

02:36:36.790 --> 02:36:37.820
them, anything at all?

02:36:37.820 --> 02:36:40.100
David Liberman: Two things.

02:36:40.100 --> 02:36:47.540
First, there is a conference about quantum
biology near San Diego in 20th, like 18th

02:36:47.540 --> 02:36:48.540
of August.

02:36:48.540 --> 02:36:49.540
Daniil Liberman: 18th or 20th.

02:36:49.540 --> 02:36:50.540
Tim Ferriss: All right.

02:36:50.540 --> 02:36:51.540
Here we go.

02:36:51.540 --> 02:36:52.540
Look at that.

02:36:52.540 --> 02:36:53.540
David Liberman: Best scientists, right now,
are gathering.

02:36:53.540 --> 02:36:57.970
And it's still a quite a small group of people,
that's why they're really supportive to each

02:36:57.970 --> 02:36:58.970
other.

02:36:58.970 --> 02:36:59.970
Daniil Liberman: It's open talk.

02:36:59.970 --> 02:37:01.990
David Liberman: They should compete with each
other, but they actually are quite supportive

02:37:01.990 --> 02:37:02.990
to each other.

02:37:02.990 --> 02:37:12.819
We will be in the conference as well, but
what we would ask is actually, first, to think

02:37:12.819 --> 02:37:16.540
who would be those individuals you would invest
in?

02:37:16.540 --> 02:37:22.210
Daniil Liberman: Likely, all of us have someone
who will, like this guy, is going to be very

02:37:22.210 --> 02:37:23.210
successful.

02:37:23.210 --> 02:37:27.620
David Liberman: Second, he sent this information
out to us.

02:37:27.620 --> 02:37:30.060
So if you know the brave person, let us know.

02:37:30.060 --> 02:37:31.370
Daniil Liberman: You would invest.

02:37:31.370 --> 02:37:33.660
You would invest your money and probably —
David Liberman: We all should invest.

02:37:33.660 --> 02:37:34.660
Daniil Liberman: — let us know.

02:37:34.660 --> 02:37:41.740
And the third one is think that maybe you
should invest in this person as well.

02:37:41.740 --> 02:37:46.399
Maybe it's your kid, and you're a parent,
and maybe you should make an agreement, and

02:37:46.399 --> 02:37:49.819
actually invest in the first —
David Liberman: Our [inaudible] publicly available

02:37:49.819 --> 02:37:50.819
online.

02:37:50.819 --> 02:37:53.860
You can all read it and check what's the closest
in there.

02:37:53.860 --> 02:37:54.860
You can use this.

02:37:54.860 --> 02:37:56.400
Tim Ferriss: And that's libermans.co?

02:37:56.400 --> 02:37:58.090
David Liberman: Yes —
Daniil Liberman: Libermans.co.

02:37:58.090 --> 02:38:00.050
David Liberman: There is an agreement there.

02:38:00.050 --> 02:38:04.320
But, I would say that this is request, so
first think about this people you would invest

02:38:04.320 --> 02:38:05.470
in.

02:38:05.470 --> 02:38:10.180
Send out the information about them to us.

02:38:10.180 --> 02:38:15.333
Tim Ferriss: So what is — just being cognizant
of the fact this is a large audience, so sometimes

02:38:15.333 --> 02:38:18.500
not good to give out your private email.

02:38:18.500 --> 02:38:20.000
I've had people do that.

02:38:20.000 --> 02:38:21.470
I warn them about it.

02:38:21.470 --> 02:38:25.229
How should people notify you of these —
Daniil Liberman: We have a form.

02:38:25.229 --> 02:38:26.440
Tim Ferriss: You have a form.

02:38:26.440 --> 02:38:27.440
Perfect.

02:38:27.440 --> 02:38:28.440
Daniil Liberman: Yes, we have a form.

02:38:28.440 --> 02:38:30.740
It's humanism.is —
Tim Ferriss: IS.

02:38:30.740 --> 02:38:32.390
Daniil Liberman: .is.

02:38:32.390 --> 02:38:33.390
Humanism.is.

02:38:33.390 --> 02:38:34.390
Tim Ferriss: Humanism.is?

02:38:34.390 --> 02:38:35.390
Daniil Liberman: .is.

02:38:35.390 --> 02:38:41.859
David Liberman: And also we communicate quite
extensively through Instagram.

02:38:41.859 --> 02:38:45.070
We use Instagram not —
Daniil Liberman: Million people will come

02:38:45.070 --> 02:38:47.620
—
David Liberman: Usually, we use Instagram

02:38:47.620 --> 02:38:54.000
not for pictures, but for larger texts in
some of the ideas.

02:38:54.000 --> 02:38:58.480
Tim Ferriss: That's instagram.com/libermans,
also.

02:38:58.480 --> 02:38:59.650
Same spelling libermans.co.

02:38:59.650 --> 02:39:00.650
David Liberman: Yes.

02:39:00.650 --> 02:39:01.710
Tim Ferriss: Just telling people.

02:39:01.710 --> 02:39:02.710
David Liberman: Thank you.

02:39:02.710 --> 02:39:03.710
Tim Ferriss: All right.

02:39:03.710 --> 02:39:10.380
So Instagram, the forum@humanism.is, and people
can find the agreement on —

02:39:10.380 --> 02:39:11.750
Daniil Liberman: Libermans.co.

02:39:11.750 --> 02:39:12.750
Tim Ferriss: Libermans.co.

02:39:12.750 --> 02:39:13.750
Daniil Liberman: Yes.

02:39:13.750 --> 02:39:18.010
Tim Ferriss: And you have —
Daniil Liberman: What the other ask can be,

02:39:18.010 --> 02:39:25.550
if we truly want to see this world happening,
we need to educate the older population and

02:39:25.550 --> 02:39:31.879
the investors and the grandmas and grandpas
who actually are shareholders, stakeholders

02:39:31.879 --> 02:39:33.260
in the pension funds.

02:39:33.260 --> 02:39:39.960
We need to educate those people who actually
own most of the money in the country to gradually

02:39:39.960 --> 02:39:45.090
start investing in this type of agreements
to at least check to see what's going to happen

02:39:45.090 --> 02:39:46.609
in five, 10 years from now.

02:39:46.609 --> 02:39:51.830
If they're going to make more money by investing
in individual income rather than private companies,

02:39:51.830 --> 02:39:58.390
public companies, and in this case, we'll
be able to have an experiment, and if it's

02:39:58.390 --> 02:40:03.680
a successful experiment, will educate country
and market to shift towards this.

02:40:03.680 --> 02:40:15.000
David Liberman: Yes, you can ask your grandma
to look where are the pension money of her

02:40:15.000 --> 02:40:20.859
are invested, and to see that probably some
companies will blame for some —

02:40:20.859 --> 02:40:22.200
Daniil Liberman: Problems.

02:40:22.200 --> 02:40:24.490
David Liberman: — problems in the world.

02:40:24.490 --> 02:40:35.180
They can be the investors, but actually, companies
which affect our ecosystem in a bad way, they're

02:40:35.180 --> 02:40:36.750
not owned by —
Daniil Liberman: Bankers —

02:40:36.750 --> 02:40:38.870
David Liberman: — rich bankers —
Daniil Liberman: — or whatever.

02:40:38.870 --> 02:40:40.189
David Liberman: — or rich people.

02:40:40.189 --> 02:40:44.410
They actually, most of them, 90 percent owned
by pension funds.

02:40:44.410 --> 02:40:52.189
So, if we can educate our previous generations
that they can be a problem.

02:40:52.189 --> 02:40:53.240
Daniil Liberman: They're shareholders in the
problem.

02:40:53.240 --> 02:40:55.370
David Liberman: They're not the source problem.

02:40:55.370 --> 02:40:58.090
Tim Ferriss: Or the shareholders in the future,
and they have to choose —

02:40:58.090 --> 02:40:59.090
Daniil Liberman: Yes.

02:40:59.090 --> 02:41:00.090
Tim Ferriss: — how their money is influencing
that —

02:41:00.090 --> 02:41:01.210
Daniil Liberman: And they can be the shareholders
of the future.

02:41:01.210 --> 02:41:02.710
David Liberman: And all of us —
Daniil Liberman: This is the heritage —

02:41:02.710 --> 02:41:04.070
David Liberman: — all of us —
Daniil Liberman: — this is the heritage.

02:41:04.070 --> 02:41:06.840
They can leave, destroying the planet or actually
switching.

02:41:06.840 --> 02:41:09.970
Tim Ferriss: And I would imagine 99.9 percent
of them have no idea —

02:41:09.970 --> 02:41:11.420
David Liberman: That they can control it.

02:41:11.420 --> 02:41:12.940
Tim Ferriss: — that they have a choice.

02:41:12.940 --> 02:41:13.940
Yes.

02:41:13.940 --> 02:41:14.940
Daniil Liberman: And they have.

02:41:14.940 --> 02:41:15.940
That's the funniest part.

02:41:15.940 --> 02:41:16.940
David Liberman: They can log in, and they
can —

02:41:16.940 --> 02:41:17.940
Daniil Liberman: They can log in and change.

02:41:17.940 --> 02:41:18.940
David Liberman: — change.

02:41:18.940 --> 02:41:20.200
Let's sit down with your grandma, maybe look
at their 401K —

02:41:20.200 --> 02:41:26.450
Daniil Liberman: Write an email saying, "I
do want some share of my pension funds, my

02:41:26.450 --> 02:41:28.520
savings, to be invested in the individual's
corporations."

02:41:28.520 --> 02:41:32.609
David Liberman: — or in sustainable companies,
at least.

02:41:32.609 --> 02:41:40.570
This education should happen, otherwise there
is this belief that the [inaudible] generation

02:41:40.570 --> 02:41:45.689
did everything right, and just the new generation
is fail.

02:41:45.689 --> 02:41:51.779
But it's really important to educate that
some decisions, which were made 30 years ago,

02:41:51.779 --> 02:42:00.779
affect us a lot today, and some of this effect
can be changed if we change where the money

02:42:00.779 --> 02:42:03.910
—
Daniil Liberman: And where is the knowledge

02:42:03.910 --> 02:42:04.910
of that.

02:42:04.910 --> 02:42:05.910
David Liberman: — invested.

02:42:05.910 --> 02:42:06.910
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

02:42:06.910 --> 02:42:07.910
Yeah.

02:42:07.910 --> 02:42:13.439
I think a lot of it is a communications problem,
and an education problem, and that net-net,

02:42:13.439 --> 02:42:19.029
humans of different generations have more
in common than they have different amongst

02:42:19.029 --> 02:42:20.029
them.

02:42:20.029 --> 02:42:25.250
But, as you pointed out, sometimes the problems
aren't obvious, and sometimes problems aren't

02:42:25.250 --> 02:42:27.910
discussed, and the options aren't discussed.

02:42:27.910 --> 02:42:34.899
The fact that I didn't know this until, shockingly,
recently for me, like 10 years ago, that with

02:42:34.899 --> 02:42:40.210
retirement accounts you can invest in privately
held companies, and all sorts of other things.

02:42:40.210 --> 02:42:41.210
It is an option.

02:42:41.210 --> 02:42:43.439
It's just not readily —
Daniil Liberman: Done.

02:42:43.439 --> 02:42:46.280
Tim Ferriss: — exploited by most people
because they don't realize that it's on the

02:42:46.280 --> 02:42:47.280
menu.

02:42:47.280 --> 02:42:50.630
So I'm excited to see what people come up
to.

02:42:50.630 --> 02:42:52.580
I'm excited to see what people come up with.

02:42:52.580 --> 02:42:58.069
Daniil Liberman: Maybe someone needs to make
a startup which actually offers a solution

02:42:58.069 --> 02:42:59.470
for the pensioners to do it easier.

02:42:59.470 --> 02:43:00.520
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

02:43:00.520 --> 02:43:02.840
David Liberman: To just do the sustainable
companies.

02:43:02.840 --> 02:43:03.840
That's it.

02:43:03.840 --> 02:43:07.590
Tim Ferriss: Some type of interface that makes
it less manual, less one-on-one.

02:43:07.590 --> 02:43:08.590
Daniil Liberman: Yeah.

02:43:08.590 --> 02:43:09.590
Yeah.

02:43:09.590 --> 02:43:10.590
David Liberman: Because right now it is hard.

02:43:10.590 --> 02:43:13.819
You need to spend some time to actually set
up your account properly.

02:43:13.819 --> 02:43:16.260
But —
Daniil Liberman: It's actually the greatest

02:43:16.260 --> 02:43:17.260
